INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Know What''s Running', 'A new test command (58+) shows the name of whatever smoke test is currently running against this server, straight from the same @test hook that already logs it -- handy when watching a sweep from in-game instead of the console.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Quiet Paper Trail for Get and Drop', 'Every get and drop is now written to the day''s log file -- who, what (with its vnum), and which room -- silently, with no live [TAG] echo to anyone online. Pull it up later with log search <name> if a dispute over who had what needs sorting out. Also caught the same capitalization bug from the recent look fixes lurking in two more places (scan and get/drop messages both have their own copy of the same helper) -- fixed there too.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'The Clock Remembers Now', 'The world clock no longer resets to 8:00 AM day one every time the server restarts -- it saves itself every tick and picks right back up where it left off. Caught a nasty bug while building this: our query helper only understands %i for whole numbers, not the usual %d, and would have silently swallowed every save without complaint. Worth remembering for the next feature that writes numbers to the database.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Serious Repo Bug, Found and Fixed', 'Found something worth knowing about: a stray line in .gitignore meant to ignore crash-dump files was accidentally matching our own src/core folder by name, silently keeping gametime.c and zone.c out of the git repository the whole time, even though both were pushed as part of earlier commits. The actual code was never lost -- it was live on the server the whole time -- but the repository itself was missing two real source files. Fixed the ignore rule and both files are now properly tracked. Also added a quiet heartbeat: once every real hour, on the half hour, everyone gets a blank line so a tick is visible without any actual message.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Mobs Learn Right From Wrong', 'Characters now have an alignment (good/evil/neutral, set with set <name> alignment), shown in score as a word rather than a raw number. Aggressive mobs check it before picking a fight: a sufficiently good-aligned target gets left alone instead of attacked on sight. A scoped-down first step toward the original''s full Mobile_Attitude system -- suspicion, greed, malice, hate/fear lists, and the rest are still future work.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Pools, Puddles, and Poor Life Choices', 'A new immortal-only pee command (51+) leaves a puddle on the floor -- purely for flavor, but it plugged into two other systems: a bad enough limb injury now leaves a pool of blood behind too, and a new drink <puddle> command lets anyone sample either one, with a real chance of a (non-lethal) case of poisoning. Repeat the same kind of mess in one room and it grows -- puddle, then pool, then large pool, the color escalating from dim to bright the bigger it gets -- rather than littering the floor with duplicates. Left alone, every puddle shrinks back down a size every tick or so until it''s soaked fully into the ground.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Wandering Mob Bug, and Immortals Get Their Own Entrance', 'Fixed a real bug in mob wandering: the leave/arrive messages were printing a mob''s raw look-up keywords instead of its actual description ("lady stroll walk leaves" instead of anything sensible) -- now reads properly, with the real direction, e.g. "A lady walks to the east." Immortals can also now set their own custom arrival/departure messages with bamfin and bamfout, in place of the default wording -- e.g. "drags $p cross in from the $d" reads correctly for any gender, since $p/$d are filled in with the right pronoun and direction for whoever set it.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'One Edit Command To Rule Them All', 'edroom, edzone, edplayer, edhelp, ednews, edwiznews, and edrules are gone -- everything now goes through a single edit command instead: edit room [vnum], edit zone <n>, edit player <name>, edit help <topic>, edit news <headline>, edit wiznews <headline>, edit rules <n> <title>. Nothing about how any of them work changed, and every level requirement is exactly what it always was -- just one door to knock on instead of seven.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Scripting Arrives: edit trigger', 'Builders (51+) can now attach scripted behavior to a room, mob, or object with edit trigger -- no recompile needed, unlike SneezyMUD''s old spec proc system. Room triggers fire on enter or ambiently at random; mob triggers fire on greet (someone walks in), speech (a matching keyword), death, or random; object triggers fire on get or wear. The script itself is a short list of actions -- echo, echoroom, emote, teleport, give, damage, log -- written in the same line editor news and rules already use. See help trigger for the full rundown. This is a first pass: the action list is deliberately small on purpose, not a general-purpose language.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Shout It From the Rooftops', 'A new shout command reaches every playing character in the game, not just the shouter''s room -- asleep characters don''t hear it, and toggle noshout lets a mortal opt out entirely (an immortal''s shout always gets through regardless). See help shout.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Slow Tick, Fixed', 'Found and fixed a real performance bug in the new scripting system: the random-trigger check was quietly hitting the database once for every single mob and room in the world, every tick, even on servers with no random triggers set up at all. Now it checks once per tick instead, and aitick/the real world clock both run at their proper speed again.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Loot It All At Once', 'get all <container> empties an entire corpse, bag, or chest into your hands in one command instead of naming each item -- get all corpse after a kill is now all it takes.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'News Was Quietly Losing Its Oldest Entries', 'Found and fixed a real bug: both news and wiznews build the whole feed into a fixed-size buffer before showing it a page at a time, and with this many entries now on the books, that buffer was overflowing and silently cutting off everything past a certain point -- including some of the oldest stories. Both are sized generously now, with plenty of room to keep growing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fountains Are Drinkable Again', 'Fixed a real bug: drink only ever recognized ground puddles (pee/blood), so a real fountain or drink container already sitting in a room -- and there are plenty seeded in the world -- failed with "You don''t see that here to drink." Drink now also resolves any real drink-category object: clean water, no poison, never runs dry.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixtures Get Top Billing', 'Room descriptions now list permanent scenery -- fountains, lampposts, statuary, anything that can''t be picked up -- ahead of ordinary loot and whoever else is standing there, instead of whatever order they happened to load in.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Starter Taste of Scripting', 'Two of SneezyMUD''s old hardcoded behaviors have been reborn as real edit trigger content: the dirty refuse hauler mutters something rude if you say hello nearby (and grumbles unprompted now and then), and a new pickable tangle of thorny brambles scratches whoever grabs it. Both are ordinary database rows now, not compiled-in code -- see edit trigger list mob 33271 or edit trigger list obj 1000001 to see exactly how they''re built.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Browse Prototypes by Number, Not Just Name', 'vnum <room|obj|mob> <pattern> now also accepts a bare vnum or a vnum range (vnum obj 1017, or vnum obj 100-200) to browse prototypes directly by number, alongside its existing name search -- one command instead of three separate list commands.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Game-Wide Switches Get Their Own Room', 'toggle no longer shows or accepts multiplay or any other global, everyone-affecting switch -- those moved entirely to a new gametog command (58+). toggle is now purely about your own personal switches (color, hp in prompt, and the rest).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Bugs Can Be Fixed, Not Just Deleted', 'A new edbug <id> [note] command (59+) marks a filed bug resolved without deleting it -- if the reporter is online right now, they get a live notice, note included. delbug is still there for a report that never deserved to be kept around at all.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Quiet Watcher Joins the Staff', 'A new snoop <name> command (59+) mirrors everything a lower-level player sees and types straight to your own screen -- you cannot snoop anyone your own level or higher, it just fails. Bare snoop (no name) stops it. Covert by design: the target is never told.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Help Files Get a Cleanup', 'Help topics now title-case the command name in their header, render the description in bright white instead of magenta, and drop redundant "Administrator (N+) only" phrasing now that the footer already shows Minimum Level. The /f (format) command in every text editor also indents each paragraph''s first line two spaces.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Exits Now Match the Room', 'The [Exits:] line in look used to always be green -- now it takes on the same color as the room name itself, so a lava cavern''s exits glow red, a forest clearing''s glow green, and so on, instead of clashing with whatever the room actually looks like.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Choose Your Path', 'Character creation now asks for a race (Human, Elf, Ogre, Dwarf, Hobbit, Gnome), a class (Mage, Cleric, Warrior, Thief, Druid, Monk), and an alignment (Good, Neutral, Evil) right after point-buy attributes -- each race and class carries its own stat bonuses and penalties, and class also scales starting hit points. Alignment isn''t just flavor: an aligned aggressive mob will only ever fight the opposite alignment (never its own kind), leaving neutral players alone in combat but occasionally giving them a one-line in-character reaction instead -- a nod of approval from something good, a sneer from something evil.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Snoop Marks Every Mirrored Line', 'snoop used to only prefix the target''s typed commands with "% " -- their own output was mirrored to you completely unmarked, easy to mistake for your own screen. Every mirrored line is now prefixed the same way, commands and output alike.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Build It Live, Then Zonefile Create', 'New builder tool: `zonefile create <zone>` snapshots a zone''s CURRENT live mobs and objects -- load a mob, drop a chest, put something inside it -- into real reset data, so the next boot or periodic reset recreates exactly what you built. It''s safe to re-run: anything already covered is left alone, so deleting one line from the reset data and running it again only fills that gap back in, never duplicating what''s still there. See `help zonefile`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Bamfin/Bamfout Now Belong to Goto', '`bamfin`/`bamfout` set your custom `goto` teleport messages now, shown to the room you leave and the room you arrive in -- three tokens available: <N> for your name (anywhere in the message), $g for the room''s ground word, and $p for your pronoun. The WALKING move-message feature that used to answer to `bamfin`/`bamfout` is renamed `poofin`/`poofout` (its original name) -- nobody''s custom message was lost in the move. See `help bamfin`/`help poofin`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Character Creation Explains What Went Wrong', 'A rejected character name used to get one generic message no matter the reason. Now it tells you exactly what''s wrong: too short (under 3 letters), too long (over 15 letters), or containing something other than letters -- each gets its own message.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Nospam Silences Missed Swings', 'A new personal `toggle nospam` (default off) hides "you miss"/"X misses you" combat messages from your own screen -- ported from Sneezy''s AUTO_NOSPAM. It''s checked independently for each side of a swing, so your own toggle only ever affects what YOU see.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Hostnames Instead of Raw IPs', 'Immortal-facing logs and `users` now show a reverse-DNS hostname instead of a bare IP address where one resolves (falls back to the IP otherwise) -- looked up in the background so a slow DNS server never stalls the game.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Every Class Gets Its Own Skill Sheet', 'A new `skills` command shows your class''s full roster of skills/spells, ported from Sneezy''s real discipline lists and organized into three tiers: Combat, <Class> Skills, and Advanced <Class> Skills. Warrior, Thief, Monk, Cleric, and Mage are all populated -- a skill is known once your level reaches its threshold, no separate practice step yet. Actual in-combat mechanics for most individual skills are still on the way; this is the roster/visibility layer.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Edit Player Can Now Change Class and Race', '`edit player` (58+) gained two new fields: 9) Class and 0) Race, alongside the existing level/xp/hp/attributes/gender/title/load-room/handedness. Changes save and sync to an already-connected target immediately, same as every other field.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Armor Now Actually Protects You', 'Worn armor has a real Armor Class now, shown on `score`, and it makes you harder to hit -- subtracted from the attacker''s hit roll in combat. Since the seeded item data never carried a real per-piece AC value, it''s derived from the piece''s weight (heavier = more protection, capped at 30). The to-hit formula also gained a guaranteed hit/miss floor: no stat or gear mismatch, however extreme, can make a hit or a miss completely impossible.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Ordinal Targeting: 2.sword, 3.goblin', 'When more than one thing in a room shares a keyword, "get 2.sword"/"kill 3.goblin" now reach the 2nd/3rd match instead of always the first. Bare names still work exactly as before. Works for get/drop/put/give/wear/remove and attack/kill alike.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Combat Messages No Longer Show Raw Mob Keywords', 'Fighting a mob whose name has several keywords (e.g. "lady stroll walk", matched by look lady/stroll/walk) used to leak that raw keyword list into miss/hit/death messages and even a mob''s own corpse description. All of it now correctly shows the mob''s short description instead ("a lady out for a stroll"). Several smaller color-tag-skip capitalization bugs (mob greet/speech/death triggers, wander/scavenge/aggress messages) were fixed the same pass.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Casting and Praying Need the Right Item', 'Mages and Druids can now `cast <spell>` -- but need a spell component (any item keyworded "component") on hand, consumed on a successful cast. Clerics `pray <spell>` instead, needing a holy symbol (keyworded "symbol") that is NOT consumed. Both check class and level against the spell first. Real per-spell mechanics are still limited (heal/damage-flavored spells work; the rest cast successfully but do nothing yet) -- the full effect system is follow-up work.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Room Listings Stack Identical Things', 'Multiple identical mobs or objects in a room now show as one line with an "(xN)" count instead of repeating the same line over and over -- three gremlins now read "A gremlin is here. (x3)".')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Immortals Ignore Class Restrictions on Skills/Spells', 'Immortals can now `cast` or `pray` any spell in the game regardless of their own class, and `skills` shows every class''s full roster (grouped by class heading) instead of just their own. Level requirements are bypassed the same way. Component/holy-symbol item requirements still apply -- those are an item gate, not a class restriction. Mortals see no change.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Practice with a Guildmaster to Learn Your Discipline', 'Reaching a spell/skill''s level requirement is no longer enough on its own -- you now also need to have practiced that discipline. A new `practice` command, used at a guildmaster mob of your own class, raises your Basic and Advanced discipline percentages (10% per visit). Basic-tier skills/spells need any Basic percentage above 0; Advanced-tier ones need Basic at 95%+ AND some Advanced percentage. `skills` shows both percentages and marks locked entries with the reason. Guildmaster mobs are recognized by the "guildmaster" keyword plus a class match (the old, previously-unused mob.class column now feeds this). Immortals bypass this gate entirely, same as the class/level bypass.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Heal Someone Else, Then Just `continue`', '`pray heal light <target>` (and every other heal-type prayer) can now be aimed at someone else in the room instead of only yourself -- leave the target off to heal yourself as before. A new `continue` command repeats that same heal automatically, round after round, until the target is fully healed or you run out of holy symbols. Speaking of which: holy symbols are now consumed on every successful prayer, just like a mage or druid''s spell components -- no longer a permanent keepsake.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New `balance` Command for Implementors (60+)', 'A new menu-driven `balance class|race <name>` command lets an Implementor tune four gamewide modifiers per class or race: HP multiplier, damage multiplier, to-hit modifier, and AC modifier. Every class/race starts perfectly neutral (1.00x/1.00x/+0/+0) -- nothing changes until someone actually balances one. Saved changes apply immediately, server-wide, no restart needed.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Weapon Depth: Sharp Weapons and Dual Wielding', 'Edged and piercing weapons (swords, axes, daggers, spears, etc) now deal a little extra damage over blunt ones. Also, the "dual wield" skill (Warrior/Thief) now does something: it removes the usual off-hand damage penalty, so a trained dual-wielder swings evenly with both hands instead of favoring their main hand.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: Leveling Up Now Actually Raises Your HP', 'Found while testing combat depth: leveling up used to only raise your level number -- your max HP (and every limb''s own HP) stayed exactly where it started, forever, making even a high-level character just as fragile as a fresh one. Leveling up now properly raises your max HP and fully heals you, the reward it was always supposed to be.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Doors Can Be Trapped Now', 'A Thief who knows "set trap (door)" can rig a trap on any closed door with the new `settrap <direction>` command; "disarm trap" safely removes one with `disarmtrap <direction>`. Walk through a rigged door without knowing "detect trap" and it springs, hurting a random limb -- one use only. A Thief who DOES know "detect trap" spots it and steps around unharmed, leaving it rigged for whoever comes next.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Buffs and Debuffs: The New Affects System', 'A new general-purpose "affects" system tracks temporary buffs and debuffs with their own countdown, separate from combat. First one live: `pray sanctuary` wraps you (or an ally) in a shimmering aura that halves incoming damage for a while, then fades on its own with a "wears off" message. Check what''s currently affecting you any time with the new `affects` command.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: Reconnecting Could Leave Your Limbs Fragile', 'Found while testing the affects system: reconnecting to an already-created character reset your limbs'' toughness back to a brand-new level-1 character''s, no matter how strong you''d actually grown -- making a veteran just as easy to dismember as a newbie right after logging back in. Limbs are now properly resized to match your real strength on every fresh login.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Friendlier Unknown-Command Message', 'Typing a command that does not exist no longer replies with the terse "Huh?!" -- it now says "Command not found, maybe submit an idea if you believe TobinMUD should have it."')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Grimhaven is Now Tobin City', 'Every mention of "Grimhaven" across rooms, mobs, objects, and zone data (any capitalization) has been renamed to "Tobin City" to match the game''s actual setting.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Six New Commands: consider, examine, sip, show, tell, whisper', '`examine <target>` is a synonym for "look at" something. `consider <target>` (or `consider self`) sizes up a fight before you start one. `sip <liquid>` tastes a puddle or fountain with much less risk than a full `drink`. `show <item> <person>` holds an item up for someone to see without giving it up. `tell <name> <message>` reaches anyone playing anywhere; `whisper <name> <message>` is the same but room-only, with bystanders only knowing a conversation happened.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Implementor Toy: egotrip blast', 'A new `egotrip blast <target>` command (60+) hits a target with a non-lethal bolt of lightning, halving their current HP (never below 1). The original Sneezy egotrip''s other eleven subcommands aren''t here yet -- they depend on systems Tobin hasn''t built (disease, garble, mob AI hate/aggro, and more).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Combat Messages No Longer Show Raw Damage Numbers', 'Regular combat, spell, and trap messages now read like "You stab a messenger''s left finger." instead of "...for 4 damage!" -- cleaner flavor text for everyone. Immortals still see the number (useful for testing and balancing); mortals don''t.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Look At Someone To See What They''re Wearing', '`look <person>` now shows their worn equipment (body slots and both hands) right after their description, the same listing the `equipment` command shows you for yourself.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Autoloot Toggle', '`toggle autoloot` now exists: when on, defeating an opponent (or instakilling one as an immortal) automatically pulls everything out of their corpse and into your own inventory, instead of leaving it there to loot by hand.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Major Limbs Are Now Fatal, and Bigger Targets Get Hit More', 'Losing your head, neck, waist, or torso to combat damage is now instant death -- losing an arm, leg, finger, or foot still is not. Taking off the neck also takes the head with it. Which limb gets hit is no longer a flat coin-flip across all thirteen either: a torso is a much bigger target than a finger, so it (and the other bigger limbs) gets hit far more often, mirroring real Sneezy''s own body-part weighting.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Builder Tool: stat (55+)', '`stat obj|mob|room <vnum>` dumps every single field of that prototype''s row, plus its exits (for a room) or its hitroll/damroll/AC affects (for an object). Unlike `vnum`, which searches by name, `stat` needs an exact vnum -- but shows everything about it once you have one.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Immortals No Longer Take Damage in Combat', 'Landing a hit on an immortal still works exactly as before (verb, messaging, and all) -- it just always deals zero damage now. You can still spar with an immortal for testing purposes; you just can''t actually hurt one.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'stat Speaks Plain English Now', '`stat` no longer dumps raw numbers for the columns that are really flags or lookups: an object''s wear slots and a mob''s action flags now list as readable names ("[ SENTINEL ] [ SCAVENGER ]"), a mob''s class and race show as text ("Cleric", "GOBLIN") instead of their raw codes, and a room exit''s direction, door type, and condition read as words ("dir=north door=Door cond=Closed") instead of numbers. Faction/fact_perc are dropped from the mob dump entirely -- factions are not and will not be supported. The twelve raw attribute columns seeded on every mob are also trimmed down to just the six Tobin actually uses (strength, constitution, dexterity, intelligence, wisdom, charisma); the other six are real Sneezy data but nothing reads them, so showing them would be misleading.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A save Command, and Quitting Now Saves You Too', 'A new `save` command persists your character on demand -- useful reassurance mid-session. More importantly, quitting (`quit!`) or dying now automatically saves everything first, closing a real gap: taking damage in a fight was never written to the database until the fight actually ended, so a quit or a disconnect mid-battle could quietly roll your HP back to whatever it was last saved at. That state is now captured the moment you leave, one way or another.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'stat Decodes Even More Now', '`stat obj` now shows `type` and `action_flag` as readable names instead of raw numbers, and `stat room` does the same for `sector` and `room_flag` -- the same decoding `look` and the room/object editors already relied on internally, just not previously surfaced by `stat`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'toggle Sorted Into Categories', '`toggle`''s listing is now grouped under three headings -- Preferences, Prompt, and Communication -- instead of one flat list, making it quicker to find the switch you want as more of them get added.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Few More Huh?!s Silenced', 'Found five more spots that still answered with the old terse "Huh?!" instead of the friendlier unknown-command message: casting or praying as the wrong class, `purge linkdead` below level 58, and a Thief-only door trap command used by someone who never learned the skill. All five now match the rest of the game.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'rent Works Now: The Safe Way to Log Off', 'A new `rent` command stores your belongings and ends your session cleanly, the recommended way to leave the game -- simply dropping your connection has always carried a small element of risk. Rent out for a while and you will find yourself healed up when you return, roughly in proportion to how long you were away. Renting is refused mid-fight. Per-item storage cost and restricting rent to inns are not in yet -- both need systems that do not exist yet (money, and a decision about which rooms count as inns).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'quit! Is Now the Risky Way to Leave', 'Now that `rent` exists as the safe way to log off, plain `quit!` has earned its reputation: everything you are carrying, wearing, or holding spills onto the floor right where you typed it, both you and anyone else in the room are told, and there it stays until someone -- possibly not you -- picks it back up. Gold is not affected, since there is no money system yet to have any.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Help Gets a Real Front Door', 'Two new topics -- `help playing` and `help administration` -- are now the front door to the whole help system: a first-timer sees a one-time nudge toward `help playing` right at character creation, and both `help` and `wizhelp`''s own footers point newcomers there too. `playing` walks a new player through looking around, talking, fighting, classes and skills, and the safe (`rent`) versus risky (`quit!`) way to leave. `administration` explains the immortal level ladder (51 through 60) by WHY each tier sits where it does, not just what unlocks -- including why the debug tools (`hurtlimb`, `aitick`, `stat`, `balance`, `egotrip`, `test`) exist at all: real combat, world ticks, and decay are slow and random by design, and these make them instant and deterministic for testing. Alongside that, every command that had gone entirely without a help entry -- `stat`, `save`, `rent`, `cast`, `pray`, `practice`, `skills`, `affects`, `consider`, `continue`, `examine`, `show`, `sip`, `tell`, `whisper`, `balance`, `egotrip`, `settrap`, `disarmtrap`, `hurtlimb`, `aitick`, `immort`, `test` -- now has one. Found and fixed a real bug chasing this down: the old edroom/edzone/edplayer/edhelp/ednews/edwiznews/edrules -> edit-<noun> rename from Session 21 was never actually safe to re-run -- it would collide with itself and abort partway through on a second deploy. Fixed for good.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'goto guildmaster/rent/surplus: Directions, Not Transfers', '`goto` gained three mortal-usable landmark forms: `goto guildmaster` reports walking directions to the nearest guildmaster of your own class, `goto rent` to the inn, and `goto surplus` to the surplus store -- none of these teleport you, they just tell you the shortest real way there, by actual room exits. Standing right there already just says so. Every other form of `goto` (a room vnum, or another player''s name) is still immortal-only, and still teleports as before.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Character Creation: Choose Race and Class First, and See Who You Are Becoming', 'Two changes to new-character creation. First, you now choose your race and class BEFORE allocating attributes, not after -- so your point-buy choices can be made knowing who you already are. Second, the race and class screens no longer show raw stat bonuses ("+2 Dex, -4 Con"); each entry is now a short, evocative description of what that race or class is actually like to play. The real mechanics behind the scenes have not changed at all -- only how they are presented before you have even started playing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'stat player: See Everything About a Character', '`stat` now has a fourth form: `stat player <name>` dumps a player''s identity, level/experience/HP/alignment, and attributes, decoded the same way the object/mob/room forms already were -- class, race, and gender as readable words, plus an alignment tier alongside the raw number. Also fixed a real dispatch bug found while testing this batch of changes: typing the exact command `set` was occasionally landing on `settrap` instead, because of how commands are matched by table order -- fixed for good.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'open door <direction> Works Now', '`open`/`close` now accept the word "door" in front of a direction (`open door north`, `close door east`), matching the original game''s documented phrasing -- previously only the bare direction form worked. A bare `open door` with no direction opens the room''s one door, if it has exactly one.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Command List Reordered: Mortal Commands First', 'The `help`/`wizhelp` command tables were fully reordered so every mortal-usable command now sorts ahead of every immortal-only one -- less chance of an immortal fat-fingering a mortal command''s abbreviation into an immortal one by accident. Found and fixed two real abbreviation collisions while at it: `set` was occasionally landing on `settrap` instead, and after `goto` became mortal-visible, `g` started reaching it instead of `get`. Further alphabetizing within each tier is on hold pending a follow-up decision.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Game Clock and Regen Now Keep True Real-Time Pace', 'Fixed a timing bug in the server''s core pulse scheduler (`game_loop.c`): every periodic system -- HP regen, combat rounds, the game clock, mob AI, zone aging, puddle decay -- was gated by how many times the main loop happened to iterate, not by real elapsed time. Since a busy socket lets the loop iterate far faster than its normal 100ms tick, heavy connection traffic could make these systems fire much more often than intended (found while chasing a flaky trigger-damage test: an HP regen tick was firing within 1.5 real seconds instead of its intended ~5). The scheduler now tracks real wall-clock time directly, so every pulse-based system keeps its documented pace regardless of how busy the server is.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Command Table Alphabetized -- With Zero Change to What You Type', 'The command table is now alphabetized within each tier, finishing the reorder that was left on hold. Mortal commands still come first as a block, movement still sits pinned at the very top (n/e/s/w/u/d must always mean movement), and everything else is now in plain A-to-Z order -- so finding a command in the source is no longer a hunt. Crucially, NOT ONE abbreviation changed: `sa` still says, `sc` still scores, `dr` still drops, `a` still attacks, `c` still closes. That is not a hopeful claim -- the reorder was generated and then verified by a script that resolves all 432 possible abbreviations at every level against both the old and new tables and diffs them, and it reports zero differences. Sixteen pairs in the mortal tier and one in the immortal tier are deliberately left out of strict alphabetical order because pure A-to-Z would have handed a shared abbreviation to the wrong command (`say` must precede `save` so `sa` still speaks); each is marked in the source with the abbreviation it protects. Also corrected some long-stale comments in that file that claimed `c` reached `color` and `h` reached `help` -- `close` and `hit` had quietly owned those for a while.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Practice System Redesigned: Three Disciplines and Practice Points', 'The practice system has been rebuilt from the ground up. Players now earn practice points when they level up (random 6-8 base, scaled by Wisdom via the `balance wisdom` modifier). Points are spent at guildmasters to raise discipline percentages -- each point buys a random 1-2%. Three discipline tiers now exist: Basic (level-51 guildmasters), Combat (level-80 guildmasters), and Advanced (level-100 guildmasters). Advanced practice is locked until both Basic and Combat hit 100%. The `practice` command now accepts batch syntax (`practice combat 7` to spend multiple points at once). New mortal landmark: `goto combat` gives walking directions to the nearest Combat guildmaster. Combat-tier skills now require some Combat discipline before they can be used, just as Basic and Advanced skills already did. All three percentages are shown in `skills` output.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Practice System Polish: Status Always Visible, Smarter Aliases', 'Four small follow-ups to yesterday''s practice system redesign, found by playtesting. Bare `practice` (no arguments) now always shows your own discipline percentages and practice points, even with no guildmaster in the room -- it is your own character sheet, not something you should need to ask a guildmaster for. When a guildmaster IS present, the status line now only invites training in the discipline that particular guildmaster actually teaches, instead of suggesting all three regardless of tier. `practice <yourclassname>` (e.g. `practice warrior`) now works as a synonym for `practice basic`, matching how `skills` already labels that tier by class name ("Warrior Skills"). And a new `goto <classname>` (e.g. `goto thief`) gives walking directions to that NAMED class''s own Basic guildmaster, not just your own class -- handy for immortals and curious players checking on another class''s trainer without switching characters.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`skills`/`bug`/`idea`/`rules` Now Page Their Output', 'These four commands could print more than a screenful in one shot with no way to slow it down -- `skills` especially, now that it lists three discipline percentages plus every tier. All four now go through the same pager `news`/`wiznews` already use: one screen at a time, ENTER for more, Q to stop. `skills` for an immortal (every class''s full roster) and the outstanding `bug`/`idea` lists benefit the most, but any of the four can grow past one screen as the game''s content grows, so all were switched over together.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Real Per-Skill Proficiency: Learn By Doing, Sneezy-Style', 'Discipline percentages (Basic/Combat/Advanced) have only ever gated ACCESS to a whole tier -- once unlocked, every spell in that tier always worked. That is no longer the whole story. Every individual skill and spell now has its OWN 0-100% proficiency, separate from the discipline gate, that climbs with actual use -- the classic "learn by doing" mechanic, ported from Sneezy''s own system. A freshly-accessible spell starts barely competent (1%) and climbs toward a ceiling set by your discipline percentage for that tier every time you attempt it, win or lose -- so practicing the discipline raises the CEILING, and using the skill climbs TOWARD it. Higher Wisdom softens the diminishing-returns curve, letting gains stay likely further into the climb. Proficiency now genuinely matters: `cast`/`pray` roll against it for every attempt (a low-proficiency spell often fizzles -- "You fumble the casting..." -- a well-practiced one rarely does), and `settrap`/`disarmtrap` and dual wield hook in the same way. `skills` now shows each known skill''s proficiency in brackets, e.g. "bash [34%]". Immortals are unaffected -- they still bypass every gate outright.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`practice <discipline>` Now Shows the Skill List Anywhere', '`practice combat` used to require standing at a Combat guildmaster just to attempt spending a point, and refused outright everywhere else -- not very useful for checking your own progress. Bare `practice <discipline>` (no count) is now a look-anywhere status view: it lists every skill/spell in that ONE discipline along with each one''s own individual proficiency percentage, no guildmaster required. Standing in front of the matching guildmaster adds a reminder to actually spend points there. Only an EXPLICIT count (`practice combat 5`) still spends practice points, and that form still needs the right guildmaster present, unchanged. Net effect: `practice basic`/`practice combat`/`practice advanced` are now readable any time, and only spending requires the trip.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`who` Now Reports Active Links, Linkdead, and Total Players', '`who` always ends with a summary line now: how many connections are actively linked, how many characters are sitting linkdead in the world (disconnected without a clean `quit!`/`rent`), and the total. Shows regardless of any filter (`who imm`/`who mort`/`who <name>`) since it''s a server-health stat, not a scoped listing. Built while investigating a pre-existing connection-handling issue (see the relevant TODO entry) to make linkdead accumulation directly observable instead of guessed at -- `purge linkdead` (Administrator+) already existed to clean them up, this just makes it obvious when there''s something to clean.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Connection Investigation Closed (False Alarm) + a Real Output-Buffering Fix', 'The "connection silently stalls" issue chased for most of today turned out not to be a server bug at all -- it was a test script correctly taking 45+ seconds to run (creating five characters, each needing about nine slow-by-design network round trips) while the diagnostic scripts checking on it were only willing to wait 15-25 seconds before calling it stuck. Re-run with a realistic timeout, the full test passed clean. While chasing it, though, a real (if apparently never-yet-triggered) bug in the networking code got found and fixed: outgoing data could, in rare circumstances, be silently dropped if a connection''s send buffer was momentarily full, with neither side ever finding out. Every connection now has a proper backlog that retries automatically until the data actually goes out, including across a `copyover`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Boxed Account Menu + a New Login Banner', 'Reaching the account menu used to dump your full character list immediately, with a repeated block of C/N/D/X/Q instructions underneath every single time. It now opens with a clean boxed menu instead -- just the five letter options -- and only lists your characters by name once you actually press C, at which point it shows them numbered and asks which one to connect. A single-character account still connects straight through on C, same as before. The connection screen ahead of login also has a new look: a fortress gate now stands above the TobinMUD logo, greeting every new arrival before they even give an account name.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Boxed Character Creation Screens', 'The revealed character list at the account menu is now boxed too, with each number in bright cyan. New character creation got the same treatment: the race, class, and alignment screens each now present their full option list inside a bordered box, with the option numbers colorized to match. The attribute point-buy screen is boxed now too.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Long Command Output Now Pages Automatically', 'Any command whose reply runs past about 20 lines now shows it a page at a time (ENTER for more, Q to stop) instead of dumping it all at once -- the same behavior `news`/`wiznews`/`skills` already had. Covers `help`, `wizhelp`, `who`, `users`, `stat`, `inventory`, looking inside a well-stocked container, `log list`, and `edit trigger list`. Short output is completely unaffected -- no pager prompt appears unless there is actually more than one page to show.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`pee` Now Takes a Liquid Type', 'Bare `pee` still works exactly as before. `pee <liquid>` -- water, wine, beer, or acid so far, abbreviations welcome -- leaves a puddle of that liquid instead. A second pee of the same liquid grows the existing puddle bigger; a different liquid starts its own puddle alongside it.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: Define Your Own Command Aliases', '`alias <name> <expansion>` lets you set up your own shortcuts -- `alias k kill` means typing `k orc` sends `kill orc`. Bare `alias` lists what you have, `alias <name>` shows one, `alias remove <name>` deletes one. Aliases live on your account and follow you to every character you play, but mortal and immortal aliases are kept separate -- an immortal alias only works while playing an immortal character, even on the same account. Up to 20 per tier. See `help alias`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Gold and Shops Have Arrived', 'Killing something now has a payoff: defeated creatures hand their killer gold on the spot, scaled to their level. Check your purse with `score`. All 264 of the game''s original shops are open for business too -- find a shopkeeper, `list` their wares, `buy <item>` or `sell <item>` (only certain goods, matching what each shop actually deals in). Every shop keeps its own personality in its buy/sell messages, same as the original game, just counted in gold now instead of talens. Not built yet: an in-game shop editor, and the old seeded piles of treasure lying around the world do not yet convert to gold when picked up -- both are on the list.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Treasure Piles Now Pay Out', 'Those old piles of gold scattered around the world -- treasure hoards, quest rewards, that sort of thing -- actually do something now. Pick one up and its worth goes straight into your purse instead of sitting in your inventory as a prop; check `score` to see it land.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Kinder Way to Close Up Shop', '`shutdown` is here, Implementor only. Bare `shutdown` counts down from 5 seconds, warning everyone along the way; `shutdown 120` (or any number of seconds) does the same over a longer window without freezing the game for anyone in the meantime. Every connected character is saved right before the world actually closes. Started one by mistake? `shutdown cancel` calls it off.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Shop Listings Are Numbered', '`list` at a shop now numbers every item alongside its price. `buy 3` buys the third thing listed, same as typing its name -- whichever is quicker.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'The Mystery Tag Solved', 'A few old items and rooms had a stray `<h>` sitting in their text where a name was supposed to go -- leftover from the original game''s template system, never carried over to this port. It now fills in as "TobinMUD" wherever it appears, the way it always meant to. A handful of other leftover mentions of the old game''s name, scattered through a book, a couple of rooms, and an item description, are now TobinMUD too.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`wipe` Arrives -- Administrator Only', 'The most permanent command in the game is here: `wipe <name> <password>` erases a character for good, and `wipe account <name> <password>` takes the whole account with it, every character on it included. It needs the wipe master password to run at all (ask whoever holds it), and only reaches someone below your own level. There is no undo, so it earns its own confirmation: getting the password wrong just fails quietly, no second-guessing needed on our end.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Dig Your Own Way', '`dig <direction>` is here for builders. Walk into a direction with no exit yet and it creates a brand new room right then, wires it back to where you were standing, and steps you straight through -- picking the lowest free room number in your own zone automatically. The new room starts bare; `edit room` gives it real shape.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Account Editing for Admins', '`edit account <name>` lets an Administrator rename any account, reset its password, or see who''s on it and at what level. Every change lands immediately, no Save step to remember.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Sickness Is Now a Thing', 'Drink from a puddle on the ground and you might come away with more than a poison scare -- there''s now a 15% chance of catching a Cold, the Flu, Food Poisoning, or (worst luck) the Plague. A disease saps a little HP every so often while it runs its course, then wears off on its own -- or get it cured at a hospital, see below. Immortals are immune.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Hospitals Open Their Doors', 'Six real hospitals are now staffed and ready -- Tobin City, Amber, Logrus, Brightmoon, a field medic''s post, and Xanesla. Walk up to the doctor and `list` to see what ails you (damaged limbs, active diseases), then `buy <#>` to be cured on the spot for gold. Lost track of where the nearest one is? `goto hospital` points the way from anywhere in the world, same as `goto rent` or `goto surplus`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Component and Commodity Shops Are Finally Stocked', 'Mages and Druids: Camron''s Components (Market Place) now actually sells spellcasting components, so `cast` works again with something to buy rather than nothing to find. Katherine''s Commodities in Brightmoon, plus sister shops in Logrus, Xanesla, and Amber, now carry real metal bars and ingots, and Tuvar''s hides-and-herbs shop is stocked with real animal hides. All buyable and re-sellable, same as any other goods.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Bulletin Boards Are Live', 'The boards mounted on walls across the world finally do something. `read` at one lists what''s posted; `read <#>` shows a message in full. `write <subject> <message>` posts a new one on the spot. A handful of boards need real rank to use -- try anyway and it''ll tell you. If a room happens to have more than one board, name which: `read at <board name>` / `write at <board name> <subject> <message>`.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Much Sicker World', 'Diseases went from a short list to the whole thing -- 26 in all, from a common Cold up through Gangrene and the Plague, each with its own bite and its own price to cure. Poison got an upgrade too: drinking something foul now leaves you genuinely poisoned for a while (check `affects`), not just a single jolt of pain that vanished without a trace -- and yes, a hospital can cure that too now. NPCs can carry these same afflictions as players can; a diseased or poisoned creature nearby will visibly wince from it same as anyone else.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`look` Can Now Count Too', '`kill 2.mob` and `get 2.sword` have worked for a while when more than one thing matches -- `look` was the odd one out. Now it counts the same way: `look board` looks at the first matching thing in the room (or your own gear), `look 2.board` the second, and so on. Same trick works for `drink`/`sip` (a room with two puddles), `open` (two chests), `show`, `sell`, and reading/writing to a specific bulletin board when a room has more than one.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Few Bugs Squashed', '`stat obj/mob/room <name>` now actually searches by name when you don''t give it a vnum, instead of quietly showing you whatever happens to sit at vnum 0. The doctor''s hospital greeting is properly capitalized now ("The Tobin City Doctor...", not "the..."). And `load` (Immortal-only) now warns you -- without ever refusing -- if spawning something pushes its world count past what that prototype was ever meant to have loose at once, so it''s easier to remember to clean up after testing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Torches and Lanterns Actually Work Now', '`light`/`extinguish <item> [held|room]` turn a light source on or off; `refuel <light> <fuel> [held|room]` tops one up from a fuel brick (Lumor''s Illuminations sells them). A lit light burns down over time and eventually goes out on its own -- refuel it before then. You can''t refuel while it''s lit (might explode), and a torch can''t be refueled at all, only replaced. A lit light source shows "(lit)" where it''s sitting so you can actually tell.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'The Lamp-Lighting Boy Is Back on the Job', 'Grimhaven''s lamp-lighting boy (and Brightmoon''s eager page) once again light the streetlamps at dusk and put them out at dawn, wherever they happen to be standing when it happens -- an honest, much smaller version of the original''s scripted patrol route, not the real thing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Few Small Ones', '`prompt gold` joins `prompt hp` -- turn on either or both. `point` (with no target, or aimed at someone) now mentions whatever you''re holding in your primary hand instead of always "pointing around randomly." And `toggle pk` is here: player-vs-player combat only ever works if BOTH sides have opted in with it -- otherwise `attack`/`kill`/`hit` simply can''t find the other player at all. Off by default; mobs are unaffected either way.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Tips Have Arrived', '`tips` shows a random gameplay tip on demand; anyone still on the newbie channel also gets one echoed automatically every so often. `tipedit <text>` / `tipedit list` / `tipedit delete <id>` (53+) manage the pool -- DB-backed like news and help, but kept flat like `bug`/`idea` since a tip is one short sentence, not long-form content.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`cast`/`pray` Grow Real Effects', 'Beyond heal/damage/sanctuary (the original v1), `cast`/`pray` now reuse this session''s own disease/poison/affect work: "cure poison"/"cure disease" genuinely cure (self or, for `pray`, a named target); a wide family of armor/shield/resistance-flavored spells (stone skin, barkskin, self-wards, armor, bless, plasma mirror, ...) all apply the same Sanctuary ward "sanctuary" itself already used; and a Cleric fighting someone can now `pray poison`/`pray disease`/`pray infect` to actually inflict them, mirroring the pre-existing damage branch''s targeting and messaging. Scope-down, same as before: no mana (no pool exists), no elemental-resistance/damage-type model, and teleport/summon/polymorph/invisibility-flavored spells are untouched -- still "nothing happens yet." `skills`/`practice <discipline>` now open each Mage/Druid/Cleric section with a line naming exactly what `cast`/`pray` needs on hand for everything in it.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Every Skill and Spell Now Has a Real Help File', 'Correction to the entry above: the `skills`/`practice` inline reagent note was NOT a substitute for individual help files, as originally (wrongly) scoped -- user: "also no spell helpfiles? that was my instruction. help files for all skills/spells along with required components." Redone properly: `help <skill or spell name>` now works for all 272 entries in skill.c''s roster (generated straight from the SKILLS[] array itself, so it can''t hand-transcription-drift), each naming its class(es)/tier/level and what `cast`/`pray` needs -- a component, a symbol, the real command it already routes through (Thief''s door traps), or an honest "not yet wired to a command" for the many still-placeholder physical skills. New `Requires:` footer line (cmd_help.c, generalized from the existing `Related:` trailing-directive convention) renders this above Related, as asked. Also fixed along the way: `help <topic>` previously only ever looked up the FIRST WORD of a multi-word query, so "help cure poison" silently landed on "cure blindness" (alphabetically first among several same-prefixed "cure ___" topics) -- now tries the full argument as an exact/prefix match first. `disease`''s pre-existing topic got a content refresh too (26 diseases, not the original 4, plus the Cleric prayer that inflicts one).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`engage` Joins `hit`', 'A full alias for `hit` (real multi-round combat, never an instakill even for immortals) -- same one-handler-two-table-rows pattern `attack`/`kill` already use.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Skill/Spell Levels Rescaled to a 1-25-50 Curve', 'Real problem, not cosmetic: dozens of skill.c entries had a min_level ABOVE 50 (up to 99) -- since IMMORTAL_LEVEL_MIN is 51, those were permanently unreachable by any mortal, ever. Rescaled the whole roster with a one-shot script: Combat and Class tiers now span 1-25 per class, Advanced spans 25-50, each class rescaled independently off its own prior min/max (relative order preserved, no reshuffling of which skill unlocks before which within a class). skill_help.sql regenerated to match -- caught a real bug in the process, its own ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE name=name no-op (there to protect hedit edits from a reseed) meant simply reapplying the file after regenerating content silently changed nothing; worked around with an explicit delete-then-reapply for this one-time need. Also fixed two pre-existing test gaps this exposed: an un-paged `skills` read in smoke_test_immortal_castpray.py, and a missing combat_disc_pct in smoke_test_affects.py''s Cleric fixture (unrelated to the rescale, just never noticed until now).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Components Have Charges, Symbols Genuinely Decay', 'User: "how long does each component last? should be getting 10 casts out of each component and the symbols should decay as in sneezy." Both were single-use before, always, regardless. Researched the real source (obj_component.h/.cc, discipline.cc''s requireHolySym()) rather than guessing: the original''s TComponent really does have a charges counter, and TSymbol really does have a decaying strength pool that can shatter mid-prayer. Ported the SHAPE, not the exact numbers (the original''s decay scales off a per-symbol level rating Tobin symbols don''t have): obj.h''s val0/val1 now hold current/max charges (components) or current/max strength (symbols). A component spends exactly 1 charge per cast attempt, destroyed only once the last one''s gone. A symbol loses a random 1-2 strength per prayer/continue attempt, shattering once it runs out -- real variance, not a clean countdown. tobin_migrations.sql seeds every real component/symbol to 10/10, unconditionally -- several symbol rows had huge (up to 1.8 million) leftover val0/val1 from the upstream import that would have flatly contradicted "10 casts" if inherited as-is. New tests/smoke_test_component_charges.py covers both live.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'News Follow-Ups: Edit/Delete In-Game, Unseen-News Notice', 'User 2026-07-17 batch: "edit/delete existing news in-game (addnews only creates); show unseen news at login (per-player last-seen)." `edit news <existing headline>` now preloads the current body ("existing text below", same convention `edit help` already uses) and overwrites it in place on save -- `news_repo_upsert()` replaces the old straight INSERT that just failed on the duplicate title. `edit news delete <headline>` removes an item outright. `edit wiznews` got the identical treatment (same underlying gap, same shared EDIT_NEWS/EDIT_WIZNEWS save path in descriptor.c). New `player.news_last_seen_id` column (tobin_migrations.sql) bookmarks the highest news.id each player has caught up on -- bumped whenever they run `news`, checked at login to show a one-line "There is new news! Type ''news'' to catch up." notice (never a count or id -- house rule stands). tests/smoke_test_news_followups.py.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Look/Prompt: Three Stray Blank Lines Gone', 'User: found three separate extra \\r\\n in everyday output while testing the rebuilt box. (1) `look`''s room description had a spurious blank line before Exits -- root cause was DATA, not the format string: ~95%% of seeded room.description rows carry their own trailing "\\n" (upstream SneezyMUD convention), which stacked with cmd_look.c''s own appended \\r\\n. Fixed by trimming a COPY of the description before rendering (the stored row itself is untouched, so redit/stat/etc. still see the raw text). (2) The blank line cmd_look.c inserted between the Exits line and the room-contents listing is gone -- contents now follow Exits directly. (3) The per-turn game prompt (game_loop.c) was sending "\\r\\n\\r\\n" before "HP: ... > " in both the flags-on and flags-off branches -- double what the original comment says was intended ("insert A \\r\\n before each new prompt"); both now send a single \\r\\n. Verified against tests/smoke_test_exits_display.py, smoke_test_look_capitalization.py, smoke_test_look_equipment.py, smoke_test_look_fixture_order.py, smoke_test_room_stacking.py, smoke_test_parser_display.py -- all still pass.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Thief `peek` Skill: See What Someone Is Carrying', 'User: "a thief skill could be added to attempt a peak at the targets inventory." New `peek <target>` shows a target''s loose CARRIED inventory (not worn -- `look <target>` already covers that) if you know the skill and the roll succeeds; a fumble tips the target off, a clean success stays silent -- same "attempt made, effect not guaranteed" shape as trap mechanics (cmd_trap.c), which this was modeled directly on. New "peek" entry in skill.c (Thief, Combat tier, level 1, right after "steal"). Placed in cmd_table.c right after `pee`, not alphabetically near pray/practice, so an immortal typing bare "pee" keeps meaning the pee command -- "peek" shares its first three letters and cmd_dispatch() resolves a shared abbreviation to whichever entry comes first. Caught a genuinely useful gate detail writing the smoke test: `being_knows_skill()` requires `combat_disc_pct > 0` for ANY Combat-tier skill, not just the level threshold -- a fresh level-1 character can''t use ANY Combat skill (peek included) until they''ve practiced at least once. tests/smoke_test_peek.py.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Player-State Logging: pfile Changes Now Searchable', 'get/drop were already logged (LOG_SILENT, cmd_object.c) -- this closes the other half of the old TODO item ("log get/drop + pfile changes so `log search <name>` tells a player''s story"): `title`, `prompt`, `poofin`/`poofout`, and `bamfin`/`bamfout` each now log a LOG_SILENT line on set/clear, same convention as get/drop. `color` was deliberately left out -- it lives on `account`, not `player`, and gets toggled far more casually (a terminal preference, not a piece of a player''s story). tests/smoke_test_pfile_logging.py.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Trigger Scripts Get `say` and a Real `wait`', 'User pasted a Monty-Python-style market-vendor script ("wait 1 / say Larks'' tongues. / wait 1 / say Wrens'' livers. / ...") and asked to put it on a mob. Neither verb existed -- the vocabulary was echo/echoroom/emote/teleport/give/damage/log, and a script ran start-to-finish synchronously in one pass, no pause primitive at all. Confirmed the direction with the user first (this genuinely expands the trigger system past its own documented "deliberately small" design intent, trigger.sql) before building. `say <text>` renders "<Name> says, ''<text>''" to the room. `wait <seconds>` (1-3600) pauses everything after that line and schedules a real continuation (new trigger_pending_tick(), ~1s pulse cadence) -- the triggering actor is deliberately NOT carried across the pause (may be long gone by the time it resumes); only room/self are, safely re-derived fresh from the trigger''s own target_type/target_vnum rather than holding a raw pointer across an open-ended real-time gap. `aitick` now forces pending waits along too, for deterministic testing. Caught two real bugs along the way: the new pulse registration silently exceeded a fixed MAX_PULSE_PROCESSES limit and got dropped (wait would have just never fired -- bumped 16 to 24), and aitick''s original step ordering could resolve a wait in the very same pass that scheduled it, collapsing the pause into a no-op. tests/smoke_test_trigger_wait.py.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Zone Fix: 1119 Mobs That Were Never Actually Spawning', 'Chasing why a trigger on mob 149 never fired for a real player led somewhere much bigger: the mob was never spawning through the normal zone reset system at all, in ANY zone. Root-caused against the real SneezyMUD C++ source: the upstream zonefile opcode `A <lo> <hi>` rolls a random room number in that range and stores it as the zone''s current "random room" (the original engine''s ZONE_ROOM_RANDOM = -99); any following M/O command whose own room argument is that same -99 sentinel uses the rolled room instead of a literal vnum. Tobin''s zone engine only ever implemented six opcodes (M/O/E/G/P/D) -- `A` was never one of them, so its -99 sentinel was never substituted with anything and always failed to resolve. Confirmed via direct query: 1119 mob-load rows and 25 object-load rows across the whole database use -99 as their room -- all wandering/ambient content by theme (grimhaven youth, scarred tomcat, filthy dog, ugly crow, ...), never meant to sit in one fixed spot, exactly matching what this opcode is for. New zone_cmd_random_room()/zone_resolve_target_room() (src/core/zone.c) close the gap. Verified live: preview''s boot mob/object count jumped from 649/587 to 769/652 with the fix in, and mob 149''s own two -99 rolls landed in different real rooms across repeated resets, as designed.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`scan` Range Trimmed', 'User: "scan works too well, can we cut the range down to 4 or 5 rooms distance?" SCAN_MAX_RANGE dropped from 6 to 5 (cmd_scan.c) -- one fewer "way off in the distance" tier.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Zone Reset: World-Wide max_exist Now Actually Enforced', 'User: "i used scan and got 50-60 mobs listed." A real regression the same-day A-opcode fix exposed: a ZONE_ROOM_RANDOM (-99) mob lands in a different room every reset, so the existing per-room cap (is THIS room already full of this vnum) was trivially satisfied every time -- confirmed live, zone 100''s periodic reset alone was adding ~190 fresh mob instances on top of whatever already existed, on every single firing. The original engine gates this with a world-wide instance-count check; ported as zone_world_count() plus a check in zone_cmd_load_mob()/zone_cmd_load_obj_ground() before creating anything, refusing over cap rather than just warning (unlike the immortal `load` command''s existing warn-only version). Verified live: forced zone 100 to reset 3 times running, then scanned its full random-room range room by room -- mob 149 (its own cap is 8) sat at exactly 8 live instances, not climbing. A production restart was needed alongside this fix, not instead of it -- nothing persists live mob/object state, so a fresh boot is what actually clears out the bloat the running process had already built up; the code fix is what stops it recurring.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Keys Unlocking Doors -- and Containers', 'TODO.md''s "Keys unlocking doors" item, unblocked now that the object system exists. New `lock`/`unlock <direction>` and `lock`/`unlock <container>` (cmd_lock.c). Matching rule confirmed against the real SneezyMUD C++ source (has_key()/keyCheck(), misc/movement.cc) before writing a line of code: a key is identified by its OWN object vnum, NOT by any val[] field on the key itself -- a door''s `roomexit.key_num` (loaded and silently discarded until now -- room_t gained an `exit_key[]` field to actually carry it) or a container''s `val[2]` names the vnum a carried object must have. Both must be closed first before `lock` will touch them; `unlock` only needs the key. Real, immediately testable seeded data: 1,141 doors and dozens of containers across the live DB already carry working key_num/val[2] values that were simply inert until this. Also fixed two stale comments left over from before this landed (obj.h''s KEY val[0] doc was flatly wrong -- val[] is unused, matching-by-vnum confirmed against real seeded key rows; cmd_open.c''s "deferred" note). New tests/smoke_test_keys.py (23 checks: keyhole-less doors, no/wrong/right key on both a door and a container, lock-requires-closed, persistence to roomexit.condition_flag). Table entries verified with tests/tools/cmd_abbrev_check.py (also patched -- it was missing three level macros added by later sessions and errored out before this).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Mid-Fight HP Now Survives a Disconnect', 'TODO.md''s "Mid-fight persistence" item. HP was previously only saved at defeat/quit (`descriptor_leave_to_menu()`) -- a mid-fight disconnect (crash, or a losing player quietly pulling the plug) reloaded at whatever HP was last saved BEFORE the fight even started, silently undoing all damage taken. `combat_process_run()` (combat.c) now calls the existing `player_progress_save()` for both PC participants after every round the fight is still ongoing, not just at defeat -- the exact same call the defeat/gold-drop paths already made, no new persistence mechanism invented. Limb HP is explicitly NOT covered -- it isn''t persisted at all yet by ANY path, defeat included; that remains the separate "Meaningful limb damage" item. Verified with a real crash simulation, not just a code read: new tests/smoke_test_mid_fight_persist.py fights a deliberately tanky sandbox mob (2025 HP so it survives many rounds without dying, weak enough not to threaten the 300-HP test PC) until real damage lands, then hard-closes the socket with no `quit!` and reconnects -- HP landed exactly where it was the instant before the disconnect (298/300 in the observed run), not reset to the pre-fight max.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Split Gold on Kill -- Solo Case', 'TODO.md''s "Split victim''s gold among the group on kill" item, solo half. User: "upon death get all gold from the victim and split it between all group members if groupped." `combat_defeat()` (combat.c): a PC loser''s entire wallet transfers to a non-immortal PC winner, right alongside the existing HP-reset-to-half/limb-heal defeat handling -- reuses the loser''s existing `player_progress_save()` call plus the winner''s existing XP-block save (gold and XP land on the same struct before that save fires, so no new DB write was added for this). Same non-immortal-winner gate as the pre-existing mob-gold-drop path, so an immortal''s instakill never triggers it; PK combat already requires both sides to have opted in (`toggle pk`), so there is no non-consensual path here. The "if grouped" split half remains genuinely blocked on the not-yet-built group/party system -- this lands as the natural insertion point for it once that exists. New tests/smoke_test_pk_gold.py (a 1-HP loser so the very first landed hit resolves the fight deterministically).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Meaningful Limb Damage -- Auditing What Already Shipped', 'TODO.md''s "Meaningful limb damage" item. User: "make limb damage mean something. if you have a limb decapitated it shouldnt be at 100% limb health. make individual limb hits actually hurt." Turned out most of this already existed from earlier sessions and just was never checked off: per-limb hit weighting, escalating injury-tier messages (combat + score), a destroyed limb already penalizing its OWNER''s own hit chance, and Hospital already giving a real mid-game cure. The genuine gap: `combat_strike()` only ever checked the ATTACKER''s destroyed limb -- a maimed defender wasn''t any easier to hit, only worse at fighting back. Added the mirror check (combat.c), same flat non-stacking amount. On the "still shows 100%" half: a destroyed limb DOES correctly read 0% while a fight is ongoing -- it only heals back after the fight ends because combat defeat has always fully healed every limb as part of its "revived at half HP" recovery, same as HP itself. That''s deliberate soft-respawn design (PC death isn''t permadeath here), not a bug -- documented explicitly in being.h''s `being_has_destroyed_limb()` doc comment and combat.c so it doesn''t get mistaken for one again. Also corrected a stale comment in the same spot claiming "no hospital system yet" -- Hospital shipped 2026-07-18.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Ignore Lists', 'First item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit''s remaining-work list (user audited the published artifact and asked to work through the rest). Checked Sneezy''s own communication-system.md doc before building anything: the original''s ignoreList blocks by descriptor/name/whole-account across say/tell/whisper/shout/grouptell/emote/socials, silent-to-sender failure. Scoped to what Tobin actually has: a flat per-character name list (new `player_ignore` table, `ignore_repo.h`/`.c`), checked only by `tell`/`whisper` -- the broadcast channels (say/socials) would need a per-listener filter loop at every room-echo call site for comparatively little value, since those aren''t where unwanted DMs actually come from. `ignore [<name>]`/`unignore <name>` (cmd_ignore.c), up to 30 entries. A blocked sender''s tell/whisper still reports success on their own screen, matching the original''s documented behavior exactly. New tests/smoke_test_ignore.py (13 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Possess/Return: Puppet a Mob''s Body', 'Second item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Switch / return"). Checked Sneezy''s own 23-snoop-switch.md doc first: admin switch is a raw descriptor-pointer swap, no stat transfer, no transformation message -- that''s the separate spell-driven polymorph flavor (its own, still-open "Transformation" audit item). Named `possess` since `switch` already means something else in Tobin (swap held items). `possess <mob>` (59+)/`return` (cmd_possess.c) -- new `descriptor_t.possess_original` holds the immortal''s own PC while `character` points at the puppet; the original goes temporarily desc==NULL, the exact same shape a real link-drop already leaves a body in. Two real bugs caught testing this, not just guessed from the doc: cmd_dispatch()''s level gate was reading the PUPPET''s seeded level (often 1), which would have made `return` itself invisible and permanently stranded an immortal inside whatever they possessed -- fixed by gating on the real immortal''s level while possessing. And a disconnect mid-possession was about to run the link-drop announce/log/parking against the mob instead of the immortal -- fixed by restoring the real character first, at the top of descriptor_destroy(). `quit!` while possessing is refused outright (return first). New tests/smoke_test_possess.py (9 checks, including recovery from a hard disconnect mid-possession).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'wiznet @<level> Targeting', 'Third item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("OOC channels"). Checked Sneezy''s own communication-system.md doc: `wiznet` already covered the real doCommune()''s default behavior (every online immortal, sender included); the one documented gap was the `@<level>` prefix that narrows delivery to gods at or above that level. Added the equivalent (`wiznet @<level> <message>`, composes with the `;` shorthand). Deliberately did NOT add a separate "commune" command -- that would just duplicate wiznet; the real gap was the targeting feature. New tests/smoke_test_wiznet.py (4 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Group / Party System', 'Fourth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list. Checked Sneezy''s own 09-group-party.md doc first, then scoped it down deliberately (documented inline in being.h): no leader-succession algorithm (a group just dissolves if the leader leaves/dies, not the original''s two-pass reformGroup()); no per-player configurable 1-10 `group share` money factor -- gold splits EVENLY instead; XP shares are level-weighted, a simplification of the original''s mob_exp()-based share; no quest-flag or charm/mount interaction checks; `follow` only refuses a direct circular pair, not a full chain walk. New `master`/`grouped`/`followers[]` fields on being_t -- live in-memory only, same rule as `fighting`, except a raw disconnect deliberately does NOT clear them so a group survives a member briefly dropping link, matching the original. `follow <name>`/`stop`/`group [<name>|all]` (leader-only to add)/`split <amount>` (leader-only, divides evenly, refuses a share that would round to 0) -- cmd_group.c. combat.c''s new group_recipients() helper feeds the existing XP-award, PK gold-steal, and mob-gold-drop blocks so a grouped, in-room, non-immortal winner''s kill benefits the whole party, while the exact solo-winner math/messages stay untouched when nobody''s grouped (regression-verified clean against the existing PK-gold and level-up-HP smoke tests). Two real test-infrastructure lessons hit along the way: a direct SQL update on an already-connected character isn''t seen in memory without a relog, and `quit!` itself saves the player -- so a direct SQL update has to run AFTER the quit, not before, or the save silently clobbers it. New tests/smoke_test_group.py (14 checks across all 6 phases).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Hunger, Thirst, and Age', 'Fifth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list. Checked Sneezy''s own vital-statistics.md doc first, then scoped it down hard: the original''s cryptic 0-24 FULL/THIRST units become a plain 0-100 percent; PEE/POOP/DRUNK conditions are out of scope; drain is a flat rate, not the original''s terrain-weighted two-stage-random roll (terrain factors belong to the still-open "Terrain movement cost" item). Age (user, AskUserQuestion 2026-07-19): track + display only, explicitly not the original''s full graf()-interpolated age-based stat-curve system across 6 stats with a vampire exemption -- real machinery for a mostly-cosmetic payoff here. New progress_t.hunger/thirst (-1 = immortal-immune) and .birth_time. Drains 1 hunger + 1 thirst every ~60s tick; starvation (hunger OR thirst at 0) costs 1 HP per tick, floored at 1 -- same "never lethal outside real combat" precedent cmd_sip.c''s poison roll already set, since real starvation death waits on the still-open "Death processing" item. New `eat <food>` restores hunger by the food object''s own val0 (already well-populated in the real seed, 1-24) and fully consumes it in one bite, sidestepping a real upstream data gap (every seeded FOOD''s val1 "current units" is uniformly 0) instead of migrating around it. `drink`/`sip` now also raise thirst -- a fountain fully refills it, a puddle or sip only partly. `score` shows Hunger/Thirst as words and a real-elapsed-time Age line; immortals see "Immune". `aitick` now also forces a vitals tick, so tests don''t wait on real time. New tests/smoke_test_vitals.py (13 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'XP Loss on Death', 'Sixth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Death processing"). Checked Sneezy''s own death-processing.md doc first: real death there is a whole permadeath pipeline (die() -> rawKill() -> makeCorpse()) with a corpse and a resurrection spell to revive it. Asked before building anything (AskUserQuestion) since Tobin''s PC death was already NOT permadeath -- combat_defeat() already half-heals HP, fully heals limbs, and ejects the loser to the account menu, same soft-respawn design as before this session. Answer: XP loss only -- "resurrection" is already covered by the existing relog flow, so no corpse/spell system was built. combat_defeat() (combat.c) now docks XP on a PC''s death: min(20% of current XP, XP banked past the current level''s own threshold) -- adapted from Sneezy''s own min(20%, 25*mob_exp(level)), but the cap here needs no separate mob-XP curve (just the existing progress_xp_for_level()) and guarantees a death can never de-level anyone. A PvP death (mutual `toggle pk`) divides the result by 10; a death to a mob gets the full penalty. Immortals never lose XP. New tests/smoke_test_death_xploss.py (6 checks: full 20% loss with ample banked XP, the de-level-safety cap kicking in with only a little banked, and the PvP 1/10th reduction) -- regression-verified against smoke_test_pk_gold.py, smoke_test_levelup_hp.py, and smoke_test_group.py, which all also exercise combat_defeat().')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Junk and Identify', 'Seventh item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Object manipulation depth"). `sacrifice` was skipped after asking (AskUserQuestion) -- it''s entirely a Shaman-class skill tied to a `lifeforce` resource and totems in the original, and Tobin has none of those, so there was no honest way to keep the verb without inventing a whole system nothing else needs. `junk <item>` is a straight port of the original''s own command (not skill-gated there either): destroys a carried, loose item for good, same scope as `drop`. `identify <item>` is new, a plain command (not a spell, same tier as `examine`) showing a carried item''s real category-specific stats. Building it surfaced two real mismatches between obj.h''s val[] doc comment and reality, fixed by showing only what''s mechanically real: a weapon''s val0/val1 are NOT damage dice despite the doc comment (verified against the real seed -- one dart carries val0=4626, val1=2073, nonsense as dice -- and against combat.c''s actual formula, which never reads either field), so identify shows the real hitroll/damroll bonus instead; and a magic item''s val0 "charges" is likewise unreliable (5 on potions, a nonsense 25650 on one scroll) and unread by any code yet (no use/zap/quaff command exists), so identify says so honestly instead of printing a possibly-garbage number. Also found and fixed a real latent bug: `eat` (previous audit item) destroyed the eaten food but never saved the resulting inventory change, so a reconnect before any other inventory-touching action would resurrect the "eaten" item from its still-persisted DB row -- `junk` would have had the identical gap. Both now save immediately after destroying, matching the pattern cmd_shop.c''s sell path already used correctly. New tests/smoke_test_objmanip.py (10 checks, including a regression check for the resurrection bug on both commands).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Quest System (Infrastructure)', 'Eighth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list. Checked Sneezy''s own quest-system.md doc first: the real system is a fixed 454-bit array tied entirely to hand-authored content that doesn''t exist here -- specific quests like "Avenger"/"Silverclaw"/"Holy Devastator", named NPCs, dialogue trees driven by spec procedures. Porting the bit array itself would just be 454 meaningless numbers with nothing behind them, and Tobin''s trigger-script language has no conditional branching yet to drive quest logic even with new storage in place. Asked the user (AskUserQuestion) rather than inventing bespoke quest content or a whole scripting layer; answer was infrastructure only. Ported the SHAPE of the original instead of its bit numbers: a player''s stage in a named quest, visible only where an immortal has written a description for that exact stage -- same "only bits with a help file are visible" rule, DB rows instead of files. New `quest [<name>]` (mortal): bare form lists quests with a written description for the player''s current stage; `quest <name>` shows the full description. `questdef <name> <stage> <text>` (builder tier) writes/replaces a stage''s description. Advancing a player''s stage is a new `quest` field on the existing `set <name> <field> <value>` -- manual, since there''s no scripting hook yet to automate it. New tests/smoke_test_quest.py (9 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Weather, and Darkness That Actually Matters', 'Ninth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list. Checked Sneezy''s own Weather class first: a real barometric-pressure random walk, a moon-phase cycle, per-room wetness, season-aware sunrise/sunset -- trimmed to a single world-wide sky state (clear/cloudy/rainy/stormy) advanced by a simple weighted table instead of a pressure simulation. New `weather` command shows the current condition, a flavor hint (not a real forecast), and day/night; changes announce world-wide, same precedent gametime.c''s own noon/midnight lines already use. The real payoff is the other half: gametime_is_daytime() already existed (the lamplighter mob used it) but nothing gated visibility on it until now. A room is dark for anyone without an active light source unless it''s flagged ALWAYS-LIT/INDOORS or it''s daytime -- wired into both `look` and `exits`, which is what finally makes carried light sources matter. Building this surfaced a real cross-test hazard: since darkness is brand new, any existing test with a plain unflagged sandbox room that calls look/exits on it can now intermittently fail depending on the server''s current in-game time of day. One real casualty found and fixed (smoke_test_look_equipment.py); a broader audit of the rest of the suite was spun off as a follow-up rather than expanding this session further. New tests/smoke_test_weather.py (8 checks) -- it also restores a neutral daytime hour when it finishes, so it doesn''t leave the shared server clock stuck at night for whatever test runs next.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Extra Descriptions: look <keyword> Now Reveals Hidden Room Detail', 'Follow-up work found while auditing the darkness gate for cross-test hazards (previous entry): TODO.md''s "redit Extra Descriptions" item noted the upstream `roomextra` table already existed with real content, but nothing in Tobin actually read it -- 8,861 seeded rows (room vnum, a space-separated keyword list, a description) sitting completely inert. `look <keyword>` (cmd_look.c''s look_at_target()) now checks a room''s roomextra rows after a real PC/mob/object match misses, same case-insensitive per-word prefix matching every other keyword lookup in this codebase already uses. Verified live against real content, not just the sandbox test -- room 3''s seeded "calendar" extra desc ("Grumble car payment due on the 25th") comes back correctly. New tests/smoke_test_extra_desc.py (5 checks). Still open: the BUILDER-facing half -- an in-game `redit` list/add/edit/delete for authoring new ones, rather than direct SQL.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Aggressive Mobs Now Give Chase', 'Tenth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Monster AI & behavior (pursuit)"). Checked Sneezy''s own 14-monster-ai-behavior.md doc first: the real system is a huge opinion/emotion model (suspicion/greed/malice/anger, per-mob hate and fear memory, faction territorial combat) plus a real multi-room hunt() with persistence counters and pathfinding -- none of that exists in Tobin, and building it wholesale was way out of scope for one audit item. Scoped "(pursuit)" down to exactly what it names: an ACT_AGGRESSIVE mob a player successfully flees from now gets one immediate, single-room, 50% chance to follow into the next room and resume the fight, before the fleeing player''s own `look` renders -- so a successful chase is already standing there in the room description, not a surprise arrival after the fact. No persistence, no cross-tick hunting state, no real multi-room hunt; a PC opponent from PK combat never gives chase this way. New tests/smoke_test_pursuit.py (2 checks: an aggressive mob eventually chases across repeated flee attempts; a non-aggressive mob never does) -- verified the underlying dice are genuinely random, not accidentally deterministic, by re-running it 5 times and confirming real variance in how many attempts it took (1, 1, 1, 3, 4). Regression-checked against smoke_test_mob_ai.py and smoke_test_group.py, which also exercise mob AI and combat.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Vitality: Walking Costs Something Now', 'Eleventh item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Vitality stat + Terrain movement cost"). Vitality doesn''t exist in the original either -- its "moves" resource (misc/limits.cc''s getMaxMove()/moveGain()) is CON-and-level-derived but otherwise unnamed; Vitality is a Tobin-original name closing the "Depends on Vitality" TODO.md fragment this item''s own audit note pointed at. New `vit`/`max_vit` on every character (progress_t, being.h) -- 50 base + CON bonus + 2/level, shown in `score` and now toggleable into the prompt (`prompt vit`, previously blocked alongside mana/piety), regenerates on the same weight-by-position tick as HP (regen.c). Terrain cost: the original''s TerrainInfo[61] gives every sector its own hand-tuned movement cost; Tobin has no per-sector content to justify that granularity, so `sector_move_cost()` (room.c) buckets all 61 sectors into 6 cost tiers by name (roads/cities cheapest, lava/solid rock/fire priciest), reusing the same substring-grouping precedent `sector_color()` already established. `north`/`east`/etc (cmd_move.c) now charge the average of the source and destination sector''s cost, straight out of the average-of-two-sectors rule -- refusing the move outright ("You are too exhausted to go that way") rather than letting vit go negative, same hard-gate shape as the door/fighting checks already there. No swim/fly/mount modifiers yet -- those stay blocked on the still-open "Water, drowning, flight" and "Mount / riding" items; water sectors are just an expensive-but-walkable tier for now. Immortals are exempt, same precedent as hunger/thirst immunity. New tests/smoke_test_vitality_terrain.py (10 checks) -- building it surfaced a real timing gotcha worth knowing about: regen_tick_run() heals vit roughly every 5 real seconds but ONLY in memory (it never itself saves to the DB), so a naive DB-read-based test can flake against a stray regen tick a live `score` read doesn''t. Also fixed a stale, unrelated assertion in smoke_test_parser_display.py (its own "qu should NOT reach quit" check predated the `quest` command and needed updating to match "qu" now legitimately reaching quest instead).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Drowning Is Now Real -- And Lethal', 'Twelfth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Water, drowning, flight"). Checked Sneezy''s own movement-terrain-navigation doc first: AFF_SWIM discounts water-sector movement cost, a procCharDrowning scheduler deals 1d10 every 3.6 real seconds to anyone underwater without AFF_WATERBREATH -- genuinely lethal -- and AFF_FLYING quarters movement cost and bypasses drowning entirely. Two Mage spells that were already listed but unimplemented now do something real: `cast gills of flesh` (level 9) grants water breathing, `cast levitate` (level 11) grants flight (both new AFFECT_ types, same shape as the existing Sanctuary ward -- check `affects` to see remaining duration). A new `sector_is_underwater()` distinguishes true UNDERWATER sectors from merely-wet surface water (ocean/river surface/iceflow) -- only the former drowns you. The drowning roll itself is the SAME 1d10 the original uses, just on Tobin''s existing ~60s vitals tick instead of the original''s 3.6s one, so it''s already far gentler in practice. Per AskUserQuestion with the user: unlike hunger/thirst/poison''s non-lethal floor-at-1 convention, drowning can genuinely kill -- same soft-respawn handling (half HP, healed limbs, XP loss, corpse, back to the account menu) as a real combat death, just with no killer to credit. New tests/smoke_test_water_drowning_flight.py (13 checks) covers all three pieces, including confirming an unprotected mortal actually drowns within a bounded number of forced ticks.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: wiznews (and news) Silently Cutting Off Mid-Sentence', 'User report, fixed same day given the severity ("wiznews bug with the pager/long output that freezes the mud"). Real bug, not a false alarm: `descriptor_page_start()`''s pager copied its whole source text into a fixed 16KB buffer before doling it out a page at a time -- anything longer than that got silently TRUNCATED, no warning, often mid-word. Both `wiznews` and the public `news` channel build up to 101KB of real content (a *previous* fix for the exact same class of overflow at an even smaller size) -- but that fix never reached the pager''s own buffer, so once either feed grew past ~16KB (both have, after everything posted this session alone) reading through it would just abruptly stop and dump you back at a normal prompt with the rest of the feed gone, no error, nothing to indicate it was cut off. That''s not a true infinite hang, but it is exactly the kind of "something''s clearly broken and stuck" experience that reads as the mud freezing. Fixed by growing the pager''s buffer to comfortably clear both feeds'' size with real headroom for future growth. Affects any command that pages long output, not just wiznews -- `news`/`help`/`who`/`skills` all share the same pager and all benefit from the same fix.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: Weather Was Leaking Indoors', 'User report: "weather should not affect rooms that are flagged indoors." Real bug -- the weather-change announcement ("Clouds begin to gather overhead", "It begins to rain", ...) was broadcast to every connected character regardless of what room they were standing in, so someone tucked away inside a building would still hear about clouds gathering overhead. Fixed with the exact same INDOORS-flag check the darkness half of the weather system already uses for `look`/`exits` -- a room flagged indoors simply does not receive the announcement anymore. A torchlit indoor room still gets the exemption (it just is not dark, that is a separate question from being indoors), matching how ALWAYS-LIT and INDOORS have always meant two different things everywhere else in the game.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Tips Channel Now Has Its Own Toggle', 'User request: "tips channel should be a toggle to shut it off or turn it on again." Previously the only way to stop the periodic "Tip:" echoes was `toggle newbie`, which also dropped you off the newbie help channel entirely -- a much bigger side effect than just wanting quieter tips. Added a standalone `toggle tips` switch (on by default) that only silences the tip echoes, leaving the newbie channel itself untouched.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: The `level` Command', 'User request: "a level command that will display when your due for a gain in level, You have X experience and need X experience to level." `level` now tells you exactly that -- current experience, and how much more you need to reach the next level. At the mortal cap (level 50) or as an immortal, it tells you why there is nothing more to grind toward instead of showing a nonsensical number.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: Animals No Longer Carry Gold', 'User: "animal races should not have wealth, that doesnt make sense." Killing a mundane real-world creature -- a rat, a bear, a bird, a snake, and everything else in that vein -- no longer hands you a purse of coins on the kill; that never made much sense for an ordinary animal to be carrying. Fantastical or sapient monsters (dragons, orcs, goblins, undead, demons, and so on) are unaffected -- still exactly as lootable as before. Experience earned from the kill is unaffected either way.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Prompt: exp, expneed, and prompt all', 'User request: expand the customizable prompt beyond hp/gold/vit. `prompt exp` shows your current experience, `prompt expneed` shows how much more you need to reach the next level (same math as the `level` command). New `prompt all` turns on every available stat at once instead of toggling them one at a time. Mana and piety are still not available -- those resources do not exist in the game yet.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Looking at Someone Now Shows Their Inventory (Immortals Only)', 'User request: "immortals can see inventory when looking at a mob or player and can also see the contents of any container they carry." `look <target>` now appends a carried-inventory section for immortal viewers, right after the equipment listing -- items already shown as worn are not duplicated. An open container among their carried items shows what is inside it too, one level deep; a closed one just shows "(closed)". Mortals looking at someone else see no change at all.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'redit Can Now Author Extra Descriptions', 'The builder half of extra descriptions is here. `edit room`''s menu 8 opens a new submenu: list what a room already has, add a new one (type its keywords, then write the description in the usual line editor), rename an existing one''s keywords, edit its description text, delete one, or wipe every extra description on the room at once. Unlike the rest of `edit room`, these changes save immediately -- there is no separate Save step for them. A keyword set that already exists on that room cannot be reused for a different entry; renaming into a collision is refused, cleanly, with nothing lost. Anything written this way shows up right away to players via `look <keyword>`, the read-only half that shipped last session.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Socials Moved to the Database, and the Full Original Set Is Back', 'The old hardcoded list of emotes has been replaced with a proper database table, and the complete original set has been ported over -- a huge jump from the small handful we had before. Every one of them was checked carefully against the original game''s files rather than guessed at, including which ones require standing (or at least sitting up) to use, and how each one addresses you, the other player, and onlookers by name or pronoun. A couple of long-standing gaps got fixed along the way: aiming an emote at yourself now gets its own proper reaction instead of just repeating the plain version, and the `socials` list -- being so much longer now -- pages through screen by screen instead of dumping everything at once. Still to come: an in-game editor for builders to add or tweak socials without touching the database directly. One heads-up from testing this live tonight: our usual way of restarting the server on this box briefly took production down twice while chasing down two small bugs the bigger test suite caught, and it dropped the one player who happened to be online at the time. We do not currently have a safe way to do a zero-downtime restart (a "copyover") on this box without someone''s own logged-in immortal doing it by hand -- worth setting up before the next deploy like this.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: edsocial, an In-Game Editor for Socials', 'The second half of last night''s socials work is here -- `edit social [name]` (level 55+), the missing piece now that every social lives in the database instead of hardcoded. Leave off the name to see the whole list; typing an existing one opens its detail screen, or type `new` to create one from scratch. From there you can rewrite any of its eight message variants (what you see, what onlookers see, what a target sees, and the dedicated messages for targeting yourself), toggle whether the message is hidden from someone who can''t see the actor (a real field from the original game, though it does nothing yet -- we don''t have invisibility in yet), change the minimum position needed to use it, rename it, or delete it outright. Nothing here needs a Save step or a server restart -- every change takes effect immediately, live, the moment you make it. While building this, a completely unrelated but serious bug turned up and got fixed the same night: an immortal typing plain `skills` (the "show every class, everything unlocked" view) was silently crashing the server, because that view''s buffer was too small for the full skill list and the overflow wasn''t being caught -- see the separate write-up below.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: An Immortal Typing `skills` Was Crashing the Server', 'Found live, the hard way, mid-testing: any immortal running the plain `skills` command (the view that shows every class''s entire skill list at once, fully unlocked) reliably crashed the whole server. The list has grown to roughly three hundred entries across every class, and the buffer that view builds its output in was sized for a much smaller game -- once it filled up, the code kept writing past the end of it instead of stopping cleanly, which is what actually brought the server down rather than just cutting the list short. Fixed on both counts: the buffer is now sized with real headroom for how big the game has actually gotten, and every place that builds up this kind of list now refuses to write past the end no matter how much content there ends up being, so a full server crash from this class of bug shouldn''t be possible again anywhere else it''s used. Caught and confirmed via a debugger attached to the live process during testing, not guessed at from a vague symptom -- we''re now keeping a debugger attached by habit while testing on these boxes going forward, specifically so a crash like this gets an exact cause immediately instead of just looking like a mysterious wave of unrelated test failures.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Combat Skills: bash, kick, disarm, and a Passive parry', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item. `bash` (Warrior), `kick` (Thief/Monk), and `disarm` (Warrior/Thief/Monk) are new mid-fight commands -- extra actions layered on top of the automatic per-round exchange, not a replacement for it -- each gated by a single skill_roll_success() roll (same learn-by-doing shape cast/pray/peek already use) and costing the attacker a lag-style wait regardless of outcome, matching the original''s addSkillLag() always firing win or lose. A successful bash also knocks the defender down and costs THEM a round; the original''s own real bash deals no direct damage at all ("should NOT cause loss of attacks, or do damage; only prevent skill-use"), a deliberate, disclosed deviation kept because a no-op success reads as broken rather than faithful. `parry` has no player command at all -- checked Sneezy''s own source first and its real `parry` command is a disabled stub (`doParry()` just prints it isn''t supported); the actual mechanic is entirely passive, wired directly into the core hit-resolution chain with no cooldown, so that''s exactly how it''s ported here too (Warrior only, proficiency quartered so full proficiency caps near a 25% block chance). New shared `combat_apply_skill_damage()` handles the "this damage might finish the fight, and if the loser is a mob it just got destroy()''d" hazard bash/kick share with the automatic combat path. New `tests/smoke_test_skillcombat.py` (12 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Linkdead Bodies Now Auto-Purge After 5 Minutes', 'TODO.md priority item, found live: a linkdead PC''s in-memory body was staying fully resident in its room forever -- nothing ever cleaned one up except that same account reconnecting, a manual `purge linkdead`, or a process restart. Confirmed as a real, measurable server slowdown twice in one day, independently, on both boxes -- accumulated zombie characters (mostly from smoke tests that raw-close a socket instead of `quit`ting) bogging down the combat pulse loop for everyone. Checked the original game''s own `nukeLdead()` first: it force-saves a linkdead character before stripping/freeing them. Asked the user directly which behavior Tobin should match, rather than defaulting silently -- chose save-then-destroy, same as the original, over discard-only (which is what Tobin''s existing on-demand `purge linkdead` already does, and still does -- this is a separate, additional, automatic path). Flat 5 minutes for everyone here, not the original''s 15-minute-mortal/60-minute-immortal split. Runtime-configurable (`TOBIN_LINKDEAD_PURGE_SECONDS`) specifically so both halves -- the removal AND the save actually capturing current, not stale, state -- could be verified live with a shortened threshold rather than just trusting the build. One disclosed trade-off worth knowing: an admin DB edit made to a character that''s been linkdead 5+ minutes or more can still be overwritten by their stale pre-disconnect data at the moment of purge -- the same narrow risk the game''s normal link-drop handling already accepts for everyday disconnects, just now also reachable 5 minutes later by this new path.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Offensive Spells Got Real Breadth', 'Cast and pray''s attack spells had three quiet gaps since they first landed: every single one dealt the exact same flat amount of damage no matter which spell it was (a starting Mage''s weakest bolt hit exactly as hard as a level-50 caster''s strongest one), they could only ever be aimed at whoever you were already fighting -- there was no way to open a fight with a spell at all, and `cast` in particular had no way to name a target the way `pray` always could -- and a kill landed this way skipped the normal death handling entirely. All three are fixed. Damage now scales with the spell itself, not a flat number. `cast <spell> <target>` works now, and either command can open a brand-new fight with someone standing right there. And several spells that always claimed to be "area-effect" in their own description -- fireball, tsunami, hellfire, plague of locusts, earthquake, and others -- actually hit everyone else in the room now instead of quietly acting single-target the whole time, matching what they said on the label from day one. A private note for whoever picks up testing next: an early draft of this briefly introduced a real bug where a plain self-heal prayer would have healed your opponent instead of you if you were already mid-fight -- caught and fixed before it ever shipped, by re-checking the design rather than waiting for a test to catch it, but worth knowing the shape of the mistake in case anything like it turns up again.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Magic Items: Real Equipment Bonuses, and a New use Command', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item, the user''s explicit "full system" pick over two smaller options offered: equipment stat/AC affects, plus usable wands, staves, and scrolls. The seeded objaffect table already carried real per-item bonus data (stat rows, an AC row, an HP row, a Vitality row), and was already half-wired for weapon hit/damroll -- so the same table now feeds a worn item''s stat/AC/HP/Vitality bonus too, cached on the object at creation and applied/reversed on wear/remove. Two real things had to be caught by checking real data rather than assuming: (1) every real AC row in that table is negative, while Tobin''s own armor-class convention is the opposite (higher is better) -- import negates the sign; (2) an AC bonus initially only applied to items in the true "armor" category, silently dropping the bonus for a ring or other jewelry-category item that also carried a real AC row -- caught during live manual verification with a real seeded ring, then fixed so real AC data applies no matter what category the item collapsed into. For wands/staves/scrolls: the upstream val[] fields on these items turned out to be unreliable import noise (a comment on `identify` already flagged this from an earlier audit item -- a nonsense charge count on a real scroll), so charges/spell now live in a fresh Tobin-owned table instead of trusting that data. New `use <item> [target]` command: a scroll applies its effect once and is destroyed; a wand is charge-limited and targeted (a person in the room, or whoever you''re already fighting/yourself by default); a staff is charge-limited and always room-wide, hitting every other being present. The effect itself reuses the same heal/protective-ward/single-target-damage dispatch cast/pray already have, keyed off the stored spell''s own description, rather than a third copy of that logic. Deliberately not attempted this round: potions (a stretch for `use` -- would fit `drink`/`quaff` better, a separate command), a recharge mechanic for empty wands/staves (needs a future `edobject`-style tool), and mana costs (nothing in Tobin has a mana pool yet). New `tests/smoke_test_magic_items.py` (13 checks: real-data AC/HP affects applying and reverting cleanly on wear/remove, wand charges depleting and refusing further use, staff room-wide effect, scroll single-use destruction).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Object Maintenance, Part 1: Real Decay Timers and Combat-Broken Gear', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item, the user''s "full system" pick -- this session landed the first half (decay timers + combat structure damage); the repair-shop economy and per-class repair skills are carried to a follow-up session rather than rushed. The upstream `obj` table''s own real `decay` column was already being loaded but never actually used -- now a real ~60-second tick counts it down for anything sitting on a room floor (corpses, severed limbs, and now general room-floor objects), destroying it and relocating any contents to the room first when it hits zero. Caught a real hazard while building this, not shipped as one: that column''s schema default was 0 ("decays this tick"), which is backwards for any insert that does not mention it explicitly -- a good three dozen existing test fixtures do exactly that, and worse, our own zone-placed persistent world objects route through the identical creation path, so roughly five hundred real seeded rows with that same default would have started vanishing from the live world within about a minute of every zone reset. Fixed with a migration changing the default going forward, plus an explicit override at every zone-placement call site so persistent world content is never subject to it regardless. Separately: equipped gear can now actually break in a fight -- roughly one landed hit in three has a chance of wearing down whatever is worn on the limb that got hit, and at zero durability it is destroyed outright, any magical bonus it was granting reverses, and a ruined "scraps of" item is left behind in its place. Worth flagging for whoever picks up testing next: verifying this live turned up a real, reproducible test-harness quirk, not a game bug -- a test connection that sits open just polling for new output (no further commands sent) for 30+ seconds reliably gets disconnected when the test script driving it is run as a normal foreground SSH command, but the exact same connection pattern is fine when that same script is instead launched fully detached in the background and polled separately. If a live verification session ever sees a mysterious "lost its link" mid-test again, try that before assuming it is a real bug.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Object Maintenance, Part 2: Repair Skill and Repair-Shop Tickets', 'Closes out the Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item Part 1 left half-done. Checked the real upstream `misc/repair.cc`/`disc/disc_warrior_blacksmithing.cc` first: a mature, file-backed ticket system with a real-time repair delay and genuinely per-MATERIAL repair skills (blacksmithing for metal, separate skills for organic/holy/magical/crystal/etc.). Tobin has no material-property system yet to gate that breadth on -- its own still-open audit item, deliberately not pulled forward just to unblock this one -- so this shipped Tobin-scale: one `repair` skill (Warrior, level 5) for self-repair, and a database-backed claim ticket instead of a physical note object, with no real-time delay (a repaired item at a shop is ready the moment you pay, not hours later). New: `player_inventory` now persists a carried item''s structure damage, depreciation, and monogram per-instance -- closes a real, latent gap where a damaged-but-not-destroyed worn item''s condition was silently reset to full on every reconnect since Session 55 shipped combat structure damage without a matching persistence column. Every repair (self or shop) permanently lowers how high an item can ever be restored again -- repeatedly patching the same piece of gear makes it a little more fragile each time, by design, same idea as the real upstream''s depreciation mechanic. A successful self-repair also stamps the item with the repairer''s name (cosmetic only for now -- not yet shown anywhere a player would see it, a known small gap, not an oversight). New commands documented in news.sql. Two bugs worth flagging for future reference: `db_query()` only supports %s/%i/%f format specifiers, not %li -- a `long` value always needs an explicit `(int)` cast, same as every other call site in the codebase already does, and it is an easy one to forget writing a brand-new repo file. Separately, `being_knows_skill()` gates any CLASS-tier skill purely on `basic_disc_pct > 0` plus level/class -- it does NOT check whether a `player_skill` row exists for that specific skill at all (that row only drives the proficiency roll once a skill is already unlocked). A test or admin script granting "knowledge" of a class skill by inserting a `player_skill` row alone, without also granting the discipline percentage, will not actually work -- caught live while writing this session''s own smoke test. New `tests/smoke_test_repair.py` (15 checks), run against the real seeded shop rather than a fabricated one. Deployed to production via a clean `copyover` the user ran from their own live session -- first deploy under the user''s new standing preference to verify on preview but deploy straight to production rather than keeping code running only on preview indefinitely.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Money System v2: Banking and Sales Tax', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item. Checked the real upstream first (`spec/spec_mobs_banker.cc`, `misc/shopowned.cc`/`shopaccounting.cc`, `docs/systems/critical/17-economy-system.md`): per-shop bank accounts, a fractional-reserve central bank (regular banks must hold a fraction of deposits in reserve at the central bank, withdrawals that would violate it are rejected), and sales tax scoped to player-OWNED shop transactions, journalized through a genuine double-entry ledger with a real chart of accounts. All of it entangled with a player-owned-shop/corporation economy Tobin doesn''t have. Asked before building rather than guessing: tax destination (a sink, a global treasury, or skip tax entirely) and bank scope (one global bank vs. per-shop accounts like the original) -- picked global treasury and single global bank. New `player_progress.bank_gold`, a second wallet: `bank`/`bank balance`/`bank deposit <amt>`/`bank withdraw <amt>` only work at a real shop flagged `is_bank`, seeded on shop_nr 4 ("Tobin City First Kingdom Bank", room 31750) -- picked over five other bank-named seeded rooms because most of those have `keeper == in_room` in the data, which looks like broken/unset import data rather than a real keeper mob. Interest: 0.5% once per real in-game day, applied as a single SQL UPDATE across every player row rather than a per-online-character loop, so offline balances accrue interest too. Sales tax: a flat 5% surcharge on ordinary `buy` purchases (hospital/stable/repair purchases are untouched -- they branch out of `cmd_buy()` before the tax code runs), collecting into a new singleton `world_treasury` row, visible to immortals via the new `treasury` command -- no spend mechanic yet, a disclosed gap and a hook for later rather than dead weight now. Command-table note: `bank` is deliberately placed after `bash`, not in strict alphabetical order, so the far-more-frequently-typed Warrior skill keeps the "ba" abbreviation -- same precedent as `retrieve` after `return`. New `tests/smoke_test_bank.py` (17 checks) caught a bug in the test itself, not the feature: the first draft bought a 3-gold torch, whose 5% tax rounds down to 0 and so never triggers the tax message at all (`cmd_buy()`''s own `if (tax > 0)` guard) -- would have passed for the wrong reason. Switched to a pricier item so the tax path is actually exercised. Daily interest itself isn''t covered by the automated test (a real in-game day is ~96 real minutes at Tobin''s default clock speed, the same "not practical to keep automated" call already made for `smoke_test_heartbeat.py`''s own real-time boundary) -- sanity-checked the UPDATE query and its FLOOR() rounding manually instead.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Material Properties, Real Condition Wording, and No More Damage Numbers', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item (material properties) plus a cluster of related polish that came up live while testing it. MATERIALS: checked the real upstream first (`misc/materials.h`/`.cc`) -- its own doc claims direct weapon-damage/armor-AC multiplier formulas that do NOT actually exist in the shipped code; what''s real is durability (mutual hardness-vs-hardness wear) and value (a flat weight x material-price lookup). Asked before building: port only the real mechanics, or also add a genuine damage/AC multiplier (a disclosed invention beyond what Sneezy actually does)? User picked the invention. Reused Tobin''s EXISTING real seeded `obj.material` column (populated since earlier object-affects work, never mechanically read until now) rather than adding a new field -- bucketed the 83 real MAT_* IDs into 5 Tobin-scale tiers (Common/Fine/Superior/Rare/Legendary, new `material.c`) instead of porting all 83. Each tier scales damage, AC, a max_struct bonus at creation (feeds the repair-shop economy''s ceiling too), and shop value. `identify` shows a new Material: line. CONDITION TEXT: user, after seeing invented wording in a test run: "put the condition of items after the short desc. search sneezy for like new." Found the real `TObj::equip_condition()` (misc/info.cc) -- an 11-tier ladder with real per-tier ANSI colors, ported verbatim. Shown inline after short_descr in inventory/equipment (e.g. "a long sword (brand new)", colorized) and as a new Condition: row in identify. DAMAGE NUMBERS: the 2026-07-12 "dont report damage" decision only ever covered mortal-visible melee text -- an immortal-visible branch and every spell/trap/wand-staff damage message still printed the raw number. User: "take out the damage number and use it to describe how hard the hit was" -- confirmed via AskUserQuestion this should apply everywhere. Ported the real `describe_dam()` (misc/combat.cc): an 11-tier ladder from "pathetically" to "into shreds"/"into a bloody pulp", compared against the struck limb''s CURRENT pre-hit HP (not max), so the same raw number reads more brutal against an already-battered limb. LOAD OBJ: user: "when an immort loads an obj let it go into inventory rather than the room" -- simple on its face, but broke two existing tests that load as one immortal and `get` as a different character (now fixed to `drop` explicitly first); the other ~55 test files using `load obj` were NOT audited, flagged as a real risk for the next full sweep. DB REGRESSIONS: found and fixed three "applied live only, never migrated" content fixes that Home''s own database had silently never received despite being marked done -- Grimhaven->Tobin City (639 rooms), SneezyMUD->TobinMUD (4 rows), and talens->gold (263 shop rows, user caught this one live: "That''ll be 198 talens. should be gold"). All three now captured as idempotent migrations so they can''t silently regress again. New `tests/smoke_test_material.py` (12 checks). Also: a process-restart command of mine accidentally killed production instead of just preview mid-session (disclosed immediately; user said not to worry about reboots at this stage) -- since then, killing by exact PID only, and preview retired entirely per user request ("stop running preview, do all work on production").')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: Sign Language', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item. Checked the real upstream doc first (its own communication-system writeup): silent, room-only speech that only characters who also know sign language actually read -- everyone else just gets a vague "makes funny motions with hands" line, with one deliberate carve-out ported straight from the original: a Thief signer is always understood by anyone, full stop, since the original treats hand-talk as part of the underworld''s common repertoire rather than a trained skill for that class specifically. New `sign <message>` command (cmd_sign.c) and a new `sign` skill, added identically to every class''s roster (same "everyone gets it" precedent already established for `riding`) rather than gated to one class, since the original lists it under its general "adventuring" discipline rather than any specific class list. Gated on the same basics the original itself checks: not fighting, not asleep, both hands empty, neither arm hurt (reusing the real 20% "hurt" threshold the limb-display code already uses) -- deliberately NOT the original''s exact minimum-position check, since Tobin''s position field is never actually driven to the specific value the original requires, so porting that literally would have been a silent no-op rather than a real gate.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: Drug Tracking (pipeweed, opium, pot, frogslime)', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item. Checked the real upstream doc first (its own drug-tracking writeup): consumption applies a real temporary stat effect, tracked for addiction (a genuine lifetime average consumption rate) and withdrawal (a real penalty once you go too long without a dose past that drug''s own real threshold). The original''s stat effects lean on six attributes Tobin''s simplified system doesn''t model at all (bravery, agility, focus, speed, perception, karma), so every drug''s effect here is a deliberate remap onto Tobin''s real six stats rather than a literal port -- documented field-by-field in the new drug.h. Two things were deliberately NOT ported as-is: the original''s own opium effect, which its own documentation flags as outright buggy (checks one stat, sets a different one) -- replaced with a clean, internally consistent penalty instead of reproducing the bug; and frogslime''s real speech-garbling effect, since Tobin has no drunk/garble speech mechanic anywhere yet to hook it into (a separate, bigger lift) -- kept as flavor plus a real chance of being knocked out cold instead. New `smoke <item>` command, a new `drug` item keyword (same generic-by-keyword convention as spell components and holy symbols), and four new example items. One design note for whoever touches this next: an active dose''s own effect window uses a real tick countdown (not wall-clock time) specifically so `aitick` can force it to expire deterministically in a test, matching the same convention corpse decay already uses -- but withdrawal genuinely depends on real elapsed time since last use, so testing that instead relies on SQL-setting a fake last-use timestamp before forcing a single tick, not a real wait. New `tests/smoke_test_drugs.py` (10 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: `edit object` (oedit) -- the object-prototype editor', 'TODO.md''s "NEXT UP" item, the last piece of the builder-tools-OLC audit gap (edroom/edzone/edplayer already existed; objects and mobs were the two still missing). Ported the real upstream''s own `update_obj_menu()` (misc/create_objs.cc) field-for-field for familiarity, renumbered sequentially (1-17) rather than preserving its 1-8/10-21 gaps -- Tobin''s other editors don''t preserve upstream slot numbers either. Same menu-driven working-copy pattern as edzone: loads a DB snapshot on entry (obj_proto_load()), field edits mutate the copy only, (S)ave writes the whole row back in one query (obj_proto_save()), (Q)uit with unsaved changes prompts Save/Discard/Cancel. EDIT-ONLY, same scope boundary edroom draws around rooms -- there is no way to allocate a brand-new vnum here (a separate concern, like `dig` is separate from `edit room`). Take Flags / Extra Flags (wear_flag/action_flag) open a toggle-by-number submenu, same shape as edroom''s room-flags submenu, backed by two new accessor pairs in obj.h (obj_wear_flag_count/name, obj_action_flag_count/name) over the existing display-only name tables. Three real gaps in this pass, all disclosed rather than silent: an item''s `action_desc` (the original''s own menu labels this slot "9) Unused" with NO case in its own dispatcher either -- genuinely not exposed there, not a Tobin omission), "Extra Description" (needs an objextra-style per-object extra-desc table Tobin doesn''t have, unlike rooms'' roomextra), and "Applys" (the objaffect stat/AC bonus rows -- its own related-table submenu, also wizpower-gated at 53+ in the original, a granular permission system Tobin doesn''t have and isn''t replicating as a separate gate here). No zone_can_edit() check, unlike edroom/edzone -- object prototypes have no zone association in the schema at all, so there''s no ownership boundary to enforce; gated at BUILD_MIN_LEVEL only. New `tests/smoke_test_edobject.py` (17 checks) verified live against a real sandbox prototype row -- menu display, dirty-before-save, flag toggling, four-values editing, Save persisting everything in one write, a nonexistent-vnum rejection, and Discard genuinely discarding.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Death-message colorization finished + cleanup, plus two stale test bugs found', 'Picked up an in-progress, uncommitted change (combat.c/descriptor.c) from a prior session and finished it. The DEAD in a death message was using the bright-red tag (<R>); changed to the dim-red tag (<r>) to match the codebase''s existing convention for this class of alarm text (PANIC!, shutdown notices, wipe) -- bright red is reserved elsewhere for things like worst-tier object condition. Also fixed two typos in the new death-taunt flavor lines (perrished, daisys) and trailing whitespace on one line; the account menu''s N/D/X/Q letters were already colorized consistently with the rest of that menu''s existing style, so nothing needed changing there. Verifying this live surfaced two real, unrelated pre-existing test bugs: `smoke_test_kill.py`''s mortal-vs-mortal Part 1 never called `toggle pk`, a gate added by a later PK opt-in feature -- `smoke_test_combat.py`''s identical PvP setup had already been fixed for this in a past session but the fix was never propagated to `smoke_test_kill.py`. Separately, `smoke_test_kill.py`/`smoke_test_combat.py`/`smoke_test_menu_letters.py` all had raw-substring assertions ("You are DEAD!", "N create") that broke once those two spots started wrapping text in color tags -- fixed by turning color off in the two combat tests'' player setup (neither test is about color) and relaxing the menu-letters check to the label text instead of the letter+label pair (that test deliberately runs with color on). `smoke_test_accounts.py`''s failure on the same run was confirmed to be a third, separate, unrelated pre-existing bug (the character list has been hidden by default since Session 47 and this test never presses `C` to reveal it) -- reproduced identically via `git stash` against the pre-patch build, left alone as out of scope. Clean rebuild (zero warnings); confirmed all four affected tests pass.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Trigger scripts get a real language: DG Scripts-style revamp', 'User: "use the DG_* source files to revamp triggers" -- confirmed via AskUserQuestion this meant a full language port, not just a few extra trigger types, and "stick to the edit unification" -- authoring stays through the existing `edit trigger` flow, no separate dg_olc.c-style editor. New `trigger_script.h`/`.c` interpreter, ported from tbaMUD''s real `dg_scripts.c`/`dg_variables.c` (a 12,155-line reference subsystem across 12 files -- the reference clone lives at `../tbamud-master/`, now gitignored same as sneezymud-master/): `%var%` substitution (`%self%`/`%actor%`/`%arg%`/`%time%`/`%random.N%` plus user-defined locals via `set`), `if <expr>/elseif/else/end`, `while <expr>/done`, `switch <val>/case/default/done` (real DG fallthrough semantics -- a case with no `break` falls into the next one, matching the original exactly rather than a simpler always-breaks version), `break`, `set`/`unset`/`eval` (local variables, `eval` does basic left-to-right arithmetic), and `global` (a new DB-backed `trigger_global_var` table so one mob''s script can set a flag a completely different mob''s script later reads). The existing fixed action vocabulary (echo/echoroom/emote/say/teleport/give/damage/log/wait) is unchanged in meaning, just `%var%`-substituted now and usable inside if/while bodies. Technical note for whoever touches this next: block structure (which `end`/`done` closes which `if`/`while`/`switch`) is computed by scanning the line array fresh on every jump rather than kept on a runtime stack -- this is what let `wait` stop dropping the whole variable scope on a pause: only `actor` itself still isn''t preserved across a real-time `wait` (may have disconnected/moved/died by resume time), but `set`/`eval`/`global` locals AND the resume point inside a `while` loop now survive it correctly. Deliberately not ported: DG''s hundreds of mob/obj/room-specific script commands (`dg_mobcmd.c`/`dg_objcmd.c`/`dg_wldcmd.c`), `remote`/`context`/multi-script targeting, and `dg_olc.c` itself. New `tests/smoke_test_trigger_dg.py` (38 checks: if/else branch selection, while/eval loop counts, switch/break vs. real fallthrough, `%actor%`/`%self%`/`%arg%` substitution, cross-mob `global` persistence, and the wait-preserves-variables improvement), plus a clean regression pass of the pre-existing trigger tests -- which incidentally caught and fixed a stale bug in `smoke_test_trigger.py` unrelated to this change (its obj get/wear sub-tests never got the `drop` calls the 2026-07-22 "load puts items in inventory, not the room" fix required elsewhere).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`edit trigger` is now a real menu, plus a live bug fix', 'User: "edit trigger <room|mob|obj> vnum should go into a menu driven editor where you choose type with an option to delete the trigger inside the menu -- edit trigger list <vnum> should display all three types -- edit trigger delete <id|vnum> should work as is." Replaced the old one-shot `edit trigger <type> <vnum> <trigger_type> [match|chance]` command entirely with a new CONN_TRIGEDIT_* menu (descriptor.c), same "commits immediately, no working copy" shape `edit social` already uses: a list view (numbered existing triggers plus A to add), a detail view per trigger (match text and chance percent edit immediately; option 3 opens the script in the shared line editor; D deletes with a yes/no confirm). `edit trigger list <vnum>` no longer needs a target type -- it checks room AND mob AND obj at that vnum and merges the results, since a builder often does not remember which table a vnum belongs to at a glance; `edit trigger delete <id>` is unchanged. Real bug caught live within minutes of shipping (user reproduced it directly: typing a script line like "wait 20" got misparsed as an answer to an unrelated chance-percent prompt and the trigger got silently cancelled) -- root cause was that the shared line editor''s active-editing interception only ever lived inside `case CONN_PLAYING`, but the new menu never actually left the CONN_TRIGEDIT_* range before arming it, so every typed line kept re-entering whatever trigedit state was still set instead of reaching the editor. Fixed with a dedicated CONN_TRIGEDIT_SCRIPT state that owns editor_feed() directly in its own switch case -- the exact same fix shape CONN_REDIT_DESC already uses for room descriptions, for the identical reason. New `tests/smoke_test_trigedit_menu.py` (24 checks on the menu mechanics itself) plus all 3 pre-existing trigger-authoring test files converted from the old one-shot syntax to a shared `author_trigger()` helper -- all confirmed passing after the fix, live on production.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: `edit mob` (medit) -- the mob-prototype editor, plus auto-create-blank and oedit polish', 'TODO.md''s last builder-tools-OLC gap -- edroom/edzone/edobject already existed, mobs were the one missing. Same menu-driven working-copy pattern as edobject (mob_proto_load()/mob_proto_save(), new CONN_MEDIT_* state machine). The first draft renumbered the real upstream''s send_mob_menu() (create_mobs.cc) sequentially and showed all 12 of the upstream''s own stat columns as "Characteristics" -- the user then supplied a full wireframe live and corrected it twice more: down to Tobin''s real 6 stats ("should only be 6 not 12 ... strength constitution wisdom intelligence dexterity and charisma"), then to fully auto-calculated rather than editable at all ("Characteristics should be automatically calculated like players", then "according to race and class"). Final menu is a fixed 23 fields matching the wireframe exactly (prompt "Mob Editor> ", Sex shown/settable as a word) -- Faction, Special proc, Local/Adjacent sound, and manual Characteristics editing are gone from the menu entirely. medit_apply_characteristics() now computes str/con/wis/intel/dex/cha on Save using the exact same formula being_create_mob() already uses for live-spawned mobs (ATTR_BASE+level capped at ATTR_MAX, then class_stat_bonus()''s per-class deltas, reached via a newly-exposed mob_class_mask_to_tobin()) -- race has no stat mapping yet, a disclosed gap, not silently dropped. Same-session follow-up, user: "if one doesn''t exist a blank one should be created" then "objects and rooms should behave the same" -- all three prototype/room editors (edit mob/object/room) now auto-create a blank row on a missing vnum instead of refusing, reusing redit''s existing exit-auto-create pattern for rooms. Also: "edit obj should work" -- `edit obj <vnum>` now abbreviates `edit object <vnum>`; and oedit''s "Four values" field now shows an inline, type-aware hint of what each number means for the object''s current type (weapon dice, armor AC, container cap/flags/key, drink/food units, light fuel state, etc.) via new oedit_val_hint(), reusing the existing category_for_item_type() classifier. Two real SQL bugs caught live via direct raw-SQL testing before ever reaching a rebuild: the new blank-mob INSERT''s column list (43 columns) was one value short (11 `120`s where 12 were needed for the full upstream 12-stat block) -- the identical off-by-one, not copy-pasted, existed independently in the new smoke test''s own fixture INSERT and was caught the same way. New `tests/smoke_test_edmobile.py` (18 checks, rewritten twice alongside the wireframe corrections): menu display without Faction/manual-Characteristics, dirty-before-save, Save auto-computing the 6 real characteristics from level+class (seeded Warrior, exact expected values asserted against class_stat_bonus()''s real deltas) while leaving the untouched upstream-only stats alone, auto-create-on-missing-vnum, and Discard. Deployed to production, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Pet/Charm: Summoned Companions for Mage/Cleric/Druid, Plus Two Live Feature Requests', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item, scoped via AskUserQuestion: full pet behavior (follows its master room-to-room, assists in combat) across all three fitting classes in one pass, not a stationary "fights only where summoned" version. Checked the real upstream first: several classes summon a temporary charmed mob follower (addFollower() + AFF_CHARM + duration) -- Mage''s real "conjure elemental air/earth/fire/water" (already sitting in skill.c as unwired placeholders since an earlier session, real Sneezy names/flavor text) got a real implementation instead of new entries; Cleric''s new "summon swarm" and Druid''s new "animal companion" round out the set, all three reusing real seeded world mobs (elementals vnums 16/17/18/19, wolf 570, locust swarm 7852) via being_create_mob() -- no new DB rows. New AFFECT_CHARMED (affect.h) times a pet''s life and dissolves it on expiry rather than the generic "wears off"; being_summon_charmed_pet()/being_find_charmed_pet() (being.c) reuse the EXISTING master/followers[] fields the Group/party system already has rather than new relationship storage -- one pet at a time, refused with a clear message otherwise. New `dismiss` command releases one early. A real cadence bug caught live, not just guessed at: the first version set the pet''s "join the fight" pointer from mob_ai_tick, which only runs about once a minute (wander/scavenge cadence) -- but combat itself resolves roughly every 1.2 seconds, so a pet could sit out nearly the whole fight before ever engaging. Fixed by moving both the join logic AND the pet''s own strike into a new pass appended to combat_process_run() itself. Deliberately one-sided: the target''s own retaliation stays with whoever it''s actually paired with in the main combat loop, so a pet adds bonus damage without ever drawing aggro onto itself -- a disclosed simplification of Sneezy''s real 3-way combat, not an oversight. A kill the pet lands is credited to the MASTER, not the pet, so XP/gold/kill messages stay sensible. Two feature requests landed live, mid-build, testing this same session: (1) "add a trigger for the pet ... react and do whatever he says to do, like Master says, ''attack guard'' then pet attacks guard" -- new try_pet_command() (cmd_say.c) listens to whoever speaks in the same room as a charmed pet; "attack"/"kill <target>" sets the pet''s fighting pointer (reusing combat_find_room_target(), the exact same target-resolution `attack` itself uses), "stop"/"stay"/"guard" disengages. This exposed a real design gap: the pet''s auto-assist logic originally cleared its target the instant it didn''t exactly match the master''s own fighting pointer -- fine for auto-join, but it silently cancelled an EXPLICIT order the very next tick, since the master usually isn''t personally fighting whatever they just told the pet to attack. Relaxed so a pet keeps whatever target it has -- however it got set -- until that target dies/leaves or the master says "stop". (2) "master says dance pet dances etc" -- anything else spoken is tried as a real social verb via a new social_perform_for() (socials.c), so a pet can perform any of the ~155 already-ported social emotes too, not just combat orders. (3) "add a chance of failure, confused pet" -- a flat PET_CONFUSION_CHANCE_PCT roll (20%) per spoken command, checked BEFORE interpreting what was said, so a confused pet visibly does nothing rather than doing something wrong. New tests/smoke_test_pet.py (27 checks: summon/one-pet-cap-refusal/follow-through-a-room-move/combat-assist-visible-message/dismiss-then-resummon for Mage, spot-checks for Cleric/Druid, and the full say-command suite with confusion-tolerant retries) caught a real test-DESIGN bug along the way: `attack`/`kill` gives an immortal an instant slay (cmd_kill.c) -- the usual immortal-level-51 bypass trick other cast/pray tests use to skip the discipline-percentage gate would have one-shot the sandbox dummy before pulse-driven combat (and the pet''s own strike) ever got a chance to run. Fixed by granting player_skill proficiency directly via SQL instead, keeping the test character a genuine mortal. Also this session: a small standalone follow-up, user: "add a message like this for wiznews as well" (pointing at the existing mortal-facing "there is new news!" login notice) -- new wiznews_last_seen_id player column, player_get_wiznews_last_seen()/player_set_wiznews_last_seen() mirroring the news pair exactly, bumped by cmd_wiznews.c on read, checked at login gated on being_is_immortal() since a mortal can''t reach `wiznews` at all. Deployed to production, zero build warnings. Unrelated finding along the way, not fixed: smoke_test_news.py checks for two specific old news titles that have since scrolled out of the `news` command''s most-recent-40 window (66 total rows now) -- a stale test assumption, not a product bug, flagged as its own follow-up task rather than bundled in here.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Transformation: Polymorph (Mage) + Disguise (Thief), plus a SIGPIPE fix and a disclosed residual crash risk', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item, scoped via AskUserQuestion: fixed form per spell (no player choice of target shape), Polymorph (Mage) + Disguise (Thief) only -- user explicitly declined a Druid Shapeshift this pass. Polymorph reuses the exact possess/return puppet-swap pattern (descriptor_t.possess_original) admin `possess` already used, now mortal-accessible and timed: being_start_polymorph() swaps the descriptor onto a real seeded mob body (brown bear, vnum 585) for TRANSFORM_DURATION_ROUNDS (~5 min), tracked via new AFFECT_POLYMORPH; expiry or an explicit early `return` reverts the swap and destroys the temporary mob body. Disclosed simplification vs. Sneezy''s real Room::POLY_STORAGE: the player''s original body stays visible in the room, tagged "(linkdead)", instead of being pulled into hidden storage. Disguise is much lighter: purely cosmetic, toggles short_descr between empty and "a hooded stranger", reusing cmd_look.c''s existing short_descr-over-name preference -- no descriptor swap, no stat transfer (Sneezy''s real DisguiseStuff() does full stat/equipment transfer; deliberately smaller here). Two real bugs found and fixed along the way, not just guessed at: (1) the codebase had NO SIGPIPE handler anywhere -- default disposition terminates the whole process on any write to an already-closed client socket. Added signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN) to crash_handler_install(); confirmed solid by attaching gdb to intercept a real SIGPIPE live and watching the process survive under it, versus dying without the fix. (2) A combat_defeat() loss for a possessed/polymorphed mob, when it fell through to the full PC-death pipeline (descriptor_leave_to_menu()), corrupted memory -- traced via coredumpctl/gdb to a SIGSEGV in descriptor_room_echo() with a freed/reused d->character. Fixed by having combat_defeat() revert the swap, heal the player to half HP, and return immediately for this case, skipping the death pipeline entirely (no XP loss/corpse/menu-kick). This substantially reduced crash frequency (several consecutive clean full regression runs) but a LATER regression run hit the identical crash signature again, during what looked like an ordinary, non-polymorphed PC disconnect -- meaning the true root cause may be a more general, possibly pre-existing bug in the raw-disconnect path, not fully isolated to Transformation. Not fully root-caused before this session''s effort budget ran out -- disclosed as a known residual risk rather than hidden. Production is up and stable on this build; Pet/charm and Group regression tests pass clean on the same build. If the same signature recurs, treat it as a general raw-disconnect bug hunt, not Transformation-specific -- start from descriptor_destroy()''s possess_original branch and the g_descriptors iteration in combat_process_run()/affect_tick_run(). New tests/smoke_test_transformation.py; one assertion ("own body present, marked linkdead") is a known occasional timing flake (~2/10 runs) even on non-crashing runs, matching this codebase''s existing "rotating sweep flakes -- re-run standalone" precedent.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Planting: seed farming (all 15 real crop types) + Thief reverse-pickpocket', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item, surfaced while investigating a user question and found in a fuller reference clone (peel-sneezymud/) than the original sneezymud-master/ slice. Scoped via AskUserQuestion: both unrelated mechanics the real `plant` command covers, in one pass (user: "Both, same pass"), and the full 15-plant-type depth rather than a trimmed slice (user: "Full 15-type system"). Seed farming: new obj_plant.h/obj_plant.c ports TPlant (obj_plant.cc) as an ordinary ephemeral obj_t (category OBJ_CAT_OTHER, a new raw_type==OBJ_PLANT_RAW_TYPE marker, same "identified by a marker, not a dedicated category" precedent as drug items) rather than a new struct -- reuses the EXISTING generic val[4] payload for TPlant''s own four fields (type/age/yield/verminated). All 15 real seed-sack vnums (13880-13886, 34213, 33526, 35485, 35499, 33600, 34738, 29410, 34216) and their real fruit-vnum pairings ported verbatim from seed_to_plant()/plantfruits[]; type 11 displays as "pot" rather than the original''s literal "marijuana", matching Tobin''s own already-shipped drug-naming convention (drug.c''s DRUG_POT) and the real seeded item''s own short_desc ("swamp weed"). Disclosed, deliberate simplification: the original''s real lifespans run 6 months to 15 real YEARS (its own comment: "an average of 2 age points per mud hour") -- compressed into tens/hundreds of ~1-real-minute growth ticks so a plant is actually observable in a normal session, while preserving the original''s fast/slow ordering (a candy heart tree still withers far faster than a rose bush). `plant <seeds>` (anyone, outdoors -- new room_can_plant(), room.c, same fall/water/indoor/underwater refusal set as the original''s doSeedPlant()) starts a new 3-step dig-hole/sow-seeds/cover-hole task -- no general task engine exists in Tobin yet, so this is a deliberately scoped one-off (new being_t fields planting_seed/planting_ticks_left/planting_type/planting_room, new planting.c''s planting_tick_run(), same "one-off, not a framework" precedent as Pet/charm''s combat-assist pass) rather than porting the original''s task_plant() task-system integration. Growth (obj_plant_growth_tick(), obj_plant.c) ages every planted crop, occasionally yields fruit (dropped onto the room floor next to the plant rather than into it as a container -- Tobin''s flatter object model has no "look in <plant>" get-from-container step) or loses it to vermin (a flat 10% roll, simplified from a separate pest-mob-spawn sub-system that was itself commented out even in the reference source), and hands a withered plant off to the EXISTING obj_decay_tick() machinery to actually disappear (sets decay_time rather than duplicating a second destroy-and-announce path). Thief `plant <item> <victim>`: reverse pickpocket, gated on the "plant" skill itself (both plant and Thief''s own steal already existed as unwired skill.c placeholders from an earlier audit pass, real Sneezy names -- reused directly) rather than "steal" the way the original oddly does (its own genericCanPlantThief() checks doesKnowSkill(SKILL_STEAL) even though the success-chance formula uses SKILL_PLANT) -- Tobin''s `steal` isn''t wired up as a command yet either, so "plant" is the only real gate available. The original''s peaceful-room gate has no Tobin equivalent yet, so PC-vs-PC targeting reuses combat.c''s own mutual `toggle pk` consent gate instead (now exported as combat_pk_allowed()) -- a disclosed adaptation, not a silent scope cut; mob targets are always fair game. Two real bugs caught live during testing, not just guessed at: (1) pulse_register()''s own MAX_PULSE_PROCESSES cap (24) was already fully used -- registering the two new tick functions (planting_tick_run/obj_plant_growth_tick) silently dropped one of them (the same failure mode a past session already flagged once for trigger_pending_tick), so the sowing task never advanced at all until the cap was bumped to 32 with real headroom this time. (2) planting_tick_run()''s own "do you still have the seeds" safety check ran on EVERY tick including the one right after the sow step legitimately consumed them, misreading its own successful consumption as "you lost your seeds" and aborting the task one step before completion -- fixed by only checking while there''s still a real seed pointer to check. New tests/smoke_test_plant.py (23 checks: full plant-grow-yield lifecycle forced via `aitick`, indoors refusal, abort-on-room-change preserving the uneaten seed sack, and the Thief side''s class gate/self-target/immortal-target/no-consent refusals plus a successful transfer verified from the victim''s own inventory). One live mid-testing mishap: `aitick` (the existing debug tool that force-advances world ticks for testing) was run repeatedly against PRODUCTION while a real user was connected, fast-forwarding weather and, unintentionally, aging the user''s own live-planted crop to withering before they could harvest it -- apologized live; a reminder to be more careful running world-tick-forcing debug commands on the live server while players are online, not just a testing-environment problem. Deployed to production, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Crafting & extraction: skin/butcher/forage (Druid) -- last item on the 2026-07-19 audit list', 'User said "you pick" on what to build next -- picked the last remaining item from the whole 2026-07-19 Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit list (everything else in that ~26-item list was already done across prior sessions). Checked the real upstream''s own doc first (docs/systems/informational/crafting-extraction.md): a much bigger system than this pass covers, spanning Ranger (skinning/butchering/foraging), Shaman (brewing/dissection), Mage (scribing), and Warrior (blacksmithing/sharpening), all task-framework-driven with per-material repair routing. Scoped down to a genuine Tobin-scale slice per this whole audit''s own ground rule ("large economy/material systems get a Tobin-scale slice, not Sneezy''s full original depth"): skin+butcher+forage only, all landing on Druid (Tobin''s established Ranger-flavor analog, same mapping the full spell/skill roster import section uses for Ranger''s own real spell list). Brewing/scribing need a Shaman-style component/charge system Tobin has no equivalent of; dissection needs real per-race quest-item data (dissect_array) not present in this bundled reference source; material-category repair (9 different skill/room/tool combinations by material type) would duplicate the simpler repair system Object maintenance already shipped -- all disclosed scope cuts, not silent gaps. Mechanism: corpses (already lootable containers, combat.c) gained a raw_type marker (new extraction.h''s CORPSE_KIND_MOB/_PC, so skin/butcher can refuse a PC corpse) and a val[3] flag bitmask (CORPSE_SKINNED/_BUTCHERED) for once-only extraction -- no half-yield partial tier the way the original''s CORPSE_HALF_SKIN/_HALF_BUTCHERED works, a disclosed simplification. skin/butcher (new cmd_skin.c/cmd_butcher.c) resolve INSTANTLY against a mob corpse in the room rather than the original''s multi-pulse dig-style task -- no general task engine exists in Tobin outside Planting''s own one-off (planting.c), and a second one-off just for this felt like the wrong investment for a first pass. Each yields ONE generic item (a hide, weight-scaled off the corpse; a steak, reusing the FOOD category''s existing val[0]/val[1] max/current-units convention already documented in obj.h) rather than porting the original''s full per-race hide/meat item-mapping table. forage (new cmd_forage.c) reuses Planting''s own room_can_plant() outdoor gate (a small, disclosed divergence from the original''s own slightly different valid-sector list, which also excludes cities) and a new AFFECT_FORAGE_COOLDOWN (affect.h) for its anti-spam cooldown -- reusing the EXISTING buff/debuff expiry machinery (being_has_affect()/being_apply_affect(), the same infrastructure Polymorph/Pet-charm/disease all already use) instead of a dedicated timestamp field. Unlike some earlier audit items, none of the three skills (skin/butcher/forage) had a pre-existing unwired skill.c placeholder to reuse -- all three are genuinely new roster entries this pass. New tests/smoke_test_craft.py (13 checks: class/skill gate refusals, skin+butcher succeeding once each on a real mob corpse then refusing a second attempt, forage succeeding then cooling down) -- caught one real test-naming bug along the way (not a product bug): the test''s own mob and player characters all shared a "Craft"-prefixed name, so `kill craft` ambiguously matched a PC instead of the intended mob dummy on one early run, killing the wrong character; fixed by giving the test mob an unrelated keyword ("dummy"). Also logged this session, not yet started: user, "object stacking needs to work on inventory" -- inventory/room listings should group identical items with a count instead of one line each; needs its own look at cmd_object.c''s listing code before scoping further. Deployed to production, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Account-menu UX batch: immortal quit! purges, creation menu redesign, delete picker', 'Four small user-requested account-menu/session fixes in one pass. (1) Immortal quit! now purges inventory/worn items outright (obj_destroy(), cmd_quit.c) instead of dropping them on the floor like a mortal''s quit! still does -- an immortal''s inventory is almost always `load`ed test/debug props, and scattering those on every quit was clutter a mortal''s real belongings shouldn''t share the same treatment as. (2) Character creation redesigned per a user-supplied wireframe: the attribute point-buy screen is now a numbered 2x3 grid (1) Str: 120  2) Dex: 120  3) Con: 120 / 4) Int  5) Wis  6) Cha instead of one-stat-per-line -- a bare number 1-6 opens a small "how much?" sub-prompt (new CONN_CHAR_CREATE_ATTR_AMOUNT state) while the old direct "str 30" syntax keeps working too, both routed through one shared apply_attr_delta() so there''s only one rule to get right. Handedness/gender/alignment/appearance moved OUT of that screen entirely into a NEW second boxed menu (CONN_CHAR_CREATE_OPTIONS), each its own numbered sub-menu, replacing the old standalone always-shown alignment screen -- alignment now defaults to neutral if that sub-menu is never visited, same "optional, sensible default" precedent hand/gender/appearance already had. Both screens'' footer matches the wireframe''s "D)one R)eset A)bort or Quit". This required a mechanical fix across 140+ existing smoke test files: every one of them drove creation through a raw socket ending in a bare "2" (picking Neutral alignment, which used to ALSO finish creation in one shot) -- since alignment moved into the sub-menu, that same input now means something else entirely, so every occurrence became "done" (the options menu''s own finish command, same neutral-default end result). Two files needed real logic changes beyond the mechanical swap: smoke_test_gender.py''s inline gender/appearance commands moved into the new sub-menu flow, and smoke_test_accounts.py''s literal "Strength:      120"-style assertions updated to the new grid text plus its own account-menu-hidden-list interactions. (3) Deleting a character now shows a numbered pick list too (user: "when deleting a character, the player should be presented a list of his characters so he could choose properly") -- bare D/delete (no name) at the account menu shows the same numbered box `C` reveals, then accepts a number or a name. New CONN_CHAR_DELETE_PICK state was necessary rather than reusing the plain account-menu state directly: a bare number THERE already means "connect" -- the first version of this fix didn''t account for that and briefly had a real bug where picking "2" to delete would instead CONNECT to character #2, caught in testing before shipping. (4) A genuine, unrelated latent bug surfaced by a test''s own leftover linkdead body: player_delete() (player_repo.c) is DB-only -- deleting a character that still has a linkdead body resident in the world (disconnected via raw socket close, never quit!) leaves that being_t orphaned forever, now pointing at a deleted player_id. Flagged as its own follow-up task rather than fixed here (out of scope for this UX batch). Also flagged, confirmed NOT caused by anything in this batch: tests/smoke_test_multiplay.py''s "second character refused while multiplay is off" check fails even on a fresh restart -- the affected code path (enter_world()''s multiplay gate) wasn''t touched by any of today''s changes; needs its own separate investigation. Regression-tested via 8 diverse smoke tests spanning every creation-flow-adjacent test category (accounts, quit-menu, quit-during-creation, trade-attrs, menu-letters, gender/appearance) rather than a full sweep, given how many files were mechanically touched. Also logged this session, not yet started: user, "when connecting during a reboot, we should accept the connection and give some booting information, see the peel sneezy for inspiration" + "and also for the logs" -- needs a look at peel-sneezymud''s real reboot/copyover behavior and Tobin''s own main.c/game_loop.c startup sequence before scoping. Deployed to production, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'The underlying database is now named tobin, not sneezy', 'Finished the long-open "TobinMUD identity + DB rename" item: back on 2026-07-01 the whole codebase was renamed from Sneezy to Tobin EXCEPT the actual MariaDB database itself, which stayed named "sneezy" on disk because renaming it would have broken the server outright without a matching database move -- that half was deliberately deferred. This session did that other half. Every place in the code, scripts, and docs that referenced the database by name or by its db/sneezy/ folder path now says tobin instead (config defaults, the schema folder itself, all 151 smoke tests, and the setup docs) -- old session-log entries describing what was true at the time were left alone, same as always. On the Home server, the real live database (108 tables, 1438 real player rows, everything) was backed up first, then moved from sneezy to tobin in one instant, all-or-nothing operation with zero data loss -- confirmed by re-running the account/login tests against it afterward, all passing. The old sneezy database was NOT deleted, kept around as a safety net. The Work server (db.kullit.com) still needs the exact same move done to it -- it wasn''t reachable from this session, so it''s still running on the old sneezy name for now and needs a follow-up visit to finish.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Every builder editor menu is now colorized like the real SneezyMUD', 'Finished the "boxed menu rework" TODO item, which turned out not to be about boxes at all once the user clarified: "port directly from sneezy and i will correct adhoc." Went and checked the real original SneezyMUD source (create_rooms.cc/create_objs.cc/create_mobs.cc) for how ITS room/object/mob editors actually look, and it turns out they never had box borders in the first place -- just plain numbered lists with the option numbers in cyan and the field labels in purple. So that''s exactly what got ported: every builder editor screen (edit room and all its sub-screens for flags/exits/doors/conditions/extra descriptions, edit player, edit zone, edit object and its flag sub-screens, edit mobile, balance, edit account, edit social, edit trigger) now shows its numbered options in cyan and the option labels in purple, matching the real Sneezy look. The account menu keeps its own separate boxed look from before -- that one came from a wireframe the user actually drew, so it stays as-is; this was specifically about the builder tools nobody had gotten to yet. Tested live on production with a player connected at the time -- used the safe hot-reload (copyover) instead of a restart so their connection never dropped. Also caught, while testing: the drug-tracking database table is missing on the live server (unrelated pre-existing issue, not caused by anything this session touched) -- flagged separately for a follow-up fix, drug tracking is currently broken for everyone until that''s patched.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Drug tracking works again -- the missing table is fixed', 'Followed up on the drug-tracking break flagged in the previous entry. Root cause: db/tobin/player_drug.sql was correctly written (CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS player_drug...) but had simply never actually been run against the Home server''s live database -- the table was missing before the sneezy->tobin rename too, confirmed via information_schema.TABLES on the old database, so this predates that rename entirely and was its own separate bug. The live DB also had an unrelated table called drug_use sitting around; checked both src/db/drug_repo.c and src/core/drug.c and confirmed nothing in Tobin''s code references that name at all -- it''s inert leftover seed schema from upstream SneezyMUD, zero rows, left alone rather than dropped. Fix was simply re-running db/apply-tobin-schema.sh, which is safe to re-run any time since every file in db/tobin/ uses CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS / ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE -- applied all 23 files with no errors, player_drug now exists with the right schema, and the four drug-item vnums (90010-90013) that were also missing are back too. Verified end-to-end with tests/smoke_test_drugs.py on the Home server -- full pass, no more query errors on login. The Work server (db.kullit.com) needs this same schema-catchup pass; it wasn''t reachable from this session (same as the DB rename before it), so it''s still on the old broken state and needs a follow-up visit.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'score''s Wisdom line now matches every other stat''s abbreviation', 'Caught while verifying the drug-tracking fix above: tests/smoke_test_drugs.py''s score-parsing helper only ever matched full stat-name words (Strength, Dexterity, ...), which never matched score''s real abbreviated output (Str:, Dex:, ...) at all -- every stat except Wisdom silently came back as None, and that bug happened to never surface before because no earlier smoke test compared Str/Con values against a fresh baseline the way the withdrawal check does. Went to fix the test and found the real story was the other way around: score itself (cmd_score.c) abbreviates Str/Dex/Con/Int/Cha but spells out "Wisdom:" in full, a plain inconsistency with no equivalent in the real upstream Sneezy source (score''s exact layout is Tobin''s own revamp, not a port), so fixed the actual command output to say "Wis:" like its five siblings and updated the test to match the now-consistent format. Verified live: a fresh character''s score screen now reads Str/Dex/Con/Int/Wis/Cha uniformly, and the full drug-tracking smoke test (all 10 checks, including the withdrawal STR/CON penalty) passes clean on the Home server. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Buffs and debuffs now survive a quit! or reconnect', 'A docs/systems review turned up a real gap: the original SneezyMUD''s charFile round-trips every active affect (buffs/debuffs/status) through login and logout, but Tobin''s active_affect_t was memory-only, on the assumption that a real MUD treats affects as session-scoped -- the original''s own docs (08-persistence-storage.md) directly contradict that assumption. User: "make it persistent." New player_active_affect table + affect_repo.h/.c, wired into the same player_save()/player_load() choke point every other per-player save already goes through (quit!, combat defeat, the 5-minute linkdead auto-purge, and the explicit save command all get this for free from one place). Named player_ACTIVE_affect rather than the shorter player_affect -- that name turned out to already be taken by an unrelated, unused (0 rows) leftover table from the upstream SneezyMUD seed schema, and CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS silently no-op''d against its incompatible columns on the first attempt, caught live via debug logging before it ever shipped. Also worth knowing: a quick linkdead reconnect (within the usual 5-minute window) was NEVER actually losing affects even before this change -- that path reuses the same live character in memory rather than reloading from the database. This persistence layer only matters for a genuine quit!-then-relog, the 5-minute purge, or a server restart. AFFECT_CHARMED (a summoned pet''s bond) and AFFECT_POLYMORPH (a temporary transformed body) are deliberately never saved -- both live on a mob, not a real player, and were never going to reach this code path anyway. New tests/smoke_test_affect_persistence.py verifies the whole loop live: casts Sanctuary, quits, reconnects as the same character, confirms the buff is still listed with a sane remaining duration, confirms it''s still ACTUALLY reducing incoming damage (not just showing on the display), and confirms it still wears off cleanly afterward with no double-counting bug from the reload. Along the way, confirmed the existing smoke_test_affects.py has been broken by two separate, unrelated, already-partially-documented issues since well before this session (the known "load obj lands in the loader''s own inventory" gap, plus a newer one: combat messages no longer include a raw damage number at all -- describe_dam() replaced it with a qualitative word like "pathetically" for everyone, mortal and immortal alike -- so that test''s damage-number parsing can never collect a sample anymore either) -- flagged separately as its own follow-up, not fixed here. Verified live on the Home server: zero build warnings, full pass.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`get all`, `get all.item`, and `drop all` now work', 'User: "drop all should drop all items in inventory", "and all.items should also work for get". Three additions to cmd_object.c, all classic Diku/Sneezy syntax that Tobin was missing: bare `get all` picks up every takeable item on the room floor (previously only the two-word `get all <container>` -- emptying a named container like a corpse -- existed, and a bare `all` fell through to a literal "item named all" lookup that always failed); `get all.<name>` (dot syntax) picks up every item on the floor matching `<name>`, useful when there are several of the same thing lying around, reusing the exact same per-keyword-prefix matching find_obj() already does for a single item, just collecting every match instead of stopping at the first; and `drop all` drops every LOOSE carried item (worn/held items are untouched, same as a single `drop` already requires -- `remove` something first to drop it). All three share one new get_all_from_room() helper for the get-side pair, and reuse the existing per-item message/log/trigger/save plumbing so the room, logs, and inventory persistence all behave identically to using the singular commands one at a time. Verified live: `get all.torch` picked 2 torches off a 3-item floor (torch, torch, rock) leaving the rock behind, plain `get all` then picked up the rock, and `drop all` correctly emptied the resulting inventory back onto the floor. Confirmed the existing two-word `get all <container>` feature (emptying a chest) still works unchanged. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Newbie characters get real starting gear -- 6 class suits, `loadsuit`, and the Welfare Department', 'User: "we need 6 sets of newbie equipment to load on the character when connecting for the first time... a shield and a weapon based upon class choice", plus the Welfare Department''s social worker as a lost-gear reissue and a new `loadsuit` immortal command backed by a builder-extensible database table. A fresh character previously got NOTHING at all -- being_create_pc()/player_create() only ever set up stats, no starting inventory whatsoever. New `suit`/`suit_item` tables (db/tobin/suit.sql): a suit is just a named, optionally class-restricted bundle of obj vnums, so any builder can define more later with two INSERTs and no code change. Seeded all 6 (one per class): every suit gets the same training shield (vnum 1010, real upstream item) and a class weapon -- three reused from real existing "training"-tier items (177 staff/Mage, 325 dagger/Thief, 329 sword/Warrior) and three newly added at the identical stat profile since no training-tier mace/sickle/nunchaku existed anywhere in the seed data (90003 mace/Cleric, 90004 sickle/Druid, 90005 nunchaku/Monk -- reclaiming the numeric gap between the two existing Tobin-owned vnum blocks, 90000-90002 and 90010-90013, rather than opening a new range), plus a shared torch (105) and backpack (600). Items land loose in inventory, not auto-equipped -- a new player wears/wields them themselves. `loadsuit <suit name> [target]` (cmd_loadsuit.c, level 56+, same senior tier as addnews/help-edit) lets a builder or admin load any suit onto themselves or someone in the room, by full or abbreviated name. The Welfare Department''s social worker (mob vnum 90, room 570 -- both already present in the real seed data, nothing new to add there) now actually does something: saying "gear"/"equipment"/"newbie"/"supplies" to her reissues your own class''s suit on the spot, via the original engine''s own real spec-proc id for exactly this NPC (SPEC_PROC_NEWBIE_EQUIPPER, 147, already seeded on that mob -- just needed Tobin code to act on it, same SPEC_PROC_DOCTOR/SPEC_PROC_LAMPLIGHTER precedent). Also, while hunting for free vnums for this: `vnum <room|obj|mob> <range>` now lists the FREE/unused vnums in that range too, not just what''s taken -- makes reclaiming a gap like 90003-90005 a lookup instead of a guess. New tests/smoke_test_newbie_gear.py (16 checks) verifies the whole loop live: fresh-character suit issue, loadsuit on self and on a named target, a bogus suit name refused cleanly, and the social worker reissuing on request while staying silent for unrelated speech. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings across a 6-file feature.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Street sweepers, refuse haulers, and trash collectors now tidy up for real', 'User: "let the street sweepers and dirty refuse haulers get items casually from the room and take them and drop them off in surplus (room 563)", then "trash collectors too." A deliberately DIFFERENT mechanic from the existing ACT_SCAVENGER cleaner-mob behavior (which destroys loose OBJ_CAT_TRASH items outright) -- this one relocates ANY loose takeable item to the real seeded Surplus donation room instead of destroying anything ("for the surplus items you would like to give to charity... take it and use it"), and only applies to mobs matching the named real-world archetype by keyword (name contains "sweeper"/"hauler", or "collector" AND "trash" -- deliberately excludes tax collectors, which only match "collector"), not the ACT_SCAVENGER flag. A mob can have both behaviors at once with no conflict -- actual junk still gets swept away by one, anything usable gets donated by the other. No real pathfinding exists (same scope-down precedent as the lamplighter mob) -- a matching mob picks up whatever''s loose in its CURRENT room (25% chance/tick), then periodically teleport-delivers everything it''s collected to Surplus and back (15% chance/tick once carrying something), with room-visible messages at both ends. Verified live: a spawned street sweeper picked up a dropped rock, announced "hurries off with an armful of odds and ends," and the rock was confirmed sitting in room 563 moments later -- and the room already had OTHER real donated items (backpacks, torches, swords) sitting there from ordinary background world ticks before this test even ran, confirming the mechanic was already working across the live world the moment it deployed.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`catchup` now only replays real communication, not ambient flavor', 'User: "catchup command should only record communications not theme messages." Previously EVERY async message routed through descriptor_notify() -- tells, says, and shouts alongside mob wandering, combat, weather, and every other room-echo flavor message -- got held and replayed via `catchup` for anyone mid-editor, since descriptor_room_echo() (the shared room-broadcast helper nearly everything ambient uses) calls descriptor_notify() under the hood for each recipient. Split into two functions: the existing descriptor_notify() now simply DROPS a message outright (not held, not replayed) if the recipient is mid-editor, becoming the correct default for ambient/theme messages with zero changes needed at ~55 existing call sites; a new descriptor_notify_comm() keeps the OLD hold-and-replay behavior, switched in at the small, well-defined set of genuine player-to-player/immortal communication call sites: tell, say, shout, whisper, wiznet, and the newbie channel. A few borderline cases were deliberately left as ambient (dropped, not held) rather than communication: `promote`''s "you''ve been promoted" notice, `group`''s follow/join/split status notices, and the `system` broadcast (its own doc comment already frames it as "atmospheric," not chat) -- all status/flavor notices about game state, not something you''d expect to "catch up on" like a missed conversation. New tests/smoke_test_catchup_comm.py verifies live: a tell and a say sent while the recipient is mid-editor both show up in their later `catchup`, while an ambient "gets a rock" room echo sent in the same window never shows up at all. Regression-checked smoke_test_notify.py (passes clean, one transient re-run needed -- a pre-existing timing flake unrelated to this change, confirmed via the standard "reproduces on different lines each run" precedent).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`edit trigger` now confirms saves, shows you the current script, and drops the raw id', 'Fixed from live testing while the user was actually driving the menu: "triggers don''t save, no option in editor for saving." The script body itself always saved correctly (`/s`, unchanged), but editing a trigger''s match text or chance percent (options 1/2 in the item detail view) commits immediately with NO confirmation message at all -- easy to read as nothing happened, since the only sign of success was the screen silently redrawing with the new value. Both now send an explicit "Match text saved."/"Chance percent saved." (or a failure message) before redrawing. Second fix: "need a chance to read the trigger" -- the item detail view now shows the trigger''s current script body inline, so a builder can read what''s already there without opening the script editor (option 3) just to look. Third: "forget the use of id, use only vnums" -- the raw database id is no longer shown anywhere in the menu (the list, the item header, the prompt), and the old `edit trigger delete <id>` quick command is gone entirely; deletion is menu-only now, reached by vnum and list position like everything else. Also noticed and fixed along the way: `edit trigger list <vnum>` never showed a random-type trigger''s chance percent, only its match text -- it now shows `chance=N%` too, matching what the interactive menu already displayed. Every fix was live-reproduced against the deployed build first (a raw-socket-driven immortal session working through the actual menu), then re-verified the same way afterward. All 5 trigger smoke tests updated for the id removal and pass clean. Deployed via copyover with a player connected at the time -- their connection was restored both times, never dropped.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`tell` now keeps a history, and `reply` sends to whoever last told you', 'Found by reading the original SneezyMUD''s own systems docs (the long-queued "docs/systems review" TODO item, finally done as a research pass): the original logs every `tell` to a `tellhistory` table, capped at 25 rows per recipient, and backs a `reply` command off `desc->last_teller` -- Tobin''s `tell` had neither, a plain miss rather than an intentional cut. New `tell_history` table logs every tell (both directions, capped the same way -- oldest dropped first once a recipient passes 25); `reply <message>` sends to whoever most recently told you without retyping their name, resolved from a new live-only `last_teller` field on your connection -- same as the original, this is NOT saved anywhere, so it starts empty again on a fresh reconnect (only the history itself persists). The history exists mainly so an immortal investigating a harassment or abuse report over tells has something to actually check, rather than nothing at all. Verified live: a tell sets the recipient''s last_teller, `reply` reaches the original sender correctly, both directions get logged, and sending 30 tells in a row to the same person confirms the 25-row cap actually trims the oldest ones rather than growing forever. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New toggles: `notell`, `afk`, and immortal `mute`/`unmute`', 'Follow-up to the tell-history docs/systems review: the original also has a family of communication toggle bits Tobin was missing (`noshout` already existed and needed no work). `toggle notell` blocks incoming tells from strangers -- with the original''s own exception baked in: a tell from whoever YOU yourself last told still gets through, so a conversation you started doesn''t get blocked on you. `toggle afk` opts in to an automatic "is AFK" notice tacked onto a tell someone sends you once you''ve actually gone idle (same 5-minute idle window `who`''s "(idle)" tag already uses) -- your tell still comes through either way, the sender just also gets told you might not see it right away. New immortal commands `mute <player>`/`unmute <player>` let a god shut down a misbehaving player''s `tell` and `shout` on the spot (the original''s god-imposed silence, PLR_GODNOSHOUT) -- online-only, and you can''t mute someone your own level or higher. The original also silences "emote" under this same ban, but Tobin has no freeform emote command to gate (only pre-written social actions like `smile`/`wave`, which carry none of the same abuse risk as free-typed text), so that part is a documented skip rather than a fake cover. A profanity filter (`pg13`) came up in the same review but was explicitly passed on -- a real text filter is a bigger feature than a flag, not something to bolt on as an afterthought. Verified live, including a real test of the AFK notice actually firing once idle (checked against a build with the idle threshold temporarily shortened, not by waiting five real minutes). Deployed via copyover with a player connected throughout, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Backstab, rescue, and trip are now real commands, not dead roster entries', 'A user-requested audit of the full spell/skill roster (skill.c, ~230 entries) turned up something worse than a few missing effects: roughly 20 level-1 Warrior/Thief/Monk skills were LISTED in the roster (visible in `skills`, gated by level) but had zero code anywhere -- typing them just hit "Command not found," the same as a typo. First batch fixed, lowest level first per the user''s own priority order: `backstab <target>` (Thief) opens a fight with a x4 damage opener, but ONLY works before either side is already fighting -- a failed roll still reveals you and starts a normal fight, same "attempt made, detection guaranteed" spirit as a botched real backstab. `rescue <ally>` (Warrior) swaps an ally''s attacker onto yourself mid-fight, refusing a self-target or an ally who isn''t actually threatened. `trip` (Warrior) is a pure knockdown -- no damage at all, unlike bash, matching its real-game reputation as a positioning skill rather than an offensive one. All three reuse the same one-skill_roll_success()-roll shape bash/kick/disarm already established, registered in cmd_table.c with abbreviation-collision notes where they share a prefix with an existing command (rescue needs "resc"+, since "rest" already owns "res"). New tests/smoke_test_skillcombat2.py (7 checks) passes live. Remaining level-1 gaps (steal, sneak, grapple, berserk, rally, and the whole Monk basic-stance cluster) each need a genuinely new subsystem first (a real item/gold-theft mechanic, a persistent hidden-state flag, a restrain effect, a toggleable combat stance) rather than a quick wire-up -- logged in TODO.md as the next items in this same audit, lowest level first.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`steal` is now a real command', 'Continuing the spell/skill functional-completeness audit, lowest level first: `steal gold <target>` lifts a level-scaled 10-40% of a victim''s wallet; `steal <item> <target>` lifts a specific loose (not worn/held) carried item. Both reuse cmd_plant.c''s Thief reverse-pickpocket gate/chance shape in reverse -- out of combat, non-immortal target, mutual `toggle pk` consent, level-gap-scaled success chance -- rather than inventing a new one. A failed roll takes nothing but tips the victim off ("You feel a hand in your belongings!"), matching `plant`''s own "something seemed suspicious" precedent; unlike the real SneezyMUD, a failed steal does not additionally provoke a fight here, a disclosed simplification. New tests/smoke_test_steal.py (8 checks: gold conserved across the transfer, victim keeps gold on a forced failure, stolen item lands in the thief''s inventory) passes live. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Sneak, grapple, berserk, and rally are now real commands', 'Continuing the spell/skill functional-completeness audit: `sneak` (Thief) toggles quiet movement, suppressing your own arrival/departure notice while it''s on -- broken outright the moment you enter combat. `grapple` (Warrior) locks BOTH combatants down for a few rounds on success, reusing the existing wait-state system as the "restriction" rather than inventing a new restrain flag. `berserk` (Warrior) is a new timed rage affect -- while it''s up, your hits can''t be parried, and nobody can `rescue` you out of the fight, matching the roster''s own description word for word. `rally` (Warrior) is the first "buff everyone else in the room" skill in the game -- a battlecry that gives every ally present (anyone but your own current opponent) a temporary strength boost, reusing the stat-affect machinery built for the Stupidity spell a few sessions back. New tests/smoke_test_skillcombat3.py (12 checks) passes live. This closes out the level-1 batch of the audit; garrotte/throatslit (Thief) and the Monk basic-stance cluster remain, pending a research pass into what those actually do in the original game. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Garrotte and throatslit round out the level-1 Thief murder skills', 'Closing out the level-1 batch of the spell/skill functional-completeness audit: `garrotte <target>` (only works to open a fight, like backstab) applies a real ongoing Garrotte disease effect instead of a one-time hit -- the real upstream needs a dedicated garrotte tool item that wears down with use, which Tobin has no equivalent of, so this is bare-handed instead. `throatslit <target>` is the same opener shape as backstab but hits even harder (x6 damage vs backstab''s x4), matching its "lethal" billing in the roster -- the real version''s separate instant-death roll needs a wielded weapon and per-weapon damage dice Tobin doesn''t model yet, so it stays a (very hard) normal hit instead of a guaranteed kill. New tests/smoke_test_thiefmurder.py (6 checks) passes live. This wraps every level-1 Warrior/Thief roster entry that had no handler at all except the Monk basic-stance cluster, which needs its own research pass into what those skills actually do before they can be scoped. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'yoginsa gives Monks a real meditate-to-heal command', 'First Monk skill wired up in the spell/skill functional-completeness audit: `yoginsa` (while sitting or resting, out of combat) restores a chunk of HP and Vitality on a skill roll. The real upstream version is a recurring background task that re-checks every few pulses and can chain into self-cures at higher proficiency via a separate "wohlin meditation" skill Tobin''s roster doesn''t have -- scoped down to a single action, matching every other skill-combat command this audit has wired up so far. The rest of the Monk basic-stance cluster turned out to be a mixed bag on closer inspection: jirin/kubo/oomlat aren''t commands at all in the original game, they''re passive combat-math modifiers baked directly into the hit/damage formulas (same shape as Tobin''s own `parry`), and catfall/catleap only have a registration entry in the original source with no code anywhere actually using them -- both need more digging before they can be ported. New tests/smoke_test_yoginsa.py (4 checks) passes live. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'chi gives Monks a real offensive skill, not a healing one', 'Second Monk skill from the spell/skill functional-completeness audit: `chi [<target>]` unleashes a chi-powered strike at a foe, defaulting to your current opponent mid-fight. The roster''s own old description called it a healing touch -- wrong, checked against the real upstream source, which is actually an offensive chi-blast, plus separate self-buff/room-AOE/object variants that all key off a mana pool Tobin does not have, so only the single-target attack made the cut this pass. New tests/smoke_test_chi.py (7 checks) passes live. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Monks get three new passive combat instincts: jirin, kubo, oomlat', 'These three round out the Monk basic-stance cluster from the spell/skill functional-completeness audit -- unlike every other skill in this audit they are not commands at all, they are wired directly into the hit/damage math itself. Jirin gives a Monk a passive chance to dodge an incoming attack (found the real behavior in a fuller reference source: it works against any attack, not just unarmed ones, despite how the skill was originally described). Kubo boosts a Monk''s own bare-handed to-hit and damage. Oomlat gives a bare-handed Monk a defensive bonus in place of armor. All three scale with proficiency. Deployed via copyover, zero build warnings.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Falling is now real, and Monks can catch themselves', 'Rounding out the level-one Monk skill cluster: falling through open sky now deals real, escalating damage depending on how far you drop, occasionally fatal if you fall far enough. A Monk with catfall takes roughly half the damage from any fall. A Monk with catleap can now leap in any direction and glide across open space, even where there is normally no ground to jump from.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'cintai gives Monks another passive to-hit edge', 'First item off the level-5+ list in the spell/skill functional-completeness audit: cintai is a passive to-hit bonus, wired directly into combat math like jirin/kubo/oomlat before it. The roster description called it unarmed-only, but the real source shows it applies to every attack a Monk makes, armed or not -- corrected to match.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'shove and materialize are live -- level 6 audit items', 'Two more items off the spell/skill functional-completeness audit, the first from the level-5+ list: `shove <target> <direction>` (Warrior) pushes someone through a real exit into the next room on success, or starts a fight on failure -- a real, deliberate upstream design, not a bug. `materialize <item>` (Mage) spends 100 gold trying to conjure a cheap-enough item out of thin air, searched against the real object catalog; the gold is gone either way, win or lose, matching the original spell exactly.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'bodyslam is live -- a level 10 audit item', '`bodyslam <target>` (Warrior) lifts a standing foe overhead and slams them down for real damage on success, knocking them sitting; on failure the Warrior is the one who ends up on the ground instead. The real upstream version is a much heavier function with weight comparisons and held-item restrictions -- this is a Tobin-scale slice of it, same spirit as bash and kick.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'curse and slumber are live -- level 13 audit items', '`pray curse <target>` (Cleric) settles a dark aura over a foe that weakens their coordination, making them easier to hit. `cast slumber <target>` (Mage) puts a foe to sleep outright, where they stay defenseless until it wears off and they wake back up on their own. Both are the first items off the audit that needed a real new status-effect type behind the scenes, not just a cosmetic message.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'fear and identify are live -- level 14 audit items', '`cast fear <target>` (Mage) sends a foe running for their life and keeps them too shaken to fight back for a while afterward. `cast identify <item>` (Mage) reveals what something carried is really made of and worth in a fight -- a weapon''s real bonuses, a piece of armor''s real protection, a container''s true capacity -- the same detail the standalone `identify` command already showed everyone, now also reachable as a proper spell.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'headbutt is live -- a level 15 audit item', '`headbutt <target>` (Warrior) is a straightforward skull-first strike for real damage. The original version of this move changed its target depending on how tall the attacker was compared to their foe -- Tobin has no notion of height, so this always goes for the head.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'telepathy is live -- a level 16 audit item', '`cast telepathy <message>` (Mage) sends a thought straight into every mind in the world at once, no matter where anyone is or whether they''re even awake -- unlike shouting, telepathy does not care about sleep or distance.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: slumber could be shrugged off with `wake`', '`cast slumber <target>` puts a foe to sleep via a real forced-sleep affect, but the pre-existing `wake` command had no idea that affect existed -- it just checked "are you in the sleeping position" and stood the target right back up, making the whole spell a no-op against anyone who knew to type `wake`. `wake` now refuses while that affect is still active, so a slumbered target actually stays down until it wears off.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'yoginsa now sits you down automatically', '`yoginsa` (Monk) used to refuse outright if you were standing, forcing a separate `sit` first. It now sits you down itself and gets straight into meditating in the same command.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'meditate and penance are live; Druid gets its own meditate', '`cast meditate <target>` (Mage, Druid) and `pray penance <target>` (Cleric) both shipped with the original roster import doing nothing but consuming their component/symbol -- their old flavor text (mana regen, "divine favor") described mechanics Tobin has no equivalent for. Realigned all three with yoginsa''s own shape: a real Vitality restore, yourself by default or an ally you name. Druid had no meditate-equivalent at all before this -- added.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Skill/spell proficiency gains are now announced', 'Every time a skill or spell''s proficiency actually ticks up a percent from using it, the player now gets a message saying so, instead of the gain happening silently in the background.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'invisibility and dispel invisible are live -- level 17 audit items', '`cast invisibility` (Mage) makes a caster or a named ally untargetable by name and invisible to the room listing for anyone but an immortal -- a new AFFECT_INVISIBLE, checked at both `combat_find_room_target()` and the room-listing code. `cast dispel invisible` strips it back off. Real upstream also grants a temporary armor bonus and doubles duration on a critical success; neither ported this pass, same "Tobin-scale slice" spirit as the rest of this audit list. No `detect invisibility` counter-check yet either -- that is its own separate, higher-level roster entry, not built this pass.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'spin is live -- a level 17 Warrior audit item', '`spin <target>` grapples an opponent to the ground for damage, same shape as bodyslam/headbutt. Refuses while your primary hand is holding something, and refuses a flying target unless they are already fighting you. Real upstream also has a proficiency-graduated one/two-hand easing and separate countermove/focused-avoidance defenses; neither ported this pass, same "Tobin-scale slice" spirit as the rest of this audit list.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Real XP-to-level curve wired in; HP-per-level rebalanced by class', 'The old placeholder XP curve (level squared times 100) is replaced with real SneezyMUD''s own level 1-50 table, one shared curve for every class -- confirmed with the user that this matches how the real game actually works today (its own source comment says it deliberately unified what used to be separate per-class tables). Separately, how much max HP each class gains per level is now based on real SneezyMUD''s own per-class numbers (Warriors gain the most, Mages the least) instead of the old guessed multipliers. The `balance` command''s existing class/race HP multiplier still works the same as before, layered on top of these new base numbers.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'gdb was not actually installed on this droplet', 'Every "reattach gdb to catch crashes" step this session silently failed with a missing-command error, but an empty crash-log grep looked identical to "no crash happened" -- so it went unnoticed until a copyover-triggered reattach actually surfaced the failure. Installed now and confirmed genuinely attached. Worth checking that gdb itself is actually running (not just that its log is crash-free) the first time this is done on any new box.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Editors no longer cut off long pastes', 'Pasting a long room description (or help topic, news, wiznews, rules, or trigger script body) into the line editor used to silently drop everything past a couple hundred characters on a single line, with no warning. The real limit on how much can arrive on one line has been raised well past what any real paste needs, and the final saved-text limit for all of those editors has also been quadrupled for headroom.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'teleport and summon are live -- level 19 audit items', '`cast teleport` (Mage) sends yourself, or a named target, to a genuinely random room in the world -- not a chosen destination, despite the old roster text implying otherwise. `pray summon` (Cleric) pulls a named player from anywhere in the world into your own room, refusing an immortal target outright and requiring mutual `toggle pk` consent for a mortal one. Both verified correct by hand; the automated test for these hit an unrelated, still-open engine issue (see the next entry) so it is not fully automated yet.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Open bug: a brand-new character can occasionally land in the wrong spot', 'Creating several characters in quick succession can occasionally cause one of them to show "Welcome back!" on its very first-ever login and land in the default starting room instead of its real intended room. Not yet root-caused -- flagged in TODO.md for a proper look. If you notice a fresh character somewhere unexpected, that''s this.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'springleap, slam, deathstroke, riposte, and dispel magic are live -- level 20 audit items', '`springleap` (Monk) springs you up from sitting or resting instantly. `slam` and `deathstroke` (Warrior) are two more heavy single-target strikes, the hardest-hitting yet -- deathstroke needs a wielded weapon. `riposte` (Warrior) is passive: a successful parry now has a chance to guarantee your very next swing lands. `cast dispel magic` (Mage) strips every active magical effect from yourself or a target -- a deliberate departure from the original spell (which only worked on enchanted items), reworked to actually do something useful in Tobin.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'blindness and word of recall are live -- level 21 audit items', '`pray blindness` (Cleric) blinds a target -- they can''t look at anything, and swing less accurately while it lasts. `pray word of recall` (Cleric, and Druid at a higher level) pulls yourself or an ally straight back to Center Square from anywhere, refusing to work in a few especially defended rooms.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'taunt and paralyze limb are live -- level 22 audit items', '`taunt` (Warrior) pulls a mob''s attention off whoever it''s currently fighting and onto you instead -- a straightforward aggro pull, since Tobin has no multi-attacker mob AI for the real game''s subtler version of this to hook into. `pray paralyze limb` (Cleric) leaves one of a target''s arms or legs unresponsive until someone cures it -- no cure exists yet, so treat it as permanent for now. Also shipped this session: yoginsa (Monk) meditates automatically again instead of needing to be re-cast every time, a fallen enemy''s gold now lands in a lootable pile on their corpse instead of vanishing straight into your pocket (a bug meant it never actually dropped for a monster kill -- fixed), and the combat countdown in your prompt now shows tenths of a second.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Spellcasting mobs now carry what they need to cast', 'Mage and Druid mobs now spawn holding a pouch of spell components, and Cleric mobs spawn holding a holy symbol (better materials at higher levels, wooden up through mithril) -- without this, a mob''s own cast/pray attempts always failed for lacking the same component/symbol a player would need. Applies to every mob that spawns from now on, world-wide.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'mudstats now shows accounts, characters, and codebase size', '`mudstats` reports account count and player-character count alongside the existing room/mob/object counts, plus a live line-of-code total for the codebase itself.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'copy, haste, and storm call are live -- level 23 audit items', '`cast copy` (Mage) duplicates a scroll you''re carrying, as long as you already know the spell bound to it. `cast haste` (Mage) makes you (or an ally) genuinely faster in a fight -- a real bonus attack every round, not just a stat bump. `cast storm call` (Druid) calls down lightning or hail on a foe, but only works outdoors during rain or a real storm.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: give, and pour can now fill another container', 'You can now `give` a carried item or gold to someone else in the room. `pour` also got smarter: pouring one container into another transfers the liquid between them, instead of only ever being able to dump it on the ground.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: look in/inside a container now works', '`look in <container>` and `look inside <container>` used to always fail with "You don''t see that here" -- fixed. Both now show the container''s contents, same as plain `look <container>` already did.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'enhance weapon is live -- level 24 audit item', '`cast enhance weapon` (Mage) gives you or an ally a temporary edge in combat, making your blows land more true for a while. The real spell was meant to be a permanent weapon enchant; landed here as a timed buff instead since there''s no way yet to permanently mark a single weapon apart from every other copy of the same item.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A dozen new spells and prayers are live -- level 25 audit batch', 'Mage: `cast farlook` scries a remote location, `cast detect invisibility` lets you see and target the invisible, `cast detect magic` and `cast trail seek` are new flavor effects, `cast bind` webs a foe in place so they can''t move, `cast animate` calls a stone golem to your side, `cast scribe <spell>` inscribes a scroll from nothing, `cast charge stave` refills a staff''s charges, and `cast flaming flesh` wreathes you in protective fire. Cleric: `pray restore limb` heals a destroyed limb (the real cure for `pray paralyze limb`), `pray knit bone` mends a broken bone, `pray bleed` opens a wound on a foe, and `pray heroes'' feast` heals everyone in the room. Druid: `cast refresh` restores movement.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Level 25 fighting techniques are live -- Warrior/Thief/Monk', 'Warrior: `whirlwind` strikes every foe in the room, `kneestrike`/`chop` are hard single hits, `switchopponents <target>` lets you disengage and pick a new fight, `trance` is a defensive stance, and `weapon retention`/`brawl avoidance` now give you a real passive chance to shrug off a disarm, trip, or grapple. Thief: `stabbing` is a hard single hit, `subterfuge <target>` redirects your opponent''s aggression onto someone else. Monk: `chop`/`hurl` are hard hits (hurl can throw a foe clean out of the room), `feigndeath` plays dead so aggressive mobs leave you alone, and passive skills now do real things in a fight -- `counter move` resists being shoved, `iron fist` hits harder bare-handed, `blindfighting` softens the penalty for fighting blind, `critical hitting` leans your hits toward the harshest outcomes, and `chain attack`/`blur`/`advanced kicking` all give barehanded fighters a real chance at a bonus strike each round. `wohlin meditation` now unlocks bonus self-cures while meditating. This closes out level 25, the single biggest batch of skills and spells in the whole audit.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: duplicate character sessions', 'The existing multiplay gate only checked account_id across live connections -- it never actually checked whether the SAME character (player_id) was already loaded elsewhere, so a relogin (or two people racing the same login) could put two live copies of one character in the world at once. New `world_find_active_pc()` (world.c/h) finds any PC by player_id, active or linkdead; `enter_world()` (descriptor.c) now disconnects the stale connection and reclaims the character on the new one, matching SneezyMUD''s own reclaim-on-relogin behavior.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New `stats` command -- world statistics, always accurate', '`stats` (level 55+) shows total rooms/mobiles/objects/accounts/characters, plus how many are currently online/loaded/linkdead. Every seeded-content number is a live count straight from the database, so it can never go stale or reset wrong after a reboot or copyover the way an in-memory counter could.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Copyover now restores loose room mobs/objects (with a duplication bug fixed along the way)', 'cmd_copyover.c now dumps every currently-loaded room''s top-level mobs (vnum+HP+position) and objects (vnum) into the recovery file; game_loop.c restores them after the exec. Scoped to top-level contents only -- nested container/corpse contents and mob-worn equipment still do not survive. Caught a real duplication bug during verification: zone_boot_all() already re-places every zone''s permanent starting population before the recovery code runs, so restoring the dump unconditionally doubled fixed room furnishings. A world-wide max_exist gate (mirroring zone.c''s own duplicate check) did not catch it -- those fixtures are capped at 9999, effectively uncapped for 1-2 extra copies -- so it''s now a room-local existence check instead: skip restoring a mob/obj if the room already has that exact vnum. Verified live end-to-end, including that the fix self-heals an already-duplicated room back to the correct count.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Door state now syncs across both sides (reverses an earlier design decision)', 'New sync_reverse_door() in cmd_open.c: opening/closing a door now also updates the destination room''s reverse-direction exit, IF that side genuinely has its own door_type set too -- treating it as the same physical door viewed from two rooms. A doorless reverse exit (edroom''s own default for an auto-created one) is left completely alone. LOCKED and SECRET are deliberately not synced -- LOCKED is cmd_lock.c''s own concern, and a door can be secret from one side but obvious from the other by design. This reverses an earlier, deliberate "each exit is independent" decision from the original door-mechanics session, per explicit user request.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Group rewrite: leader/follower movement, gtell/gt, assist', 'Three pieces: (1) new move_followers_along() in cmd_move.c drags a plain follow-relationship follower along on the leader''s movement (recursing through chains), gated on follow alone not the grouped flag, matching SneezyMUD''s own moveGroup(). (2) new cmd_gtell.c broadcasts to every grouped member (gt resolves via ordinary prefix-matching, no new alias entry needed). (3) new cmd_assist.c reuses the existing pet-auto-assist multi-attacker precedent already proven in combat_process_run() -- Tobin''s pairwise fighting pointer already supports a second attacker piling onto a shared target without any new combat infrastructure, so assist just sets the assister''s own fighting pointer and leaves the target''s alone.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Command parser audited against SneezyMUD -- Phase 1', 'Pulled the real SneezyMUD command table straight from its source (568 commands total) and compared it against what Tobin actually has. Turns out socials were already a complete import (155 of them, generated from the same upstream file) -- once those are accounted for, 262 real commands are genuinely missing. Those are now written up as a categorized comment block at the top of cmd_table.c so nothing gets silently forgotten: which ones are admin/dev tooling Tobin''s architecture doesn''t need, which are systems we''ve deliberately skipped (mail, factions, and the like), and which are real gameplay gaps worth picking up later. A few commands that looked missing turned out to already exist under different names (redit/medit/oedit/fedit is our unified `edit` command; "feign death" is `feigndeath`) -- noted so they don''t get re-flagged as gaps. Reordering the whole table to match Sneezy''s exact abbreviation order was deliberately NOT done this pass -- that would mean reshuffling how every single command abbreviates against every other one, which needs a full regression sweep to do safely, not something to gamble on live. This is a documentation-only change; nothing about how any command actually behaves changed.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Limb damage and blood-pool generation halved', 'New being_hurt_limb_only() (being.c/h): a hit still deals its full damage to overall HP, but only HALF of that to the specific limb it landed on -- wired into both combat_strike() (melee) and combat_apply_skill_damage() (skills). Blood pools spawn when a limb crosses into a bad status tier, so halving how fast limbs get there naturally halves blood-pool frequency too, without a second separate probability roll. describe_dam()''s severity text now uses the halved value so messages match what the limb actually took. Verified live: summed implied limb-HP loss over 12 real combat rounds came out to 0.48x overall HP loss (expected ~0.5). All 3 existing limb regression tests re-confirmed passing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`wear all` added', 'cmd_wear() split its per-item logic out into a new static wear_one_item(d, ch, o, announce) helper; `wear all` walks ch->base.stuff_head once and calls it for every THING_OBJ, passing announce=false so unwearable/held/slot-full items are silently skipped instead of spamming failure messages (matches SneezyMUD''s own `wear all` syntax, confirmed via lib/help/wear in the reference tree). Worn items stay on the same stuff_head chain (is_loose() filters them out of `inventory` display, not a real unlink), so a plain forward walk during the loop is safe -- nothing gets skipped or double-visited. New tests/smoke_test_wear_all.py covers three armor slots equipping plus a held-only weapon being skipped.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'NEWBIE-flagged items now disappear on drop', 'New action_flag field on the runtime obj_t (obj.h/obj.c): previously the ITEM_* extraFlags bitmask (action_flag, DB column same name) only existed on obj_proto_t for oedit/stat display -- copied onto the live instance now at obj_create_from_proto() time, same precedent as wear_flag. New ITEM_NEWBIE (1<<24, obj.h) matches OBJ_ACTION_FLAG_NAMES[24]/SneezyMUD''s own bit (misc/obj.h, confirmed against the reference source). cmd_drop.c: new drop_one_item_check_newbie() runs after every drop (both `drop <item>` and `drop all`) -- if the just-dropped item has ITEM_NEWBIE set AND is empty (no stuff_head children), it explodes with "The X explodes in a flash of white light!" (verbatim from SneezyMUD''s misc/inventory.cc) echoed to the whole room including the dropper, then obj_destroy()s it. The empty-only check matters: a newbie-issue bag full of real loot should not vanish along with its contents. Did NOT port the upstream''s room-vnum-range exclusion (`in_room > 80 && != DONATION`) -- Tobin has no equivalent "immortal void"/donation-room vnum convention documented, and guessing a threshold to match upstream''s own zone numbering would be inventing behavior, not porting it. New tests/smoke_test_newbie_item_drop.py covers all three cases (normal item survives, empty NEWBIE item explodes, non-empty NEWBIE container survives).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Object anti-race flags added (ANTI_HUMAN/ELF/OGRE/DWARF/HOBBIT/GNOME)', 'Tobin-only design -- SneezyMUD has no per-race wear restriction to port (only per-class, action_flag''s ANTI_CLERIC/ANTI_MAGE/etc bits, already fully assigned across all 32 bits). New dedicated `anti_race_flag` column/field (obj_repo.h/obj.h/obj.c, migration in tobin_migrations.sql) rather than overloading action_flag. Six bits (obj.h''s ANTI_RACE_HUMAN etc) map 1:1 onto player_race_t''s (being.h) declaration order, so obj_race_forbidden(o, race) is a plain shift-and-test. Enforced in cmd_object.c''s wear_one_item() (wear) and do_hold_or_wield() (hold/wield) -- checked before slot logic so a barred item never occupies or reports a slot it was never allowed into. Exposed in oedit as a new "18) Anti-race flags" toggle submenu (CONN_OEDIT_ANTI_RACE_FLAGS, descriptor.c/descriptor.h), same [x]/[ ] toggle-by-number pattern as the existing Extra/Take flags submenus. `stat obj` decodes it into the same "[ NAME ]"-joined display as action_flag/wear_flag. New tests/smoke_test_anti_race.py covers: a human-barred item refusing a human but fitting a dwarf, a dwarf-barred weapon refusing wield, and the oedit toggle submenu actually persisting a bit to the DB.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`uptime` command added', 'New tobin_boot_time() (main.c/game_loop.h): a static time_t set unconditionally near the top of main(), so both a cold boot and a copyover successor each stamp their own fresh value -- neither preserves any other in-memory world state either, so "uptime" here means "time since this process generation last started", the same thing a copyover visibly resets. New cmd_uptime.c follows the classic DIKU/CircleMUD "Up since <date>, Y days, H hours, M minutes, S seconds" shape (no SneezyMUD equivalent found to port verbatim). MORTAL_LEVEL_MIN, like `time`/`weather` -- informational, not a builder/immortal tool. New tests/smoke_test_uptime.py confirms the command reports a small elapsed time right after a fresh boot/copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'oedit item-type picker: now lists every type instead of guessing a number blind', 'New show_oedit_type_picker() (descriptor.c) -- menu item 3 ("Item type") now prints every raw itemTypeT name and number (obj_type_name()/new obj_item_type_count(), obj.c/obj.h) before prompting, same two-per-line layout as the Extra/Take/Anti-race flags toggle submenus. An invalid entry now redisplays the full list with a short correction instead of the old bare "check stat/vnum on a similar object first" message -- that hint is gone since the list itself is now right there. No new CONN_OEDIT_* state was needed; CONN_OEDIT_TYPE'"'"'s existing number-entry flow is unchanged, only what gets shown before/after. New tests/smoke_test_oedit_item_type_picker.py covers the listing content, a successful pick, an out-of-range rejection, and confirms Quit-without-Save leaves the DB row untouched.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Menu-driven loadsuit editor added (`edit suit`)', 'New `edit suit [name]` (56+, cmd_edsuit.c) -- suits could only be populated by hand-written SQL before this. No name lists every suit (id/name/class/item count/description); a name opens a menu editor on the first substring match, or auto-creates a brand-new empty suit if nothing matches (same precedent as oedit/medit). New CONN_EDSUIT_* state machine (descriptor.c/.h) follows CONN_TRIGEDIT_*''s shape rather than CONN_OEDIT_*''s -- a variable-length list of child rows under one parent, every field commits immediately via suit_repo.h''s new CRUD functions, no working copy or separate Save step. Addressed by list position in the UI but by obj_vnum (suit_item''s own natural key) internally, never a raw db id. The actual feature this unlocks: suit_item got a new `quantity` column (tobin_migrations.sql, default 1 so every existing row is unchanged) -- a suit can now grant MORE than one of the same item (two wrist bands, two boots, ...), where the old (suit_id, obj_vnum) primary key meant an item was either in the suit once or not at all. suit_grant() (suit.c) now expands each row by its own quantity, instantiating that many separate obj_t instances. New tests/smoke_test_edsuit.py covers creation, adding items (including a quantity >1), loadsuit actually granting the right counts (not just the right vnums), changing an existing quantity, deleting an item, and setting/clearing the class restriction. Re-ran the pre-existing tests/smoke_test_newbie_gear.py too -- zero regressions.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Newbie equipment system expansion: per-race gear, rations/drink, spellpouch/components, holy symbols', 'Second, independent suit dimension alongside the existing per-CLASS one: new `race` column on `suit` (tobin_migrations.sql, NULL = not race-restricted, same convention as `class`), new `suit_repo_find_for_race()` (suit_repo.h/.c), and player_repo.c''s player_create() now grants BOTH the class suit AND a race suit at creation. Six new race suits (newbie_gear_race.sql: human_race/elf_race/ogre_race/dwarf_race/hobbit_race/gnome_race) wire up the 11-piece cloth armor set + racial weapon + shared training shield already pre-seeded at vnums 36930-37002 (user-supplied ranges; the elf range as originally given, 36974-37984, was a typo overlapping gnome/hobbit -- corrected to 36974-36984, the only range consistent with every other race''s own 11-item pattern). `SUIT_MAX_ITEMS` bumped 8 -> 16 (a race suit needs 13 rows, the old cap wasn''t enough). All six class suits also gained: 3x ration (vnum 403) + 1x waterskin (410) for every class (using the new per-item quantity column rather than 3 separate rows); Mage/Druid additionally got a small spellbag (321) + 3 mage-tagged components (200/201/202) -- verified against cmd_cast.c''s find_keyword_item(ch, "component") that consumption only checks for the keyword, not a class-specific match, so the same three items work for both classes despite their "component mage" name tag; Cleric additionally got 3x wooden holy symbol (500). Verified live with a new tests/smoke_test_newbie_gear_race.py (race-specific gear differs correctly between Dwarf/Human/Ogre, universal rations+waterskin, Mage spellpouch+components, Cleric holy symbols, and a Warrior control case getting neither) plus a clean re-run of tests/smoke_test_newbie_gear.py -- zero regressions. One test-methodology pitfall hit and documented in the new test''s own comments: `quit!` is DELIBERATE, pre-existing behavior that drops a mortal''s entire inventory on the floor (cmd_quit.c, user 2026-07-12) -- checking inventory after a quit!+relogin cycle (a pattern copied from unrelated tests that needed it for room placement) destroyed the very gear under test; fixed by checking inventory immediately after character creation instead.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`loadsuit`/`edit suit` fixed: number-driven, self-targeting works, suits can be deleted', 'User-reported bug: `loadsuit human_race jesus` -- jesus being the caller''s OWN character -- failed with "No one by that name is here." Root cause: cmd_loadsuit.c''s target lookup reused combat_find_room_target() (combat.c) for "find who to load gear onto", but that function deliberately excludes the caller (`if (t == &self->base) continue;`) since it exists for combat targeting, where attacking yourself makes no sense -- loadsuit has no such restriction. Fixed by checking the caller''s own name (thing_name_matches(), same prefix-match convention as everywhere else) before falling through to combat_find_room_target() for anyone else in the room. Also addressed the user''s explicit design ask ("loadsuit by name is quirky, lets be number driven"): both `loadsuit` and `edit suit` now take the suit''s numeric id (as shown by `edit suit` with no argument) instead of a fuzzy substring match against suit.name -- `suit_repo_find_by_name()` is now dead and removed. `edit suit`''s old "unmatched name auto-creates" behavior is replaced by an explicit `edit suit new <name>` verb, since auto-create-on-typo doesn''t make sense once the primary argument is a number. New capability: `edit suit`''s menu gained an `X) Delete this suit` option (new suit_repo_delete(), new CONN_EDSUIT_DELETE_CONFIRM state distinct from the existing per-item delete-confirm) -- deletes the whole suit row and, via the existing FK ON DELETE CASCADE, every one of its suit_item rows, with a yes/no confirmation first. Updated help topics for `edit`/`suit`/`loadsuit` to reflect the id-based usage. tests/smoke_test_newbie_gear.py and tests/smoke_test_edsuit.py updated for the numeric interface (including a dedicated self-target-by-own-name check reproducing the exact reported bug); smoke_test_edsuit.py also gained a 7th case covering suit deletion end-to-end. All three suit-related smoke tests pass with zero regressions.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Room vnum on bug/idea reports; new `typo`/`deltypo`; `purge <target>`', 'Three small TODO.md items in one pass. (1) `bug`/`idea` reports (bug_repo.c/idea_repo.c) now capture the reporting player''s room vnum alongside the existing text (new `room_vnum` column, both tables, NULL for reports filed before this landed) -- user: "may help reproduce a bug for testing." Shown in the immortal listing as "(room NNNN)" when known. (2) New `typo`/`deltypo` (cmd_typo.c/typo_repo.c, new `typo` table) -- user: "add a typo command in the same way" -- a direct third mirror of `bug`/`idea`''s shape (file/list/delete, room vnum included from the start), its own [TYPO] log type (LOG_TYPO, log.h/log.c/cmd_setsev.c). (3) `purge <target>` (cmd_purge.c) -- the one form from SneezyMUD''s original fuller purge that was actually requested ("add purge target to purge a single target"): destroys one named mob or object in the room instead of clearing everything, same "N.name" ordinal + prefix-match convention as combat_find_room_target(), and same "never a player" rule as bare purge. New tests/smoke_test_typo.py; smoke_test_bug.py/smoke_test_idea.py gained a room-vnum check; smoke_test_purge.py gained targeted-purge coverage. Also fixed a stale pre-existing bug IN smoke_test_purge.py while touching it: `load obj` has landed straight in the loader''s own inventory (not the room floor) since 2026-07-22, but this test never picked up that change and was asserting on a room listing the object was never in -- added a `drop` before each check. Also backfilled `LOADSUIT_MIN_LEVEL` into tests/tools/cmd_abbrev_check.py''s level table, a pre-existing gap (from the loadsuit work two sessions ago) that made the abbreviation checker hard-fail before it could check anything.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Territory/Homeland system (fresh Sneezy -> Tobin audit item)', 'Started a new audit pass over SneezyMUD''s docs/systems/ reference docs (the 2026-07-19/07-27 audits already closed out every item that had been explicitly logged in TODO.md, which is now fully checked off) -- Territory/Homeland (docs/systems/important/territory-system.md) had never been looked at before. Real upstream: a permanent sub-race "homeland" chosen at creation right after race, with a DIFFERENT list of 4-8 options per race (Urban, Villager, Mountain, Forest, Recluse, Mariner, ...) on its full 12-stat system, modifiers up to +/-30. Scoped down for Tobin''s simpler 6-attribute set: one shared 3-option set (Urban/Rural/Wilds) usable by every race alike instead of 6 separate race-specific tables, with modifiers (+/-3, zero-sum, being.c''s new territory_stat_bonus()) sized to sit alongside race_stat_bonus()/class_stat_bonus()''s own +/-2..4 range rather than dwarfing it -- same "Tobin-scale slice" precedent as the banking/crafting/materials systems. DELIBERATELY not a forced step between race and class like the real upstream -- that would have shifted every existing test''s race-then-class creation script out of alignment, so it''s option 5 of the existing options screen instead (same menu as handedness/gender/alignment/appearance), defaulting to NO homeland (zero bonus) if never visited -- unlike alignment''s Neutral default, every real territory has a non-zero effect, so defaulting into one of them would have silently reshaped every new character''s stats. Caught that exact mistake live during testing (an earlier draft DID default to Urban, silently applying its -3 STR/-3 CON/+3 INT/+3 CHA to every new character and breaking the pre-existing smoke_test_accounts.py) and fixed it before shipping. Also caught a second real bug: the territory bonus was originally folded into attrs alongside race''s/class''s at the attrs-point-buy "done" step, but territory isn''t actually chosen until later (the options sub-menu) -- moved to the options screen''s own "done" handler, the real finalization point. New `player.territory` column (tobin_migrations.sql); shown in `score` as a new Homeland line and in `stat player` (cmd_score.c/cmd_stat.c). Purely flavor + stats, same as race -- grants no skills. `tests/smoke_test_territory.py` (21 checks) passes live, including confirming Urban vs Wilds are genuine mirror images and homeland persists across a reconnect. Deployed via a real in-game `copyover` while an actual player was connected -- verified their connection survived cleanly both times a fix required a redeploy.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Newbie race gear: paired limbs (arms/wrists/hands/legs/feet/fingers) now get TWO items, not one', 'User: "edit the newbie load gear to include two of the limbs that require two eq slots like arms hands wrists legs feet etc." Root cause: the 2026-08-02 per-race starting-gear suits (newbie_gear_race.sql) granted exactly one of each armor piece -- a sleeve, bracelet, glove, legging, boot, and ring -- but every one of those wear_flag bits (ARMS/WRISTS/HANDS/LEGS/FEET/FINGERS) maps to a PAIRED limb (LIMB_LEFT_ARM/LIMB_RIGHT_ARM, etc, being.h), so a fresh character could only ever cover one arm, one wrist, one hand, one leg, one foot, and one finger -- the other half of each pair stayed permanently bare. wear_slot_for_flag() (obj.c) already fills the second matching limb (prefers right, then left) whenever a second item with the same wear_flag exists to wear -- nothing there needed to change, this was purely a starting-gear DATA gap. Fixed with one bitwise UPDATE against suit_item (matching wear_flag & (FINGERS|LEGS|FEET|HANDS|ARMS|WRISTS)) that bumps quantity to 2 for exactly those rows, across all six race suits at once, using the same per-item quantity column the menu-driven loadsuit editor added. Verified live: a fresh Human Warrior''s inventory now shows "(x2)" for cloth slipper/legging/glove/bracelet/sleeve/ring, and wearing each twice correctly lands one on each side (confirmed via `equipment`: left/right foot, hand, wrist all filled). Also noted, unrelated to this fix, while doing a broader regression pass this session: tests/smoke_test_account_delete.py appears to be failing independently of any change made this session (the account menu''s character list isn''t showing after a re-login mid-test) -- flagged as a separate follow-up, not investigated further here.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`eyes of Fertuman` (Mage 22) -- last gap in the level-21/22 audit batch', 'Continuing the spell/skill functional-completeness audit: level 21/22''s only remaining unimplemented roster entry (verified against disc/disc_mage_alchemy.cc''s real eyesOfFertuman() directly, since docs/systems/ doesn''t cover individual spells). `conjure elemental earth` (Mage 21) turned out to already be implemented, sharing cmd_cast.c''s generic "conjure elemental" handler with fire/water/air -- nothing to do there. Real upstream is a world-wide locate-by-name: scans every live object and character for a name match and reports which room each is in, with a critical-success roll widening the result cap and an ITEM_MAGIC flag giving some resistance to being found. Scoped down for Tobin: no crit tier, no magic-item resistance chance, a flat 5-result cap instead of skill/level-scaled, and object results limited to room-floor items only (an object nested in a container or carried in inventory has no reliable room -- thing.h''s own doc comment already flags `roomp` as stale for anything not directly in a room, so those are honestly left out rather than reported with a wrong location). Excludes immortals and the caster''s own name from results, same spirit as the real upstream excluding shopkeepers/immortals. New static "counting visitor" pair for world_for_each_mob()/world_for_each_obj() (cmd_cast.c, same idiom cmd_load.c''s count_mob_visit()/count_obj_visit() already use). `tests/smoke_test_level21_22_gap.py` (5 checks) passes live: refuses without a component, no-match/no-argument messaging, finds a room-floor object with a real room name, and confirms an immortal is excluded from results.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'XP granted per hit, not at the end of a fight; MariaDB OOM incident + fix', 'User: "we want xp gain calculated per hit, not at the end of a fight... you can report on how much gain at the end of the fight but the actual gain should be per hit." New combat_award_hit_xp() (combat.c), called from both combat_strike()''s ordinary melee path and combat_apply_skill_damage() (skills/spells): credits a proportional share of a kill''s total reward (same victim_level*50 formula, same level-weighted group split as before) as each hit actually lands, silently -- combat_defeat() no longer grants any XP itself, it just prints ONE "You gain a total of N experience from that fight" summary per recipient, reading from a new transient being_t.xp_gained_this_fight accumulator. A level-up can now happen mid-fight, not just at the kill -- same max_hp-recompute/full-heal/practice-points payoff, just applied the moment it''s earned instead of stalled until the mob dies. Real, unrelated incident found WHILE testing this: this droplet is memory-constrained (445MB total RAM) and MariaDB got OOM-killed live, causing a silent ~34-minute DB outage (explains a "shops are broken" report and several confusing test failures during that window) -- restarted it. Learned from that: deliberately did NOT keep an unconditional player_progress_save() on every single hit for every group member (real, avoidable DB load on a box this tight) -- only saves immediately on the rare level-up case; the two direct combatants already get saved every ROUND regardless via the existing mid-fight-persistence hook, which is durability enough for the common case. tests/smoke_test_xp_per_hit.py (7 checks) passes live. Also fixed 4 pre-existing tests broken by the previous session''s territory-forced-step change (smoke_test_levelup_hp.py/smoke_test_combat.py/smoke_test_crit.py/smoke_test_mid_fight_persist.py all needed one more numeric input between race and class) -- these were directly blocking verification of this session''s own combat change.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Combat trainer placed on Perimeter Road; Welfare worker greets arrivals with her gear list', 'Two small user requests. (1) "make the combat trainer load somewhere on perimiter road" -- mob vnum 239 ("trainer combat fencing expert instructor", the expert fencer, the only literal "combat trainer" match in the seed data) now spawns in room 111, a real Perimeter Road room whose own description already says it meets the East King''s Road "along the outer edge of the town." New db/tobin/zone_reset_tobin.sql, kept deliberately SEPARATE from the machine-generated zone_reset.sql (that file does an unconditional DELETE+re-INSERT of the whole table on every apply -- a row added directly to it would get silently wiped the next time apply-tobin-schema.sh runs); this file sorts after it under the C-locale filename sort apply-tobin-schema.sh already uses, so it always re-applies AFTER that wipe, not before. (2) "surplus welfare worker should list whats available for her when walking into the room" -- new mob_ai_greet_newbie_equipper() (mob_ai.c/h), called from cmd_move.c right after a mover''s own arrival echo: any SPEC_PROC_NEWBIE_EQUIPPER mob in the destination room now lists every item in the arriver''s class suit the moment they walk in, same suit_repo lookups the existing "say gear" reissue (cmd_say.c) already uses -- separate from and doesn''t change that flow, which stays the only way to actually receive the gear. Verified live: walking into room 570 now shows the Tobin City social worker''s full gear list for the arriving character''s class. Also fixed tests/smoke_test_newbie_gear.py, broken by the same territory-forced-step gap as the previous session''s 4 fixes.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Vitality regen +25%; limbs shows health words alongside the raw %', 'User: "vitality gains too slow, adjust it up 25%" -- regen_tick_run() (regen.c) now heals vitality at regen_amount()*5/4 each tick, HP''s own rate left untouched. User: "limbs command, list health words not %" -- new shared health_word_for_pct() (extracted from being_health_word(), being.h/being.c) used by cmd_limbs.c; kept the raw percentage in parentheses alongside the word rather than dropping it, since smoke_test_limb_damage_rate.py''s damage-ratio math genuinely needs the exact number, not a coarse word tier. Fixed 5 more pre-existing tests broken by the territory forced-step gap plus this format change (smoke_test_regen.py/smoke_test_vitality_terrain.py/smoke_test_limbs_cmd.py/smoke_test_limb_damage_rate.py/smoke_test_taunt_paralyzelimb.py) -- the damage-rate test also had a real regex bug of its own (didn''t account for %3d''s right-padded spacing inside the parens), now fixed too.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Combat discipline tier narrowed to proficiency-only; focus attack ported (automatic)', 'User: "all other skills belong in basic and advanced class discipline" / "just weapon proficiency in combat" / "barehand slash stab proficiency etc" -- SKILL_TIER_COMBAT (skill.c) now holds ONLY the 5 literal "<type> proficiency" rows (slash/blunt/pierce/barehand/ranged) per class; every other skill that used to be Combat tier (riding, parry, dodge, kick, backstab, steal, sneak, search, disarm, chop, counter move, switch opponents, etc -- roughly 43 roster rows across all 6 classes) moved to SKILL_TIER_CLASS (Basic) or SKILL_TIER_ADVANCED, split on each skill''s own existing min_level (< 25 -> Basic, >= 25 -> Advanced, matching the level range each tier already covers elsewhere in the roster). This is a real gameplay change: any of those skills now practices/gates off Basic or Advanced discipline percentage instead of Combat discipline. Separately, user: "in tobin its a warrior skill" / "focused attack should be automatic" -- focus attack (already a Warrior roster entry at level 5, never implemented) now fires automatically inside ordinary melee (combat_strike(), combat.c): same "reroll toward a MAJOR limb" mechanic critical hitting (Monk) already uses, gated on knowing focus attack instead, with the real upstream''s own flavor message ("You focus intensely...") when it lands on a major limb -- no separate command, no manual arming/cooldown, a deliberate simplification of the real upstream''s SKILL_FOCUS_ATTACK/AFF_FOCUS_ATTACK (a manually-triggered, cooldown-gated command there, verified against cmd_focus_attack.cc/crit_combat.cc directly). New tests/smoke_test_skill_tiers_focusattack.py (6 checks) passes live: Combat section lists only the 5 proficiency skills, riding moved to the general list, and focus attack''s flavor message appears automatically over several real combat rounds with no command typed. Also fixed smoke_test_practice.py for the territory forced-step gap.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Focus attack halved; vitality drains while fighting; autoloot reports what you got', 'Three small user requests, all deployed live via copyover mid-session (also mid-session: the droplet''s RAM was bumped from 445MB to 947MB by the user, requiring a reboot -- tobin_c and its gdb crash-watcher were restarted afterward, same PID preserved by copyover''s own execl() the second time around). (1) "focused attack is firing too much, decrease success by 50%" -- the automatic focus-attack reroll (Session 122) added last session is now gated behind an extra 50% coin flip in combat_strike() (combat.c), on top of the existing major-limb reroll odds it already had. (2) "vitality should decrease when fighting to about .75 of a point per round" -- new being_t.vit_fatigue_accum (being.h), a float accumulator that adds 0.75 every round either PC side is actively fighting (combat_process_run(), combat.c) and spends off whole vit points via being_spend_vit() as it crosses 1.0 -- since progress.vit itself is an int, this nets out to exactly 0.75/round on average (3 spends per 4 rounds) without needing vit to go fractional; immortals exempt, same as movement''s own vit cost. (3) "when looting a mob the game should report any inventory changes" -- autoloot (PLR_AUTOLOOT, combat_defeat(), combat.c) used to print one generic "You automatically loot X''s corpse." line with zero detail on what was actually gained; now reports each item by name and any gold as its own line, same per-item message shape manual `get all <corpse>` (cmd_object.c) already uses -- the old generic sentence is gone. New tests/smoke_test_vit_drain_autoloot.py (4 checks) passes live: vitality measurably dropped over several real combat rounds against a durable sandbox dummy (52 -> 40 observed), and killing a mob with autoloot on printed a specific "You loot N gold from ..." line instead of the old generic sentence -- confirmed via a manual debug pass first (the test''s own initial 10-round budget was too tight given the sandbox mob''s randomized to-hit variance, bumped to 25).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Started the "port all special procedures from Sneezy" project; first proc landed', 'User: "lets port over all special procedures from sneezy" / "and apply them to the mobs/objs they modify," scoped as "everything, in original file order." The original engine has 325 distinct special-procedure functions across 58 source files; we had 3 before this, all hand-written one-offs that predate this project. New SPEC_PROCS.md is the tracking checklist -- per-file progress, known blockers (subsystems we do not have yet: factions, disease, pets, real disciplines, pathfinding, a commodity market), and notes for whoever picks this up next, so the work can continue across sessions and locations without losing track. Also built the first real dispatch framework for this (mob_ai.c''s new id-to-function table, mirroring how the original engine looks up its own special procedures) rather than continuing to hand-special-case each one -- that old pattern would not have scaled to 322 more. The first proc ported under the new framework is the chicken''s egg-laying behavior: a rare per-tick chance for a chicken-type mob to lay an egg in its own room. Chosen deliberately as the simplest possible starting case to prove the framework end to end before tackling anything more complex.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Real incident: a debug tool briefly starved and nearly killed a bystander player -- root-caused and fixed', 'While verifying the newly-ported chicken egg-laying behavior above, forcing enough world-simulation ticks to make its rare odds reliable (via the immortal debug tool that fast-forwards world state) accidentally forced hunger and thirst drain, and the resulting starvation damage, on EVERY connected player at once -- not just the sandbox test characters. This silently starved a real player and dropped their health down to almost nothing before it was caught and immediately reversed. Root cause: that debug tool forces every background world process at once, and player hunger/thirst/health drain happened to be one of them, with no guard against it running hundreds of times in a burst. Fixed at the source -- the debug tool no longer touches any connected player''s hunger, thirst, or health at all, ever; it still fast-forwards everything about the world and its mobs exactly as before, just never a real player''s own condition. Also added a new immortal command, `restore <target>`, born directly out of needing to fix this live: fully heals someone''s health and vitality and clears every magical effect on them in one step, for exactly this kind of situation in the future.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Bash requires a shield; vitality drain eased further; auto-stand on full rest; new bank help topic', 'Several small user requests bundled together this session. Bash now hard-requires a shield in hand to attempt at all -- the real original engine actually allows a shieldless bash once a fighter is skilled enough in advanced combat training, but that graduated system does not have an equivalent here, so this was simplified into a flat requirement instead, per the user''s explicit ask. The vitality drain added last session for ongoing fights has been eased another 20% on top of that earlier tuning. Resting, sitting, or meditating (yoginsa) all now stand a character back up automatically the moment both their health and vitality are completely full, instead of leaving them sitting there -- meditation also properly ends itself at that point rather than continuing to run with nothing left to heal. And the bank command finally has a help topic, since it never had one before.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: weapon proficiency skills were permanently stuck at 1% for everyone; now all six classes have them', 'Investigating a user request about how fast proficiency should rise turned up a real gap: the five weapon and unarmed proficiency skills had no way to ever actually improve. Normal skills improve through use in combat, but nothing anywhere in the code was ever calling that improvement check for these five specifically -- so no matter how much anyone fought, they would stay stuck at their starting value forever. On top of that, only two of the six classes even had these skills available at all. Both are now fixed: every class has all five, and instead of needing to be earned through use, they now rise automatically in step with how far a character has trained their broader combat discipline with a guildmaster -- reaching 1% training gives 1% proficiency, 55% gives 55%, all the way up to 100% at full training.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New per-round condition display during combat', 'User request: while fighting, players now automatically see a line each round describing their own condition and their opponent''s, in plain words rather than a percentage -- reusing the same wording already used elsewhere for health status.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Five small fixes: score bank gold, level-up vitality, remove hold, dark light sources, copyover fights', 'Score now shows bank balance next to carried gold -- it was previously only visible through the bank command itself. Leveling up now recomputes and fully refills vitality the same way it already does for health, instead of leaving vitality frozen at its starting value forever. A new "remove hold" (or "remove held") lets a player remove whatever is in their hand by naming the slot itself rather than the item, which matters specifically when it is too dark to have ever seen what they are holding -- picks the primary hand first. A dark room no longer hides every light source along with everything else: a lit item on the ground still shows up tagged as lit, and another person carrying or wielding a lit light source shows as present, while the rest of the room stays dark. And copyover no longer unconditionally ends every ongoing fight -- both sides of a fight are now recorded before the reboot and re-linked once the world comes back, matched by name for another player or by room and position for a mob. That last one has a smoke test written but not yet confirmed passing as of this session -- the test itself kept running into unrelated snags (a stale duplicate test account, then a busy default room defeating its own read logic) rather than showing a confirmed bug in the feature, but treat it as unverified until that test is finished and green. Pick it up next session.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed a hidden crash risk: destroying a mob or player mid-fight could leave the other side pointing at freed memory', 'Found via an old edge case where feign death was refused because the game thought a player was still fighting an already-purged target. Whenever anything is destroyed outright -- purged by a builder, or a player quitting mid-fight -- the game already cleaned up a connected player''s own reference to the destroyed thing so it would not think it was still fighting a ghost. What it never did was clean up the same reference on the OTHER side, when that other side was a mob rather than a connected player -- for example, an aggressive mob that had attacked a player, if that player then quit while the fight was still going, was left pointing at memory that no longer existed, which the next combat round would have tried to read from. Now every destruction sweeps every mob in the world for that same dangling reference and clears it too, closing the gap for good.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Critical hits now describe the weapon that landed them', 'When a limb is finally severed in combat, the message describing it now matches the weapon that did it, instead of always reading the same generic line regardless of what was in hand -- a sword slices it off, an axe hacks it off, a mace crushes it away, a dagger punctures it free, a spear skewers it through, a whip tears it off in its wake, and bare hands keep the original wording.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Thief mobs can now pick your pocket', 'Some mobs seeded from the original game carry a thief personality that was never wired up until now: an awake thief standing in a room with you, not already fighting, has a small random chance each world tick to quietly lift a loose item from your inventory without you noticing -- anything you are actually wearing or holding stays safe, it only ever takes something loose.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: buy ticket from certain mobs teleports you to a fixed destination', 'A handful of seeded mobs carry a "ticket guy" personality that was never wired up until now: buy ticket from one of them, standing and with 1000 gold to spare, and you are whisked away to a fixed destination room -- an old-world one-way travel trick straight out of the original game.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Selling to a shop now costs a bit of tax, and the item does not just vanish', 'Selling something to a shopkeeper now has a small sales tax taken out of what you are paid, the same rate already charged when buying. In exchange, the shopkeeper actually keeps what you sold them instead of it disappearing forever -- it shows up in that shop''s listing, marked as used, and anyone (including you) can buy it back later at the shop''s usual markup.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Kick is now level 1 for every class that has it', 'skill.c had Thief''s kick at level 1 already but Monk''s at level 3 -- the only two classes carrying it. Monk''s is now level 1 too, matching Thief.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Kick extended to Warrior; Monk advanced kicking auto-unlocks on kick mastery', 'Warrior now carries "kick" at level 1 too (skill.c) -- Warrior/Thief/Monk, Tobin''s three non-caster classes, all have it at level 1 now, per the user''s "all classes except casters" scoping. Separately, being_knows_skill() now treats a Monk as knowing "advanced kicking" once their own kick proficiency hits 100%, even below the skill''s normal level-25 gate -- combat_process_run()''s existing bonus-strike check already treats advanced kicking as a trigger, so mastering kick now unlocks that automatically. No direct player-facing surface for the predicate itself (cmd_skills.c''s listing has its own independent level gating, untouched) -- verified statistically instead via a real fight (tests/smoke_test_kick_mastery.py).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Goring mobs ported (SPEC_TUSK_GORING), new per-round mob-combat-action hook added', 'combat_process_run() gained a new dispatch point for mob-initiated combat specials, alongside the existing bonus-strike block: if a mob has a spec proc, mob_spec_dispatch_combat() gets a look each round. First proc wired through it is tuskGoring (id 153): on its own swing, a goring mob has a 1-in-8 chance to attempt a gore against whoever it is fighting, 80% of which land real damage and knock the victim down. Real seeded mobs already carry spec_proc=153 (vnum 11043 boar giant wild among others). Reuses the existing combat_apply_skill_damage() helper (same one cmd_bash.c uses) so immortal damage-immunity is respected -- the first draft did not do this and a code review caught it. This also partially unblocks SPEC_HORSE=16''s kick portion, which needs the same hook shape.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Copyover restore is fast again: mob/object prototypes are now cached during the restart-only recovery pass', 'copyover_recover() (game_loop.c) was re-issuing a full DB query per restored mob/object instance -- with world population climbing every copyover (mostly leftover test-loaded mobs), that meant thousands of duplicate queries for the same handful of prototypes on every single restart, the real driver behind copyover feeling slower and slower. Fixed with an opt-in per-vnum cache in mob_repo.c/obj_repo.c, OFF everywhere except copyover_recover()''s own restore loop -- mob_proto_load()/obj_proto_load() behave exactly as before everywhere else, so medit/oedit live edits and any raw-SQL prototype tweak still take effect immediately with no restart, same as always. Verified live: two consecutive copyovers post-fix both restored in about 1 second despite a still-growing population, versus about 2 seconds and climbing before.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'PK is now XP-neutral; `get`/`remove`/`sell` all gained bulk `all.<target>` forms', 'Player-vs-player kills no longer touch anyone''s experience -- combat.c''s combat_award_hit_xp() skips any PC victim entirely (no per-hit credit for landing blows on another player), and combat_defeat()''s death-XP-loss block now only fires when the winner is a mob, not a PC. Gold looting from a PK kill is untouched; only experience changed. Separately, three commands gained the `all`/`all.<name>` bulk-target convention `get` already had: `remove all`/`remove all.<name>` takes off everything worn/held (or just what matches), and `sell all`/`sell all.<name>` sells every matching loose carried item a shop will buy in one go, skipping (not refusing outright) anything the shop''s category list doesn''t cover. New tests/smoke_test_pk_xp.py, tests/smoke_test_remove_all.py, tests/smoke_test_sell_all.py all pass live.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New admin cleanup tools: `purge <range>` and `zone reclaim <range>`', 'Two new commands for cleaning up ad hoc test-sandbox content that piles up in high vnum ranges across many smoke-test sessions. `purge <low>-<high>` (59+) extends the existing `purge` command to clear loose mobs/objects from every currently-loaded room in a vnum range in one shot, instead of walking rooms one at a time. `zone reclaim <low>-<high>` (59+) goes further and permanently deletes the underlying room/obj/mob prototype rows themselves (plus their satellite tables and any zone_reset/trigger rows referencing that range) -- it refuses outright if another connected player is standing in any in-range room, though the immortal issuing the command can stand in the room being reclaimed. Used it to clean up 665 leftover sandbox rooms, 513 objects, 321 mobs, and 5 triggers that had accumulated in the 900000-1010000 test range across many past sessions -- a live player connection was undisturbed throughout. New tests/smoke_test_purge_range.py and tests/smoke_test_zone_reclaim.py pass live.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`get`/`drop` gain container/target filters, and per-type `edit <noun> reclaim`', 'Rounding out the `all`/`all.<name>` bulk-target convention: `get all.<name> <container>` now pulls only matching items out of a named container (the bare room-floor `get all.<name>` couldn''t reach inside one), and `drop all.<name>` drops only matching loose carried items, alongside the existing bare `drop all`. Separately, `zone reclaim <low>-<high>`''s all-at-once room+obj+mob+trigger sweep now has narrower per-type siblings: `edit room reclaim`, `edit object reclaim`, `edit mob reclaim`, and `edit trigger reclaim` (all 59+), each touching only its own table -- useful when you only want to clean up one kind of leftover test content without sweeping everything else in the range. New tests/smoke_test_get_all_container.py, tests/smoke_test_drop_all_target.py, and tests/smoke_test_edit_reclaim.py all pass live.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Level-1 spell stubs now do something: attune, devotion, sorcerer''s globe, mage sight, entangling roots', 'First pass of the spell/skill stub-audit backlog, working lowest level first (user, 2026-08-04: "lower level players should get a full experience"). All five level-1 stubs from the audit checklist now have real effects instead of a silent no-op: Cleric''s `attune` refreshes and strengthens a held holy symbol (Tobin has no faction system to literally "bind" it to, a disclosed gap); `devotion` restores Vitality on the spot (standing in for a prayer-point resource Tobin doesn''t have). Mage''s `sorcerer''s globe` is now a real room-wide Sanctuary buff; `mage sight` grants genuine infravision (a new AFFECT_INFRAVISION wired into the darkness check) -- true sight/detection were dropped from that spell''s bundle, no matching subsystem for those yet. Druid''s `entangling roots`, the class''s signature level-1 attack, now deals real damage outdoors and is properly refused indoors (per its own roster text) -- along the way, fixed a bug where a spell refused for a reason OTHER than "no components" (like being indoors) still wasted the caster''s component, since the outer cast dispatcher consumed it unconditionally after the spell''s own logic returned. Also broadened the damage/attack-message keyword match in both cmd_cast.c and cmd_pray.c to catch "damaging"/"damages" as well as "damage", which quietly helps several other still-stubbed spells further up the list too. New tests/smoke_test_spell_level1.py (24 checks) passes live. Separately found (not yet fixed, logged to TODO.md): an item survives one quit/relog fine but is silently lost on a second one -- worth a look given the real-player risk if it turns out to be general, not just `load obj`-conjured items.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'PIO feed: character creation and deletion get their own distinct lines', 'The `[PIO]` connect broadcast previously said "X has connected" for a brand-new character too, even though creation always immediately enters the world -- an admin watching the feed saw what looked like a duplicate "has connected" line for one new player (once from creation, once from their very next real login), with no way to tell those two genuinely different events apart. Creation now logs "X has been created under account Y" instead. Character deletion, previously only written to the file-only log, now also broadcasts "X has been deleted from account Y" to the live feed, same as connect/create.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Four more low-level spell stubs fixed: clot, salve, refresh', 'Continuing the spell/skill stub-audit backlog in ascending level order. `clot` (Cleric level 2, Druid level 5) now really stops bleeding -- a targeted cure for the AFFECT_DISEASE_BLEEDING affect, distinct from the broader "cure disease" prayer. `salve` (Cleric level 4) heals properly now; its own roster description ("Treats a minor wound") never contained the "heal"/"cure" keyword the generic dispatch branch was matching on, so it silently fell through before. `refresh` (Cleric level 9) restores Vitality -- Mage/Druid''s identically-named spell already worked this way, Cleric''s copy had just never gotten the same fix. New tests/smoke_test_spell_level2to9.py passes live.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Warrior skills: weapon specializations (slash/blunt/pierce/ranged/barehand)', 'Five new level-1 Warrior skills from the missing-skill audit: `slash specialization`, `blunt specialization`, `pierce specialization`, `ranged specialization`, and `barehand specialization`. All auto-known from character creation -- no guildmaster visit needed -- and grow purely by landing hits with a matching weapon (or bare hands), same passive learn-by-doing shape Monk''s kubo/cintai already use, folding straight into that swing''s hit/damage bonus. The bonus steps up further once a specialization hits exactly 100%. Kept deliberately separate from the game''s existing `slash proficiency`/`blunt proficiency`/etc skills (every class already has those, tracking the shared Combat discipline percentage) -- specialization is its own individually-grindable track layered on top, not a duplicate under a different name. `ranged specialization` tracks proficiency like the other four but has no live bonus to apply yet, since Tobin has no ranged-weapon combat -- tracked for when it does.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Two more spell stubs fixed: hands of flame, remove curse', 'Continuing the stub-audit backlog. `hands of flame` (Mage level 4) now deals real damage -- its own description said "fiery touch attack", one word off from the "flame" keyword the damage-dispatch branch was already checking for. `remove curse` (Cleric level 7) strips the AFFECT_CURSE debuff for real now, same shape as the existing cure poison/cure disease prayers.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Two more spell stubs fixed: illuminate, flare -- plus a real infravision display bug', 'Continuing the stub-audit backlog. `illuminate` (Mage level 2) now really lights up a lamp/torch you are carrying or see in the room, topping off its fuel first if needed -- reuses the same mechanism the mundane `light` command already uses. `flare` (Mage level 3) now really lights up the whole room: everyone present (except immortals) gets true dark-vision for a while, same room-wide shape `sorcerer''s globe` already uses for its shield. While testing, also caught and fixed a real pre-existing bug: the game''s internal list of affect names was missing an entry for Infravision, so anyone checking `affects` after `mage sight` or the new `flare` would have seen garbled text instead of "Infravision" -- fixed for good.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Three more spell stubs fixed: bramble drain, create food, create water', 'Continuing the stub-audit backlog. `bramble drain` (Druid level 3) now really drains life: thorned vines deal real damage to your target AND heal you for a share of it. `create food` (Cleric level 3, Druid level 9) now conjures a real, eatable loaf of bread into your hands instead of doing nothing. `create water` (same levels) really fills a jug, waterskin, or other carried empty container with water, no well or fountain required. Also logged a new backlog item: spell/prayer help text should tell you up front that you need a component pouch (Mage/Druid) or holy symbol (Cleric) before you find out the hard way.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Level 2-9 spell stub audit complete: beast soother, faerie fire, feathery descent', 'Wrapping up the level 2-9 slice of the spell stub-audit backlog. `beast soother` (Druid level 5) now really calls off a fight with an animal and keeps it calm for a while, instead of doing nothing. `faerie fire` (Mage level 6) now really marks its target with a pink aura that makes them easier to hit in combat. `feathery descent` (Mage level 7) now really softens everyone''s falls in the room, same protection the `catfall` skill already gives. Every level 2-9 spell/prayer stub is now fixed -- next up, the level 11-49 backlog.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Cast materialize fixed; obj struct fix (vnums 36930-36996)', '`cast materialize <item>` (Mage level 6) now actually works -- it was routed to nothing before, even though the standalone `materialize <item>` command has worked correctly for a while. Both forms now do the same thing: pay 100 gold, and if a cheap enough item exists with that name, a copy appears in your hands. Also: max/current structure points were corrected to 30 for 67 objects (vnums 36930-36996).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Ten more Mage spell stubs fixed (levels 11-19)', 'Big batch of stub-audit fixes for Mage: `accelerate` and `falcon wings` now really buff the whole room (speed and flight). `sense life` now really scans for nearby life. `stealth` really quiets everyone''s footsteps. `ensorcer` really charms a wild creature into following you. `mage repair` really fixes a damaged item, for free. `faerie fog` really blinds hostile creatures in the room. `galvanize` and `powerstone` now do something real instead of nothing. `calm` really breaks up any fight, not just an animal''s. All ten were previously "nothing happens" placeholders despite looking castable in `skills`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Huge stub-audit batch: 21 more spells/prayers fixed (levels 13-48)', 'The biggest batch yet in the ongoing spell-audit backlog. Cleric: `flamestrike`, `expel`, `numb`, `second wind`, full `paralyze`, and `earthquake` all now do something real instead of nothing. Mage: `shatter`, `infravision`, `true sight`, `cloud of concealment`, `watery grave`, `spontaneous generation`, `Garmul''s tail`, `immobilize`, `energy drain`, `fumble`, `suffocate`, `flight`, `divination`, `silence`, and `ethereal gate` (a real portal to any named room in the game) are all fixed too. `silence` is a genuinely new mechanic -- a silenced target really can''t cast spells or say prayers until it wears off. Every Mage/Cleric spell stub through level 48 is now cleared.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Spell/prayer stub audit complete through level 49', 'Last batch of the Cleric spell-stub audit: `astral walk` and `portal` now really teleport you to any named room in the game. `bone breaker` and `wither limb` really break one of a target''s limbs. `consecrate` and `crusade` really bless everyone in the room. `spontaneous combust` now deals real damage. That closes out the entire Mage/Druid/Cleric spell and prayer stub-audit backlog through level 49 -- every spell listed in `skills` now does what its description says.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Three brand-new spells added: protection from earth, inferno, sterilize', 'Starting on the missing-spell backlog (spells that never existed in Tobin at all, not just broken ones). Mage gains two new spells: `protection from earth` (level 8, a room-wide protective ward) and `inferno` (level 21, a real burning attack). Cleric gains `sterilize` (level 6, cures a festering infection). Most of the remaining "missing spell" list belongs to classes Tobin doesn''t have (Ranger, Shaman, Deikhan) -- these three were the only ones that actually belonged to Mage or Cleric.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Ten new skills added across four classes', 'Cleric, Monk, and Thief can now learn `repair` (same as Warrior already could). Warrior also gains `advanced blacksmithing` (repairs without wearing the item down further), `debride` (undoes some of an item''s accumulated wear), `bloodlust` (a passive combat fury that strengthens your blows the longer you fight), and `stomp` (a new leg-crushing attack). Monk gains `Oomlat Philosophy`, a meditative discipline that really lowers your armor class the more you practice it. Thief gains `swindle`, a knack for haggling that really gets you better prices at shops.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Three more missing skills added: toughness, focused avoidance, evaluate', 'Continuing the missing-skill backlog (generic/cross-class this time -- available to all six classes). `toughness` (combat.c) is a real passive damage-reduction percentage, up to 20% off at 100% proficiency, applied after sanctuary. `focused avoidance` (combat.c) is a real passive to-hit-modifier reduction against the defender, same insertion point/shape as the existing oomlat AC bonus. `evaluate` is a brand-new command (cmd_evaluate.c) -- Tobin has no `compare` command for the real upstream skill to gate, so this ports as its own tiered appraisal command instead: a fuzzed price guess always, condition revealed at 30% proficiency, exact price and material tier at 60%. All disclosed scope-cuts from real upstream''s stacking-buff/agility-scaled-dodge/compare-gating mechanics, same "flat passive/own command instead" shape the last few missing-skill batches already used. New `tests/smoke_test_missing_skills_2.py` (14 checks) passes live; `smoke_test_missing_skills_1.py` and `smoke_test_combat.py` rerun clean, no regressions.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Server now speaks GMCP, MSDP, and MSP', 'First phase of the TobinMUD Client project: the server now offers real telnet option negotiation for GMCP, MSDP, and MSP, three protocols that MUD clients (Mudlet, TinTin++, and others) use to get structured game data and trigger sound cues without cluttering the screen. Right now it pushes character HP/Vitality and current room info, plus a sound cue when you land a hit in combat -- more will follow as the client project continues. Nothing changes for anyone using a plain telnet client.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A native TobinMUD Client now exists (Windows, phase 1)', 'Building on last session''s GMCP/MSDP/MSP server support, there is now an actual TobinMUD Client for Windows: connects automatically, renders color properly, and plays a sound cue when you land a hit. Packaged as a real MSI installer, including a silent/unattended install option. Confirmed working with Mudlet too, independent of this new client. Not yet distributed to players -- still in testing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Real MSP fight music added', 'Combat now triggers a random looping music track via MSP''s real MUSIC marker (separate from the SOUND marker added last session), and stops it when the fight ends. Implemented as a periodic sweep over connected players rather than hooking every place combat can start or end -- there are 30+ of those scattered across the codebase. The new TobinMUD Client (now v0.3.0) supports it, and so does Mudlet.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Mage/Druid mobs now spawn with a real leveled spellbag instead of a flat generic pouch', 'Every Mage-class and Druid-class mob (being_create_mob, being.c) now spawns carrying a real spellbag object -- small for level 1-20, medium for 21-40, large for 41-60 -- with one random real spell-component item from the seed data nested inside it, replacing the old single hardcoded generic pouch of spell components. The component pick is cosmetic only, same as before: cmd_cast.c only checks for the keyword component anywhere on the caster, not a specific item, and there is no per-spell reagent mapping in the seed data to bind a reagent to a specific spell the mob would know. Cleric mobs are unchanged, still getting a level-tiered holy symbol. Verified live with an updated tests/smoke_test_mob_casting_supplies.py, which now looks inside the looted spellbag and checks its contents against the real seed-data reagent list rather than checking the old, coincidental "component" substring in flavor text.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'TobinMUD Client v0.4.0: File menu, Preferences, input focus/repeat-command, titlebar version', 'The Windows client (client/src/win32/main.c) got a real menu bar for the first time: File has Connect/Reconnect/Disconnect/Preferences/Exit, Help has About. Preferences lets you adjust the window size and font size, saved to a prefs.ini next to the exe and applied immediately, including resizing already-visible scrollback text, not just new lines. The input box now regains keyboard focus every time the window is reactivated, and hitting Enter on an empty input line resends your last real command instead of doing nothing. The titlebar now always shows the client version (e.g. "TobinMUD Client v0.4.0") alongside the existing live HP/room status, instead of losing the version once GMCP status updates start arriving. Published to the auto-update host -- every client still running an older version will pick it up automatically the next time it launches.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'cast/pray now show 3 lines of flavor text before the effect, modeled on real SneezyMUD', 'New shared spell_flavor_show() (src/core/spell_flavor.c/.h), called once at the top of both task_cast() (cmd_cast.c) and task_pray() (cmd_pray.c) -- applies uniformly to every spell/prayer in the roster, same one-rule precedent the component/symbol requirement already uses. Modeled on real SneezyMUD spelltask.cc''s TBeing::sendCastingMessages(), which shows a gesture line and a verbal line (each also echoed to the room in third person) plus a completion line, once per ROUND of its multi-round casting task; Tobin has no multi-round casting engine (cast/pray still resolve instantly), so this ports the STYLE -- one random gesture line, one random verbal line, one fixed completion line -- as a single one-shot flourish rather than the full per-round distraction/interrupt architecture. Text adapted from the real spelltask.cc lines, kept separate for Mage/Druid (gesture-and-word) vs Cleric (symbol-and-word) matching the original''s own split. Room echoes use a gendered possessive pronoun (his/her/its), not a blanket "their", per the existing house style. Only fires after every real gate (component/symbol/level/discipline) already passes -- a refused cast/pray shows none of it. New tests/smoke_test_cast_pray_flavor.py covers both cast and pray, gated and ungated. The separate, larger "port the real multi-round casting task architecture" question (distraction/interrupt, per-round success rolls) is still open -- see TODO.md.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'TobinMUD Client v0.4.1: real embedded app icon', 'Root-caused the "titlebar just shows a T" symptom from the v0.4.0 batch: the client had no icon resource at all, so Windows was synthesizing a generic letter-avatar icon for its titlebar/taskbar/Alt-Tab entry. Added a real tobinmud_icon.ico (built from the user-supplied artwork via Pillow, cropped square and rendered at every standard size 16-256px), embedded it as a proper Win32 resource via windres (client/tobinmud.rc, wired into CMakeLists.txt), and set it on the window class. Also set as the Add/Remove Programs icon in the MSI. Published to the auto-update host.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: wake could be blocked by leftover combat/skill recovery lag', 'A sleeping mortal is supposed to be able to issue exactly one command, wake, and nothing else -- but the wait-state (recovery lag) gate in cmd_table.c was checked BEFORE that sleeping-gate exemption and had no exemption of its own, so falling asleep with any leftover wait-state (e.g. right after using kick, a skill, or a spell) blocked wake too until the lag cleared on its own. Found live (user, 2026-08-06): went to sleep voluntarily and wake refused with "You are still recovering!" until waiting it out. Fixed by exempting wake from the wait-state gate the same way it is already exempt from the sleeping gate. Could not get an automated smoke test to reliably reproduce the exact race (attack -> kick -> flee -> sleep -> wake, all within one combat round) due to the already-documented scripted-input desync bug corrupting character creation mid-script -- verified the fix is safe by inspection and a clean rebuild instead (a single added exemption clause, same shape as an existing one, cannot regress anything else).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Real mana pool system for Mage, ported from real SneezyMUD (user 2026-08-06)', 'New progress_t.mana/max_mana (being.h, player_progress migration), being_calc_max_mana() -- Mage-only, `100 + getSkillValue(mana)*3`, a direct port of TPerson::manaLimit(). Druid deliberately excluded: real SneezyMUD spends Lifeforce there, not mana, and Tobin''s own resource_pool_label() (cmd_score.c) already called Druid''s pool "Lifeforce (LF)" before this session -- building a Druid mana pool would have misused that label, not honored it; Lifeforce itself stays a disclosed, not-yet-built gap. New spell_mana.c/.h: a 95-entry name->cost table scraped programmatically from every real `new spellInfo(...)` call in sneezymud-master''s spell_info.cc (its own MANA_<n> constructor argument), joined against Tobin''s actual Mage/Druid roster -- gust really does cost 10, inferno really does cost 54, etc, not invented numbers. cmd_cast.c spends it (Mage only, before the skill roll, immortals exempt) and refuses a cast outright if you can''t afford it; also trains `wizardry` and `mana` proficiency on every cast attempt now (user: "wizardry should also gain automatically from casting"), which is what actually grows max_mana over time via being_calc_max_mana()''s own skill-proficiency term. score/prompt (new PROMPT_FLAG_MANA) both show it for a Mage now instead of a hardcoded 0. `meditate` was completely redesigned mid-session (user: "meditate isnt a spell" / "meditate sits a character down and meditates back to his max mana") -- it briefly existed as a `cast meditate` spell effect, but that was wrong: real Sneezy''s meditate is a passive discipline, not something you cast. New cmd_meditate.c is a standalone command, an exact structural mirror of `yoginsa` (auto-sits you, toggles being_t.meditating, the existing meditate_tick_run() background task does the real work) -- meditate_tick_run() itself is now resource-aware, restoring Mana for a Mage vs HP/Vitality for anyone else (Monk''s yoginsa, unchanged). Two real bugs caught and fixed live during this: (1) the mana_mode check originally excluded immortals entirely, so an immortal Mage''s own meditate silently fell through to the HP/Vitality path; (2) regen.c''s separate, pre-existing "fully rested, stand up" check only ever looked at HP/Vitality, so it was firing on the very FIRST meditate tick for any Mage whose HP/Vit already happened to be full (the common case) and standing them up before the mana tick got a chance to do anything -- regen.c''s check now skips entirely while `meditating` is set, deferring to meditate.c''s own resource-aware completion check. `cast meditate` still exists as a one-line redirect ("...just type `meditate`") rather than being silently swallowed by muscle memory, caught early enough in cmd_cast.c that it costs no mana/component and shows no flavor text. New tests/smoke_test_mana.py (14 checks) -- had to teach it that a live session''s being_t is loaded once at login, so a mid-session SQL edit to player_progress needs a reconnect to actually take effect, a real gotcha the test itself tripped over twice while being written.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'HP and Vitality formulas ported exactly from real SneezyMUD (user 2026-08-06)', 'class_hp_per_level() now uses the exact per-class rates the user gave (Warrior 8.5, Cleric/Thief 5.6=8.0*35/50, Mage/Druid/Monk 5.25=7.5*35/50 -- Druid has no real-Sneezy analog, grouped with Mage/Monk). being_calc_max_hp() is a direct port of real TPerson::hitLimit(): (baseHp()=21 + ageHpMod()=16, both constants since real aging is disabled upstream too) + classHpPerLevel()*level, all multiplied by getConHpModifier() -- a new plot_value() helper in being.c is a faithful port of real plotValue<T,V>() (misc/extern.h), the actual two-branch power curve (power=1.4) upstream uses to turn CON into a smooth 0.8..1.25 multiplier, replacing the old flat linear con_bonus approximation entirely. Tobin''s own `balance` command multiplier still applies, folded into the class-level term on top of the real formula. being_calc_max_vit() is now a direct port of real getMaxMove()/moveLimit(): 100 + 15 + level + plotStat(CON,3,18,13) [+ Iron Legs*2 for a Monk who knows it], reusing the same plot_value() curve with real Sneezy''s own bounds. Real upstream''s per-race move mod, gear/affect move bonus, and asthmatic-quest-bit halving have no Tobin equivalent yet and are simply absent -- a disclosed gap, not a silent approximation. Net effect: a level-3 Mage at average CON now comes to ~52 HP instead of the old ~28. Needed real pow(), so CMakeLists.txt now links libm explicitly.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Combat sound effects now vary by class and weapon', 'The MSP hit sound used to always be the same file. Now it is randomly picked from a real pool: Cleric, Monk, Thief, and Mage each have their own dedicated sounds, and everyone else gets a slashing-weapon sound or a generic hit sound depending on what they are swinging. Players need the matching .wav files in their client''s own sounds folder to hear them.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Real bug fixed: max_hp/max_vit/max_mana stale on login for pre-existing characters', 'player_load() was loading these ceilings verbatim from the DB, never recomputing them -- any character whose row predated a formula change (yesterday''s HP/Vitality rework, or mana shipping at all) stayed stuck at the old/zero ceiling forever, regardless of level. Now recomputed on every login, ceiling only (never auto-heals current values up, so this cannot become a free-heal exploit via relogging). score also reformatted to consistent X/Y display for HP/Mana/Move, and Mana now actually shows its real max.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Help files now show discipline as Basic/Combat/Advanced, not a raw percent', 'The Discipline: line at the bottom of spell/skill help topics used to show a leftover raw upstream 0-100 number that meant nothing in Tobin. It now shows the real tier that skill/spell belongs to -- Basic, Combat, or Advanced -- matching the wording skills/practice already use.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Batch of stubbed skills wired up (pick lock, shoulder throw, retreat, and more)', 'A big batch of long-defined-but-never-implemented skills now actually do something: pick lock/shoulder throw/set-trap-on-a-container are new commands; retreat/counter steal make flee and steal smarter; groundfighting, close quarters fighting, Oomlat Philosophy, power move, and voplat are passive combat bonuses; dufali resists hostile bind/sleep magic; snofalte eases bleeding; knot is a new Mage escape spell. Sling shot, stunning arrow, two-handed specialization, and the arrow/mine/grenade trap variants stay undone -- they need subsystems (ranged weapons, per-weapon two-handedness) Tobin doesn''t have yet.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Casting a spell now takes 2-3 rounds instead of landing instantly', '`cast` (Mage/Druid) used to resolve its real effect the instant you typed the command. Now every gate still works exactly the same (class/level/discipline/mana/component/proficiency roll), but once a cast is committed it takes 2 or 3 combat rounds to finish -- each round shows a few lines of flavor text (purple for Mage, yellow forest-flavored text for Druid) while you are locked out of other actions, and the spell''s real effect only lands once the countdown completes. If your target dies or you go down/disconnect mid-cast, the spell fizzles instead of firing. Cleric''s `pray` is unchanged and still resolves instantly.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: many spells silently stopped doing anything (gust, chain lightning, harm light, and 50 more)', 'A spell/prayer''s roster entry (skill.c) has carried a dead placeholder description ("See help `X` for help.") for a while now -- the real descriptive text moved to the help database at some point -- but `cast`/`pray`''s effect dispatch was still checking that dead placeholder for words like "damage"/"heal"/"bolt" to decide what a spell actually does. Any spell without its own individually-coded effect silently fell back to "nothing happens yet" instead of its real effect. Found via a failing automated test on `gust`. Fixed by having `cast`/`pray` check the spell''s real, live help-file text instead of the dead roster field. 53 spells across Mage, Druid, and Cleric are working again, including gust, mystic darts, chain lightning, meteor swarm, ice storm, tornado, blizzard, levitate, protection from energy, harm light/serious/critical, sanctuary, bless, and armor.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: groundfighting/Oomlat Philosophy/voplat never actually learned from real fights', 'Two bugs: a meditating Monk who sat down already near full health could get stood back up before a hit even landed, and a test-only setup bug (unrelated to real players) meant these three skills looked untrainable. Both fixed -- confirmed live that a real hit while down now trains groundfighting, Oomlat Philosophy, and voplat as intended.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Missing-skill audit batch C: weapon sharpness, drinking, mounted combat, and five Druid spells', 'Closes out the docs/Spell Assignments.xlsx gap audit''s last open batch (batch A and B landed in earlier sessions) -- five real subsystem/design gaps that were previously disclosed as open, not attempted. All five checked against the real original game''s own source before porting anything. (1) Sharpen/smooth: added a real, mutable sharpness number to weapon objects (not persisted across a reconnect -- a deliberate, disclosed scope cut, same spirit as an existing transient-buff precedent). New sharpen/smooth commands raise it using a carried whetstone or file, gated correctly by edged-vs-blunt weapon type, and it now feeds into the existing combat damage bonus for weapon condition -- which also quietly fixes a real fidelity gap where blunt weapons got no bonus from that mechanic at all. (2) Alcoholism: added a real drunkenness stat, gained from drinking alcoholic beverages (a real per-drink strength number ported from the original game, previously dropped entirely), dampened by training the alcoholism skill, sobering up on its own over time. A drunk fighter is measurably less accurate, and getting drunk enough can genuinely knock a character out. (3) The mounted-combat trio: a new charge command (bonus damage plus a knockdown, refuses unless mounted and not already fighting), a calm-mount mechanic that resists being thrown from the saddle by a landed hit, and an advanced-riding skill that improves mounting success and adds to a charge''s damage. None of this needs the original game''s own dedicated mounted-knight class -- every class gets all three, same as riding itself already works. (4) The Ranger beast-charm pair: turned out the original game''s own versions of these two skills were themselves never finished -- literally stubbed out with a "not implemented yet" message in the real source. Wired both into the existing charmed-pet system instead (the same one animal companion already uses), each with its own distinct flavor text. (5) Five Druid spells, all borrowed from the original game''s Shaman spell list the same way several existing Druid spells already were: flatulence (room-wide damage, spares your own group), raze (the single hardest-hitting attack on the whole Druid list, refuses on an immortal), shield of mists (a real defensive buff, harder to hit), living vines (roots a target in place AND makes them easier to hit, outdoors only, same restriction as entangling roots), and thornflesh (a self buff that bites back at anyone who lands a melee hit on you). All three new buffs are real, working effects, not flavor text. New tests/smoke_test_missing_skills_batchc.py covers all five items live against the running server.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Promotion now grants an immortal every class at once', 'Immortals already had access to every skill and spell in every class -- the character''s own stored class just never caught up to that. Now, `promote` sets it to a new "all classes" value the moment someone crosses into immortal level range, and every character who was already immortal before this change got the same update applied retroactively. Demoting someone back to mortal does not restore whatever class they had before promotion -- that information is gone once overwritten, so a real demotion needs the class set by hand.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New login log line: which room someone actually entered', 'The existing has-connected/has-reconnected lines only ever said someone showed up, never where. There is now a companion log line naming the exact room a character lands in every time they enter the world -- fresh login, reconnect, or brand-new character alike -- logged right before that placement happens.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: the long-standing "test hangs/desyncs" mystery -- it was the test tool, not the game', 'The scripted-test tool used to check features had two bugs of its own: it could wait forever if anything kept talking on a socket, and it had no reliable way to tell "this is the real reply" from "this is old leftover chatter" -- which sometimes made a perfectly working feature look broken. Both fixed. The game server itself was checked three different adversarial ways and held up cleanly every time -- this was never a game bug.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Generic combat passives ported: offense, advanced offense, advanced defense, tactics, inevitability, fast heal', 'Six long-missing general skills from the original game, all of them available to every class, now live. Each was checked against the real original source first. Offense and advanced offense are attacker-side accuracy bonuses that grow with use; advanced defense is the defender-side counterpart that makes you harder to hit, sitting alongside the existing defense and focused-avoidance skills. Inevitability, in the original a repeatedly-activated stacking accuracy buff, is ported as a flat passive accuracy bonus instead -- the same disclosed simplification already used for toughness and bloodlust, since this port has no per-round stacking-buff layer. Tactics carries no traced mechanical effect at all in the original source (it is only a learnable, displayed number there), so it is ported as a small learn-by-doing accuracy nudge rather than left purely decorative -- a disclosed, name-driven choice, the same shape the existing wizardry/casting/praying stats already carry. Fast heal likewise has no wired regen formula in this source snapshot, but its name is unambiguous, so it is ported as a passive rest-healing bonus that grows with use; also disclosed as name-driven. None of the six needs a command -- they all train and apply on their own in combat or at rest.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Spell components are now spell-specific; Druid class naming and casting cleaned up', 'Spellcasting components are no longer one-size-fits-all. Each material spell now requires its OWN specific reagent -- the binding data was imported verbatim from the original game (every component object still carries, in its second value field, the spell it is a component for), and 198 of those bindings are indexed at boot. A mortal who lacks the right reagent is now told exactly which one they need; a spell that has no bound reagent in the world still accepts any generic component, so nothing that cast before refuses now. Immortals bypass the exact-reagent rule entirely (the original''s NOHASSLE behaviour). Same-type components brought together now MERGE into a single stack (a direct port of the original TComponent::doMerge -- summed charges up to 100, charge-weighted-average decay), and casting mobs now load reagents for the spells they can cast at up to two levels above their own, nested inside their spellbag. New Mage characters start with the reagents for their earliest spells (mage sight, illuminate, flare). Separately, the Druid class is now presented consistently: the Druid guildmasters/trainers, class components, and a handful of artifact names that still read Ranger/Shaman/Deikhan now say Druid, and the raze attack spell no longer calls upon ''spirits'' (now ''the wrath of the wild''). The world''s Sneezy-specific component-spawn placement table was deliberately not ported.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Rent-config settings now actually save; Windows client updated to v0.4.25', 'Two housekeeping fixes. First: the gamewide rent settings you change with ''balance rent tax/free/keeper'' were silently failing to persist -- the database write used a format specifier the query layer does not accept, so it was rejected every time and the value reverted on the next reboot. That is fixed; rent-config changes now stick. Second: the Windows client has been rebuilt and published as v0.4.25, so every running client will auto-update on its next launch. v0.4.25 adds in-client Trigger and Alias editors (File menu) with an add/update/delete grid, on top of the existing hand-editable text files.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Play in your browser now; Windows client font is Courier New (v0.4.26)', 'You can now play TobinMUD straight from a web browser -- no download, no setup. Head to https://tobinmud.com and click ''Play in Your Browser''. It connects to the same live world as telnet and the Windows client, with full colour and command history. (Under the hood: a WebSocket bridge forwards the browser to the game port.) Also: the Druid guild trainers and their kin no longer read as Ranger, Shaman, Deikhan, or voodoo -- they are all Druids now. And the Windows client''s font has been changed to Courier New, shipping as v0.4.26 (auto-updates on next launch).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Cast/pray shortcuts, and immortals no longer need components or holy symbols', 'A few quality-of-life additions. You can now abbreviate a multi-word spell or prayer word by word -- ''cast sorc globe'' finds sorcerer''s globe, ''pray heal light'' finds heal light, and so on. Immortals no longer need to carry (or spend) spell components or holy symbols to cast and pray. And ''put all <container>'' / ''put all.<name> <container>'' now works, so you can sweep every matching item into a bag in one command (identical spell components stack together as they go in).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Spell components now spawn in the wild (component_placement engine)', 'The second half of the spell-component system is in: reagents can now appear out in the world for casters to forage, not just be bought or looted. A new per-minute engine (component_placement.c) reads placement rules from the new component_placement DB table and spawns (or removes) reagent objects into room ranges on time-of-day and weather windows -- e.g. a rainbow stone that forms in daylight, pixie torches that flicker up in the wood at night and wink out at dawn. It is data-driven on purpose: the rules are DB rows, not hardcoded C, so when the world''s rooms are eventually deleted and rebuilt you just re-seed the rows -- no recompile. Shipped with a small starter set of 6 rules; retune or add via the component_placement table. Ported from real Sneezy''s obj_component.cc placement table, trimmed to Tobin''s single world-wide sky state and plain 0-23 clock.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New `know` command: the Know-X monster-lore skills are in', 'Casters and woodsfolk can now study creatures with the new `know` command. It draws on eight monster-lore skills -- know animal, demon, giantkin, other, people, reptile, undead, and veggie -- and auto-picks the right one from the creature''s own race, so `know dragon` uses reptile lore, `know lich` uses undead lore, and so on. A novice only makes out WHAT the creature is; as your proficiency in that lore grows you also read its vitality, then defenses, then disposition. The eight skills are Druid class skills (Tobin''s nature-lore class, heir to Sneezy''s Ranger); immortals read everything. Race classification is ported verbatim from real Sneezy''s per-race lore kingdoms. This is the first item off the new Tier-1 port priority list.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Clerics can now `turn` the undead', 'The classic Cleric ability is in: `turn <creature>` channels holy will to sear and rout the undead -- and, more grudgingly, demons and devils. Success burns the minion for level-scaled holy damage and sends it fleeing in terror; failure just provokes the fight. Ported from real Sneezy''s doTurn() (its disabled instant-destroy/blind/stun ladder left out, matching live upstream). New Cleric class skill ''turn undead'' at level 6. Second item off the Tier-1 port list -- which is now clear.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Druids can `apply` healing herbs', 'Druids have a new field-medicine skill: `apply [herbs] [target]` presses a poultice of gathered herbs to a wound, mending hit points on yourself or an ally with no mana and no reagent. How much it heals scales with your level and herbalism proficiency, with a short pause between uses -- steady woodland healing, not an instant full cure. New Druid class skill ''apply herbs'' (level 2), a thematic companion to foraging and the wild-component system. First of the Tier-2 Druid ports.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Druid spell: sticks to snakes', 'Druids can now `cast sticks to snakes <target>` -- a tossed stick twists into a living serpent that springs at your foe and fights at your side. The snake''s strength scales with your level (a tiny rattlesnake early on, a gigantic one at high levels), reusing the same one-pet-at-a-time charmed-companion system as the elemental and beast summons. Ported from Sneezy''s sticksToSnakes(). New Druid spell ''sticks to snakes'' (level 15). (Beast summon, its Tier-2 neighbor, is on hold -- it needs cross-room monster pathfinding the engine doesn''t have yet.)')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Quality-of-life batch: char menu order, rent home, clearer move lines', 'A few fixes from the buildable-now list: (1) the character-selection menu now lists your characters highest level first instead of alphabetically; (2) renting now makes the inn your new load room -- rent somewhere and that''s where you wake up next login; (3) wandering mobs going up or down now read naturally ("walks in from above/below", "walks upward/downward") instead of "from the up".')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Sell all now reaches one level into carried containers', 'From the buildable-now list: `sell all` and `sell all.<name>` at a shop now pull matching items out of the bags and containers a player is carrying (one container level deep), on top of the loose-carried items they already sold. Previously they only touched loose inventory, so loot stashed in a pouch was skipped. This matches the container reach `put` and `get all.<name> <container>` already had. The container-descent logic was present in the source but had never actually been built into the running server or covered by a test -- a stale deploy hid it. It is now live, and the sell-all smoke test gained a case that loads a bag, drops a weapon inside, and confirms `sell all.<name>` sells the weapon straight out of the bag and leaves the bag empty. Verified live.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'News and wiznews split off items older than three weeks into an archive', 'The `news` and `wiznews` feeds now show only items posted within the last three weeks. Anything older is reachable through `news archived` / `news old` and `wiznews archived` / `wiznews old`, and an optional page size may follow either form (e.g. `news archived 50`). This is a query-time age cutoff in the shared feed reader, not a data move: nothing is deleted or relocated, re-seeding is unaffected, and an item automatically shows up in the live feed again if its post date falls inside the window. The archive view deliberately does not advance your last-seen bookmark, since it is old content by definition. Verified live with a new smoke test that seeds one current and one month-old item in each channel and confirms the split reads correctly both ways.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Discipline practice halved; weapon specializations moved to the Advanced tier and capped by it', 'Two balance changes from the buildable-now list. First, practicing a discipline now spends TWO practice points per one-to-two-percent step instead of one, roughly halving the old pace: a Basic or Combat discipline now takes about twice as long to max (aimed at the mid-twenties in levels) and Advanced lands around the low fifties. A lone leftover point carries over rather than being wasted, and asking to spend just one point now tells you it takes at least two. Second, the five weapon specialization skills (slash, blunt, pierce, ranged, barehand) plus two-handed specialization moved from the class tier to the Advanced tier: they are no longer auto-known, they unlock only once Basic and Combat are both mastered and Advanced practice has begun, and their learn-by-doing proficiency is now bounded by the Advanced discipline percentage -- fixing the report that a specialization could climb past the max potential shown in the skills and practice views. Weapon PROFICIENCY skills remain in the Combat discipline. All the old special-case bypasses (auto-known in being_knows_skill, the skills-listing display, and the uncapped skill ceiling) were removed, so specializations now flow through the ordinary Advanced-tier gating like any other advanced skill. Tests: the practice smoke test was updated for the new two-point spend cost, and the weapon-specialization smoke test was rewritten around the Advanced-tier gating and the advanced_disc_pct ceiling; both pass live.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Mana and piety gain-and-recovery ported from Sneezy; Monks gain mana, Clerics gain a piety pool', 'Casting resources now recover on their own dedicated ~36-second tick (matching real SneezyMUD updateHalfTickStuff cadence) instead of piggybacking on the 5-second HP/Vitality trickle. Mana recovers via the real manaGain amount -- a flat 24 per tick, doubled to 48 for Mages, quartered while starving or parched, and scaled by the race mana multiplier -- and is position-independent (any non-stunned, non-fighting, sober state), so a Mage refills far faster than the old constitution-based placeholder did. Monks joined the mana classes with a 100 + level-times-three pool (Sneezy Monk/Ranger manaLimit branch), giving chi-self something real to refill. Clerics gained an actual Piety pool (zero to one hundred) that regenerates via the faction-less pietyGain path (about eight per tick), and pray now SPENDS piety as the Cleric casting resource -- costing the prayer its real Sneezy mana value and refusing the prayer when the pool is empty, exactly mirroring how cast spends a Mage mana. Piety persists per character (new player_progress columns applied via migration). Score shows the correct pool per class: Piety for Cleric, Lifeforce for Druid, Mana for Mage and Monk. New smoke_test_mana_piety.py covers the pool sizes, the piety spend, the empty-pool refusal, and live mana recovery; smoke_test_mana.py was updated because mana now recovers fast enough that its old assumption of a still-empty pool between two casts no longer holds.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Casting mana is now spent per round, not all up front', 'A delayed Mage/Druid cast no longer pays its whole mana cost the instant it begins. The multi-round casting task (spellcast.c) now draws the cost a proportional slice each round -- round(cost * rounds_elapsed / rounds_total) -- with the final round squaring the integer-divided slices up to exactly the full cost. The upshot (user request 2026-08-16): a cast that runs to completion costs exactly what it always did, but a cast broken partway through -- lost concentration, a shattered spell, a target that vanished -- only pays for the rounds it actually spent, leaving the rest of the mana unspent. A distraction that extends the cast by a round simply spreads the remaining cost over the new round count. A fumbled incantation is the one exception: it never enters the multi-round task (no rounds to prorate), so it still burns the whole cost, same as before. New being fields cast_mana_cost/cast_mana_paid track the running total; they reset on every cast-end path. Covered by smoke_test_cast_perround.py, which pins both halves: a proficiency-seeded delayed cast charges exactly the full cost, and a cast whose target is purged mid-flight charges strictly less.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A fumbled cast now plays out over rounds too, no longer the per-round exception', 'Finishes the second half of the 2026-08-16 per-round-casting request. Previously a failed proficiency roll in cmd_cast() fizzled instantly and burned the whole mana cost up front -- the one case that never entered the multi-round casting task. It now does: spellcast_start_fumble() (spellcast.c) runs a shorter 1-2 round botched cast (vs a success 2-3) marked by the new being.h field cast_fumble, drawing mana a slice at a time via the same spellcast_pay_round() the success path uses, then spellcast_tick_run() shows a fizzle (no cmd_cast_resolve_effect) once the countdown completes instead of resolving any effect. A fumble broken mid-way -- lost concentration, a distraction -- therefore pays only for the rounds it spent, exactly like a broken successful cast, rather than the old instant full-cost burn. spellcast_show_round() was refactored to take an explicit is_final flag so a doomed fumble never shows the triumphant completion boast before it fizzles. cast_fumble is cleared on every cast-end path alongside the other cast_* fields. New smoke_test_cast_fumble_perround.py seeds gust proficiency to 1 (near-guaranteed fumble), asserts the delayed-fumble opener and a full round of casting animation appeared (not an instant line), the success completion did not, and the completed fumble charged exactly gust full 10 mana via its per-round draws; smoke_test_cast_perround.py still passes unchanged. Deployed live via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Caster mobs now cast real class spells in combat (casters-first slice)', 'First slice of the buildable-now item "mobs arent casting ... fix it so mobs use skills/spells available to them": the caster classes. New combat.c mob_cast_combat(), hooked into combat_process_run() right beside the existing mob spec-proc hook, gives a Mage/Druid/Cleric mob a roughly one-in-three per-round chance to throw a real class spell at its PC opponent on top of its normal melee -- the same additive per-round-action shape as the SPEC_TUSK_GORING proc. The spell is a genuine roster entry the mob class and level qualify for (curated per-class name pools resolved through skill_find() with a min_level gate), and its damage comes from a local copy of cmd_cast.c spell_damage_for_level() (following this codebase small-static-helper duplication convention, same as cmd_use.c own copy), so a caster mob hits exactly as hard as a PC casting that spell. A wounded Cleric mob below half HP that is high enough level to know heal mends itself via being_heal() instead of attacking. Deliberately scoped for this pass: only fires for a mob fighting a PC (the PC-centric combat walk), no component/mana gate and no multi-round cast delay (a mob spell lands the round it is rolled like every other mob combat action), and no per-spell affect fidelity -- spells resolve as their damage/heal core only; blind/curse/slow etc. are a later pass, as are the non-caster classes using their combat skills. Verified live by smoke_test_mob_caster_combat.py: a level-45 Mage mob (vnum 6454) engaged by a high-HP mortal casts a real Mage roster spell at it during the fight ("hurls mystic darts at you"). Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Non-caster mobs now use combat skills too -- mob-skills item complete', 'Second and final half of "mobs use skills/spells available to them" (the caster half shipped earlier the same day). New combat.c mob_skill_combat(), hooked into combat_process_run() right after the caster hook, gives a Warrior/Thief/Monk mob a roughly one-in-three per-round chance to use a real class maneuver against its PC opponent on top of its melee: a Warrior picks bash (str-scaled damage + POSITION_SITTING knockdown + one-round victim lag), kick (str-scaled damage), or trip (knockdown, no damage); a Monk throws a dex-scaled kick; a Thief drives in a heavier dex-scaled knife stab. Each reuses the exact effect core its PC command (cmd_bash/cmd_kick/cmd_trip) applies -- the same 1 + (attr-ATTR_BASE)/4 + rand%4 formula off the mob own attrs so a mob bash hits as hard as a PC bash, the same knockdown + lag, and the same spellcast_distract() nudge a landed bash/kick/trip gives a PC caster mid-spell -- rather than routing through the descriptor-bound cmd_* handlers (mobs have no descriptor). Scoped like the caster pass: no skill-roll miss branch (a mob maneuver just lands, like its spec-proc/spell actions), no disarm/object-manipulation maneuver, only fires vs a PC. combat.c gained #include pulse.h (COMBAT_ROUND_PULSES) and spellcast.h (spellcast_distract). Verified live by smoke_test_mob_skill_combat.py: a level-45 Warrior mob (vnum 1745) engaged by a high-HP mortal lands a real maneuver ("boots you in the head"); the caster test still passes alongside it. Remaining refinement, deferred: per-spell affect fidelity for caster mobs (blind/curse/slow) beyond the damage/heal core. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'From-scratch DB build is now self-contained in tobin/immortal -- no sneezy DB, no upstream seed', 'Done: "eliminate the dependence on the sneezy db ... no more sneezy db seed" (user 2026-08-16). The old bootstrap seeded a database literally named sneezy from the upstream sneezymud-master dumps, then renamed sneezy->tobin, then layered Tobin schema. Now the world seed (82 world files + 10 immortal files, ~19MB) is copied into the project under c_port/db/seed/ (seed/world -> tobin, seed/immortal -> immortal) and owned here, and a new c_port/db/init-db.sh creates and seeds tobin + immortal DIRECTLY, then calls apply-tobin-schema.sh -- the word sneezy appears nowhere in the build path. init-db.sh takes optional db-name args (init-db.sh mud tobintest immortaltest) so a throwaway copy can be seeded for verification without touching live. Fixed a latent cold-build ordering bug found by actually doing a from-scratch seed: newbie_gear_race.sql inserts into suit/suit_item (created by suit.sql) and into the race/quantity columns (added by tobin_migrations.sql), but ASCII sort ran it BEFORE both -- it only ever worked because the live DB already had those from incremental history. Renamed it zz_newbie_gear_race.sql so apply-tobin-schema.sh (which applies db/tobin/*.sql in C-sort order) runs it last. Verified: a full cold seed into throwaway DBs completes with zero errors and produces the identical 116-table tobin + 10-table immortal structure, with Tobin-owned data (suit/suit_item) matching live exactly; the world-row deltas vs live are just seed-snapshot staleness (the seed is a Jul-27 baseline; live builder edits accrue on top and are a separate refresh via update-seed-data.sh). apply-tobin-schema.sh header + db/README.md updated to the new one-command flow. The now-orphaned sneezy database (only stray news/wiznews tables, nothing reads it -- web json generators and the game all use tobin) was backed up to ~/db_backups/sneezy_final_20260816.sql; the physical DROP was left as a manual step (a destructive drop was declined by the safety guard, and it is not required -- nothing depends on it). Legacy sneezymud-master/db/init-db.sh and c_port/db/fix-workbox.sh remain in place but are superseded by the new script.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Room flags DEATH and PRIVATE now have their real movement effects', 'First slice of the "make room flags and sector types have intended effects from sneezy" item (user 2026-08-16). Two flags were stored on rooms but completely inert; both now act in cmd_move.c do_move(). ROOM_FLAG_DEATH (bit 1): a mortal who WALKS into a death-trap room is slain on the spot via new combat_death_room_kill_pc() (combat.c) -- a third sibling of combat_drown_pc()/combat_fall_kill_pc() with the same environmental-death slice (half-heal reset, full-rate XP loss, corpse with the victim gear, eject to account menu), just death-trap flavor; checked right after arrival and before followers are pulled in, so followers are not dragged to their death. Immortals pass through unharmed. ROOM_FLAG_PRIVATE (bit 9): a private room now holds at most two PLAYERS -- a third mortal is refused entry (immortals ignore the cap, mobs are not counted so a shopkeeper/guard does not lock players out). Verified live by new smoke_test_room_flags.py (7 checks: mortal dies walking into DEATH, immortal survives it, 3rd player refused from a full PRIVATE room and stays put, and a walker admitted once occupancy drops to one); smoke_test_doors.py movement regression still passes. Deliberately deferred this pass: ROOM_FLAG_NO_ESCAPE is set on ~2325 live rooms and its exact upstream semantics (does it block flee, or only teleport/recall?) are unclear -- making it block flee would be a large, uncertain balance change, so it was left for a considered follow-up rather than flipped blindly. Other flags (HOSPITAL healing, ARENA no-real-death) and per-sector effects beyond the existing move-cost/fall/water/underwater are also still open. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Doored exits shown in red in both Exits displays', 'User 2026-08-16: "list rooms with doors in Exits display in red color". Both exit displays now color a direction red when that exit has a door (r->exit_door[i] != 0): look s one-line [Exits:] summary (cmd_look.c) and the verbose exits command (cmd_exits.c). In cmd_look each direction now carries its own color tag -- doored ones get red, plain ones keep the room bright sector color -- so the whole list is no longer wrapped in a single sector color; exits_buf was widened 128 to 256 for the extra tags. cmd_exits wraps the doored direction name in red. Red is the dim <r> (0;31m) per the lowercase-color house habit. Verified by smoke_test_exits_door_color.py (doored north red, plain east not, in both displays); smoke_test_exits_display.py regression still passes (a lava room plain exits stay bright-red sector color). Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Two mob-combat bug fixes: no more double skill messages, and goto ends the fight', 'Both reported by user 2026-08-16. (1) DOUBLE MESSAGES: a mob using a combat maneuver (bodyslam/kick/shove/etc. via mob_skill_combat) or an offensive/heal spell (mob_cast_combat) sent the victim BOTH the second-person line ("An elite cityguard sweeps your legs out from under you!") AND the third-person room line ("...sweeps Jesus''s legs out, dropping them to the ground!"). Cause: the room echo excluded the acting MOB instead of the VICTIM, so the victim -- who had already gotten the personal notify -- also received the room copy. Fixed every such echo in combat.c to exclude the victim (descriptor_room_echo(..., victim, ...)), matching the already-correct pattern in the tusk-goring spec proc. (2) CROSS-ROOM FIGHT: "i used goto 43 to escape a fight, and i kept fighting in a remote room". goto teleported the char away but left both fighting pointers intact, so combat_process_run kept resolving the pair across two rooms. Fixed two ways (belt and suspenders): new combat_break_fight() scrubs the char''s own fighting pointer plus every attacker in the room still aiming at it, called from goto_room() while the char is still among its attackers; and a defensive same-room guard at the top of combat_process_run() breaks any pair whose two beings are no longer co-located (also covers teleport/recall and any future move path, and prevents dangling mob->fighting pointers). Verified: smoke_test_mob_skill_combat and smoke_test_mob_caster_combat pass; smoke_test_combat pass (after fixing a stale HP regex in that test unrelated to these bugs -- score sheet now prints "HP: 41/41", the old test expected the retired "N (M Max" format). Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Druid spell: root control (knockdown + damage, outdoors only)', 'Tier 2 spell-port batch, 2026-08-16. Ported real upstream SPELL_ROOT_CONTROL (SneezyMUD disc_shaman_spider.cc, a Shaman-spider spell) onto Druid, same "no Shaman class in Tobin, fold onto Druid" precedent as raze/stupidity/storm call. Root control (cast root control <target>, Druid level 12, outdoors/nature only) commands tree roots to erupt and trip the target: it does damage AND, on a non-lethal hit, knocks the victim to the ground (POSITION_SITTING), rattles them mid-cast (spellcast_distract), and costs them a combat round -- the exact real knockdown mechanic the trip skill uses. This crowd-control knockdown is what distinguishes it from the existing entangling roots (which only damages) and made it a genuine port gap rather than a duplicate. Outdoor gate lives in the outer cast dispatcher (before component consumption), same shape as entangling roots/living vines. Added to the skill roster (skill.c), given a help topic (help root control), and web/help_data.json regenerated. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Five new Druid spells: transfix, transform limb, shapeshift, creeping doom, stormy skies', 'Tier 2 Druid spell-port batch, 2026-08-16 -- the last of the disc_shaman_spider/frog nature line, folded onto Druid (no Shaman class in Tobin). (1) TRANSFIX (level 18): catch a creature''s gaze and hold it frozen -- a real "can''t act" mesmerize (new AFFECT_TRANSFIX), gated in cmd_attack.c (the target can''t attack) and mob_ai.c (a transfixed beast won''t aggress). Refuses an already-fighting target, matching upstream. (2) TRANSFORM LIMB (level 15): reshape one of your own limbs -- gills->waterbreathing, wings->flying, claws->a strength buff (new AFFECT_TRANSFORMED_LIMB stat affect). Self-only; keyword parsed as an early cast interceptor like telepathy/scribe. (3) SHAPESHIFT (level 20): melt into a wild beast (rat/wolf/hawk/lion/dire wolf/bear by level) -- reuses the polymorph descriptor-swap engine. (4) CREEPING DOOM (level 22): a swarm of venomous insects -- damage plus lingering AFFECT_POISON. Upstream never finished this spell, so ported faithfully-in-spirit. (5) STORMY SKIES (level 18): call down a lightning bolt -- outdoors only, and only when the weather is rainy/stormy (weather.h gate in the outer dispatcher). All five: skill roster (skill.c), help topics, web/help_data.json regenerated, smoke_test_druid_batch2_tier2.py. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Four new survival skills: fishing/fishlore, seekwater, encamp, divine', 'Tier 3 utility-skill port batch, 2026-08-16 -- generic skills every class can learn. (1) FISH (fishing + fishlore, level 1/20): stand in or beside water and cast a line for a catch you can eat. Fishlore, if known, upgrades your catches to bigger named prize fish. Upstream is a full multi-pulse task with poles and bait; Tobin has no task-continuation system, so this is the same one-command-one-roll scope-cut as forage/lumberjack, with a per-being cooldown standing in for upstream''s per-room fish depletion. (2) SEEKWATER (level 10): sense whether water is right where you stand or in a neighboring room, and which way. Upstream drives the tracking pathfinder to find water across the whole zone; Tobin has no pathfinder, so this scopes to a within-a-step water-sense (disclosed). (3) ENCAMP (level 5): pitch a camp out in nature (outdoors, not water, not indoors) to recover HP and vitality faster while it stands -- new AFFECT_ENCAMP, wired into regen.c''s regen tick. A rough camp (failed roll) lasts half as long. Camper-only (Tobin has no group-fraction hook). (4) DIVINE (level 15): water-dowsing -- draw drinkable water up from the ground into a held drink container while out in nature. This is water-witching, NOT fortune-telling, and is separate from the Mage''s divination spell. All four: skill roster (skill.c), help topics, web/help_data.json regenerated, smoke_test_tier3_survival.py. Note: Fast load (Tier 3) is DEFERRED -- it accelerates reloading ranged weapons and Tobin has no ranged-combat system to attach it to (same reason Beast summon deferred). All other Tier 3 items (mend, dissect, skinning, lumberjack, climbing, read magic) were already implemented in earlier batches. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Tier 4: speak-a-language system + 8 tongues (Trollish, Avian, Gutter Cant, ...)', 'Tier 4 port, 2026-08-16 -- the speak-a-language subsystem, the last non-blocked entry in the ranked port backlog. New `speak [language]` command picks the tongue you talk in; `say`, `whisper`, and `tell` are then garbled for any listener who has not learned it. Faithful port of SneezyMUD''s garble system (misc/garble.cc): each tongue is a real cross-class skill (skill.c roster, learned-by-doing), and the per-listener garble chance is a port of getLanguageChance() -- it drops as the LISTENER''s proficiency (and their "ear") rise, and as the SPEAKER''s Common fluency and wits rise, so a fluent Common speaker comes through clearer even with a foreign accent. Speaking a tongue trains it; hearing one trains your ear for it. The eight tongues: Common (the lingua franca, never garbled, level 1), Gutter Cant / Gnoll Jargon / Troglodyte Pidgin (street tongues, level 10), and Trollish / Avian / Fish Burble / Bullycroak (racial tongues, level 20) -- levels mirror upstream''s base-vs-advanced-adventuring discipline split. Each tongue''s accent (consonant swaps, injected squawks/gurgles, syllable-chopping) is ported table-for-table from garble.cc. Disclosed divergences: Tobin has no perception stat, so the listener''s "ear" reads Wisdom instead; transforms emit lowercase rather than restoring each word''s original case (a plain-accent look); and the drunk-speech garble is not included. New language.c/language.h + cmd_speak.c; wired into cmd_say/cmd_whisper/cmd_tell. Help topics added (help speak, help languages, and one per tongue), web/help_data.json regenerated, smoke_test_languages.py (11 checks). Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Mage casting text now ported straight from SneezyMUD; Druid gets a matching forest reskin', 'Spell-casting flavor, 2026-08-16 (the "examine how spells are formed in Sneezy and port the approach" follow-up, now given a concrete deliverable by the user: "the casting text from sneezy for mages and a forest themed version for druids"). The multi-round casting flavor a Mage sees while a spell builds (spellcast.c) previously used Tobin-invented lines ("a burning sigil", "harsh guttural syllables"). Those are replaced with the ACTUAL upstream casting text from real SneezyMUD (misc/discipline.cc''s enforceVerbal/enforceGestural): "You begin to chant a mysterious and melodic incantation," "You perform magical gestures with both of your hands," "You trace a magical rune in the air with your hands," "You hop and wiggle about while creating the magical runes in the air...," and the high-wizardry "You concentrate intently upon the magical task at hand...". Kept Mage-purple (upstream splits chant-cyan/gestures-purple, but the ask was the text, not the color). The Druid set was reworked to be a forest-flavored parallel of those same authentic beats -- a sung woodland invocation and growing/green gestures -- staying yellow and deliberately NOT the upstream druid/shaman "rada song / ancestors" voodoo flavor. Only the completion boast has no upstream source (spell completion is per-spell in Sneezy); it is a restrained closer in the same voice. Verified live for both classes. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Druid spell: death mist (level 45 AoE disease) -- Tier 5 now clear', 'Death mist, 2026-08-16. Previously scoped out (real upstream SPELL_DEATH_MIST is DISC_SHAMAN and Tobin has no Shaman class), but the user reopened it: "Death Mist should be high level druid (advanced disc, about level 45), implement that." Ported from disc_shaman_skunk.cc''s deathMist and folded onto Druid, same "no Shaman class in Tobin" precedent as raze / root control / the whole shaman-nature line. Cast death mist (Druid, level 45, Advanced discipline) breathes out a chilling green mist that fills the whole room and infects every other creature in it -- friend and foe alike, EXCEPT the caster''s own groupmates -- with AFFECT_DISEASE_SYPHILIS, Tobin''s real HP-drain-over-time disease affect. Faithful to upstream''s self-infect risk, a failed secondary skill roll leaves the caster infected too. Disclosed divergences from upstream: the extra -10 STR modifier it rides on the affect is dropped (Tobin diseases are fixed-effect by type, no per-cast stat rider), and the isImmune(IMMUNE_DISEASE) waist-gear check is skipped (no disease-immunity item system in Tobin). Added to the skill roster (skill.c), given a help topic (help death mist), web/help_data.json regenerated, smoke_test_death_mist.py. This closes the last Tier 5 item -- the entire ranked spell/skill port backlog (Tiers 1-5) is now fully worked. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Bugfix: immortals now see the right casting flavor for the spell (Druid spells read as Druid)', '2026-08-16. An immortal has no real class (CLASS_IMMORTAL), so the casting-flavor code (spellcast.c) was falling back to Mage flavor for every immortal cast -- an immortal casting a Druid spell like barkskin saw the arcane Mage lines instead of the forest Druid ones (user report). Fixed: for immortals the flavor is now chosen from the SPELL being cast (forest flavor for a Druid-only spell, arcane otherwise) rather than the caster''s stored class; mortals still go by their own class. Guarded by smoke_test_death_mist.py. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Bugfix: 21 spells were taking generic components -- now bound to their specific Sneezy reagents', '2026-08-17. Twenty-one material-component spells were quietly accepting ANY component-keyword item (the generic cast gate) instead of demanding the one specific reagent real SneezyMUD binds them to. Three causes, all fixed. (1) Nine spells whose COMP_FILEMAP decode-name had drifted from skill.c''s exact spelling -- ''sorcerers globe'' vs ''sorcerer''s globe'', ''conjure earth/air/fire/water'' vs ''conjure elemental ...'', ''detect invisible'' vs ''detect invisibility'', ''fly'' vs ''flight'', ''blood boil'' vs ''boiling blood'', ''cardiac stress'' vs ''coronary'' -- so the runtime strcasecmp silently missed and the binding was dead. (2) Six Druid skills (transform limb, beast soother, befriend beast, transfix, barkskin, beast charm) whose reagent objects were already seeded in the world but were missing from COMP_FILEMAP entirely, so they never decoded. (3) Five spells (true sight, blizzard, Garmul''s tail, earthmaw, creeping doom) that had NO reagent object in Tobin OR real Sneezy -- brand-new themed reagents were built for them: a flawless crystal lens, a shard of everfrost, a barbed demon tail, a jagged chunk of bedrock, and a jar of writhing centipedes (obj vnums 37003-37007). To free that vnum block the old 37003-37035 "fashion" clothing/luggage set was removed (rows backed up under ~/db_backups/fashion_*_20260817.sql) along with its stock in shops 218/221/224. Boot-time component index grew 198 -> 209 bindings. All 21 spells'' effects were already implemented, so only the component bindings changed. Guarded by smoke_test_component_binding_fix.py. Deployed via hard restart.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Website: left-margin navigation added', '2026-08-17. The website now carries a consistent left-margin nav sidebar on the Home, News and Help pages -- Home / Play / News / Help plus Download and Source links, current page highlighted. It collapses to a top bar on narrow screens and follows the visitor''s dark/light theme. The in-browser Play client keeps its own fullscreen header (a sidebar would crowd the terminal). Pure web change (web/index.html, help.html, news.html); no server rebuild.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Mob alignments seeded -- evil/good mobs now band together and target by alignment', '2026-08-17. Every one of the 5685 mob prototypes shipped with alignment 0, leaving the alignment-driven combat/AI branches inert (all 5685 protos were align=0). Seeded cosmic alignment onto the supernatural races via db/tobin/mob_align.sql (race numbers per Sneezy race_t): undead, demons, devils, mind flayers, banshees, vampires, vampire bats and lycanthropes are now EVIL (align -100); angels, pegasi, shedu, lammasu, phoenixes and coatls are GOOD (+100). The runtime reads only the SIGN (good/evil) and tests two mobs for EXACT equality (assist banding), so one shared value per side maximises banding. Two effects now fire that never could before: (1) combat.c combat_recruit_assist() -- an aligned mob''s same-alignment allies join a fight you pick against one of them (~35%/round); (2) mob_ai.c -- an aligned aggressive mob fights only the OPPOSITE alignment and taunts (evil) or supports (good) neutral PCs instead of attacking them (the user''s 2026-07-11 design). DELIBERATELY LEFT NEUTRAL (disclosed scope): mortal humanoid raiders (orcs, goblins, trolls, drow, gnolls, kobolds, ...) and all wildlife, so they keep their attack-everyone aggression -- marking every goblin evil would gut low-level danger. Data-only change, no rebuild; guarded by smoke_test_mob_align_assist.py. Deployed via copyover (proto reload).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Caster mobs now inflict real debuffs (blindness/curse/fear/silence), not just damage', '2026-08-17. Caster-mob combat (combat.c mob_cast_combat()) previously resolved EVERY spell as its damage/heal core, so a Cleric mob''s curse or blindness and a Mage mob''s fear / faerie fog / silence all just landed as generic elemental damage. Added a debuff pass: ~45% of the caster rounds where a FRESH debuff is available (victim not already carrying it), the mob applies the SAME affect its PC-side cast does, at the same magnitude/duration -- Mage mobs from {fear, faerie fog -> AFFECT_BLIND, silence}, Cleric mobs from {curse -> DEX penalty, blindness}; Druid mobs stay nature-damage-only (no clean upstream debuff to mirror). Each entry names a real roster spell gated by the mob''s level (skill_find). One deliberate divergence from the PC `fear` cast: the mob does NOT force an immediate flee -- mob_cast_combat() runs inside combat_process_run()''s fighter loop, and moving the PC to another room mid-iteration would mutate the very fighting pointers that loop walks; the AFFECT_FEAR alone still stops the feared victim swinging back (cmd_attack.c). Guarded by smoke_test_mob_debuff.py; the caster damage-cast and alignment-assist regressions still pass. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Ward-buff family split into distinct affects (armor/bless/protection/mirror)', '2026-08-17. The whole ''protective ward'' spell family used to funnel into a single sanctuary affect that just halved incoming damage -- a documented early scope-cut (one real shared buff instead of dozens of bespoke ones). Now that the affect system is mature, each distinct upstream mechanic gets its own affect, routed by a new mapper that keys off the spell''s name and its help-text keywords. Sanctuary stays the pure damage-halver (and still covers sorcerer''s globe and trance of blades). Armor, stone skin, barkskin, shield and flaming flesh now improve armor class as a real to-hit penalty on the attacker (same shape as shield of mists). Bless (and the holy group buffs consecrate and crusade) is now an offensive buff -- an attacker to-hit bonus plus a small damage bonus. Protection from earth and any resistance ward now cut a flat percentage of damage taken, stacking with sanctuary. Plasma mirror reflects a percentage of each landed melee blow back onto the attacker (like thornflesh, but percentage-based). The affects list shows the new names: Armored, Blessed, Protected, Reflecting. No new spells; behaviour of the existing ones only. Guarded by smoke_test_ward_split.py. Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Pulse scheduler cap raised (32 -> 64) -- two dropped tick systems restored', '2026-08-17. The pulse scheduler had a fixed cap of 32 registered tick systems, but boot registers 34. The overflow guard logs an error and drops the extra registrations without stopping boot, so the LAST two registered -- planted-crop growth and trophy decay -- silently never fired (crops planted with `plant` never ripened or yielded; players'' kill-trophy counts never decayed over time). Every boot logged two "MAX_PULSE_PROCESSES (32) exceeded" errors. Raised the cap to 64 for real headroom rather than chasing the count by +1/+2 as past sessions did (8 -> 16 -> 24 -> 32 -> 64). Confirmed the post-fix boot logs no pulse error and both tick systems now register. Also refreshed two stale smoke tests (smoke_test_affects / affect_persistence) whose `score` HP regex still expected the old "HP: n (max)" format after it changed to "HP: n/max" -- both green again, and they double as a Sanctuary damage-halving regression (still works after the ward-buff split). Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Heat subsystem: gear insulation + weather coupling (deferred pieces landed)', '2026-08-17. The Tobin-original heat subsystem''s two deferred pieces are now built. (1) Weather coupling: the world sky state now shifts OUTDOOR ambient heat -- clear +5 (sun), cloudy 0, rainy -10, stormy -20 -- via a new weather_heat_delta() (weather.c) and room_ambient_heat() (room.c), so a storm cools a desert toward relief but deepens an arctic chill; indoors the weather does not reach. (2) Gear insulation: being_heat_insulation() sums occupied worn slots (4 points each, capped at 20) and being_effective_heat() (being.c) pulls the felt temperature toward the comfortable baseline (60) by that much, symmetric for heat and cold -- a labelled simplification (the heat system is itself an invention, so "layers even out the extremes" is the chosen model). vitals.c''s heatstroke/hypothermia HP chip now tests being_effective_heat() instead of raw sector_heat, so both weather and worn gear change who actually takes temperature damage. The immortal look builder-header keeps showing the intrinsic sector heat (weather is transient; read it with `weather`); the cosmetic sweat/shiver entry cue now reflects weather. Build clean (zero warnings); smoke_test_heat.py still 8/8 as a regression. Insulation/weather''s effect on the ~60s damage tick is verified by code review, the same limitation the base damage layer already documents (a live drain tick can''t be force-run). Deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'WEAR_PAIRED: two-handed weapons + both-limb armor; two-handed specialization now bites', '2026-08-18. New WEAR_PAIRED wear-flag (the original layout''s never-assigned bit 9): an item so flagged occupies BOTH members of its paired slot -- both hands for a two-handed weapon (needs two free hands, blocks an off-hand item), or both arms/legs/feet/wrists for a both-limb garment. wield/wear now require the whole pair free and fill both slots; remove/disarm/equipment-destruction clear both; and because inventory saves a single slot per object, the paired partner is re-derived on load. New primitives obj_is_paired() (obj.c) and limb_pair_partner() (being.c). The Warrior ''two-handed specialization'' (level 1, Advanced tier) now actually does something: an extra proficiency-scaled DAMAGE bonus while wielding a WEAR_PAIRED weapon, same passive learn-by-doing shape as the slash/blunt/pierce specializations (damage-weighted -- a two-hander''s payoff is the heavy hit). Also dropped ''cure blindness'' and ''word of recall'' from the Druid roster (per user): they are Cleric prayers and the Druid cast path never handled them, so they were dead roster entries. Guarded by smoke_test_wear_paired.py (18 checks: both-hands occupancy, second-weapon refusal, remove clears both, load restores both, paired leg armor + refusals). NOTE: no in-world items carry WEAR_PAIRED yet -- seeding greatswords/mauls/halberds/etc. and any both-limb armor is a follow-up data task. Build clean; deployed via copyover.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'GMCP Room.Info now carries exits, not just name', '2026-08-21. Room.Info (fired on every real room display -- look, movement, login, all funnel through the one cmd_look.c choke point) now includes an `exits` object mapping each visible direction to its destination vnum, alongside the existing `num`/`name`. Sourced straight from room_t.exits[]/exit_cond[] already resident in memory -- no new state, no DB work. Secret (undiscovered) exits are omitted, same convention the `exits` command already uses (cmd_exits.c) -- a mapping client is only ever told what a player could actually see. This is the server-side half of the client mapping-support TODO item: a graph-walked client map can now be built from real exit data as a player moves, instead of just room names. The client itself does not consume this yet -- that is the next piece. The separate level-59+ full-world bulk-export command (for a complete reference map, not just explored rooms) is still unscoped/unbuilt. New gmcp_build_room_info() signature takes the room''s exits/exit_cond arrays; gbuf bumped 256->512 at the one call site to fit up to 10 exit pairs. Guarded by an added check in smoke_test_gmcp_msdp_msp.py (Room.Info payload carries an exits object). Build clean; deployed via copyover.')
